      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>HE: "Darling, will you love me when I'm gone ?" She: "Yes, if you're not too far gone." THERE is this about the ladder of fame—the rungs might be a good deal closer. BRAINS cannot be measured by the size of the head, nor eloquence by the extent of the mouth. Lehman : "Did your watch stop when you dropped it on the floor?" Magley : "()f course it did. Did you think it would go through?" "What is that racket upstairs?" said a caller to little Bonny Bloobumper. "That's ma dropping a hint to pa,' replied Bonny. SLOW AND SURE.— Flint : "Fine chap that clerk of yours Steel." Steel: "Yes, slow and sure." "He looks it." " Yes; slow to learn, sure to forget 1" NONE Too EVIDENT.— Aggressive Stranger: "I'd have you know, sir, that I come of a good family." New Acquaintance: "You must have travelled a long distance." "PA, what's descendants ?" "'Why, they are the people that come atter you, my son. Who is that young man in the reception-room ? " "That's one of sister's descendants come to take her out riding on a tandem." "YOUR son, I hear, is becoming an excellent landscape painter." "He is." " Does he imitate Nature well ?" " Imitate Nature? He beats Nature. He can put colours into the landscape that Nature never dreamed of." Mamma: "I don't see why you call Daisy Martin selfish. I think she is a very nice little girl." Ethel : Oh, mamma, but she is selfish She's always at the head of the class, and she won't let any of the rest of us get ahead of her." "I want to buy a make-up box," said the young married man. A make-up box?" the confec- tioner echoed. "We don't keep theatrical sup- plies." "I mean a box of candy to take home to my wife, I promised to be home three hours ago." Young author (to editor): "Getting a publisher, I have heard, is the most difficult thing in authorship." " Editor : "I don't think so." Author: " Ah, you encourage me. What, then is the most difficult ? " Editor: "Getting readers.', SHE • "Yes, I told you I'd always be a sister to you; and I'll be glad to hear anything you have to say to me." He: "Six months ago you told me I'd thank you some day for refusing me. Let me do so now., You can't hold a candle to the girl I'm engaged to now." CAREFUL HOUSEKEEPER : "Have you boiled the drinking water?" Faithful Servant: " Yes'm." "And sterilised the milk?" " Yes'm." "What is this in the soup?" "Oh, that isn't no bacillus, mum. That's only a cockroach." CLERK (to employer): "What shall I mark that new lot of black silk at?" Employer "Mark the selling price three shillings a yard." Clerk : "But it only cost one shilling a yard." Employer z "I don's care what it cost. lam selling off regard- less of cost." O'Hoommix "Pais, Lonnie! An' phat are yez afther doin' now? ' O'Hoolihan : " Begob, Rosy, it's meself as has bought a music-stool for Katie, an' Oi've been woinding the bastely thing up for over an hour, an' not a dhrop of musie can Oi get out of it at all at all 1" • SCHMIDT : "Some funny mistakes occur in this world." Miller : "What's up now ? " Schmidt : "Well, you see, Dr. Soonover had to fill out a certificate for one of his patients, and he put his own name in the place where he should have inserted the cause of the patient's death." PAPA (reading) : "The naturalist, who had slipped from the edge of the precipice, gathered momentum as he fell." Mamma : "Mercy !*Kept right on picking flowers even when he knew he was going to be killed !" "SEE here, young man, I'll have to take your name. Youi lantern's out." "Hold on, officer ; I'll explain. You see I bought one of those cheap bicycle suits this afternoon and I was caught in that thunderstorm miles from home. Now I'm trying to get back as quietly as I can in the dark. You see the necessity of it ? " "That's all right— go ahead." A SCOTTISH farmer recently paid a visit to a South of England cattle show, and while walking round got talking with a native farmer. Neither could well understand what the other said. The Scotsman got a little nettled at this, and put it down to the Englishman's stupidity. "Man," he at last said, "yer kye moos a'right, and yer cocks craw quite plain, but I'm hanged if I can mak' you oot." "EVER had a cyclone here?" asked a Kansas man who was visiting a country aunt in the East. "A cyclone I Oh, yes," said his aunt. "Deacon Brown's son brought one from Boston a spell ago, but law Ihe couldn't ride it. Tumbled off every time he tried." THE CLERK'S HOLIDAY.---Sir John Lubbock once insisted on a clerk going for a holiday, and mine down to the office next week to find him standing at the counter. Asked as to why he had not gone away, the clerk said he could think of no better way of amusing himself than by coming to watch someone else do his work. "THE fastest I 'ever walked," said O'Leary, "was in company with Altamont. We walked ten miles in one hour." "Impossible I" cried his hearers in chorus. "It's a fact. We walked on the road from the second milestone to the seventh." "That's only five miles." "Well, and don't two fives make ten ?" DIFFERENCE IN DURABILITY.—"It isn't at all safe to judge by appearance," said the restless boy's father. "Yes," replied the mother, "people are very deceptive.' "And so are inani- mate objects. You'd never think, merely judging by external indications, that the heavy pair of shoes I brought that boy was only going to wear for six weeks, while his fragile-looking Latin grammar wiliest him a lifetime. Sue is a very affable woman, and she would in- variably say the right thing at the right place if she were not near-sighted. "I see," she said, as she entered the drawing-room of her friend, "that you have caught the annual craze." "To what do you refer ? " "The rage for chrysanthemums. And that one which you have tossed so carelessly into the corner is one of the biggest and most beau- tiful I ever saw. What an exquisitely odd colour ? " " Yes," was the reply, ‘; it's beautiful, and I prize it very highly. Only it isn't a chrys- anthemum. It's my sitye terrier taking a nap." SUCH IS FAME.—" Who is that little man talk- ing to all tilos people crowding about him? He's been attracting no end of attention 'to-night." "Why, haven't you heard of Jinkins, the great explorer, just returned from his expedition into the very heart of Bangaboo, where he had the most thrilling experiences?" "Certainly ; and you don't mean to tell me that's Jinkins ? " "Oh no! Jinkins isn't here. That's Firkins, who chums.' 114 used to go to the same school with Jinkins." gy INTUITION.—Mrs. Worreigh : "I want to engage a special detective to shadow my husband." Chief of the Detective Bureau: "Very well, madam. What are your suspicions regarding him?" Mrs: Worreigh : "Oh, I can hardly tell, but I know he is doing something he wants to keep from me." Chief: "What makes you think so?" Mrs. Worreigh: "Well, he gave me a bicycle the other day without my asking for it, and he bought me a new dress and said I could go to the seaside this season. He never does such things unless he is up to something." Nox AN ORDINARY CLOCII.—WhiIe out shooting in the rural districts of the Highlands a short time back, I found that my watch had Stopped. Enter- ing a farmhouse, and noticing an old grandfather's clock, I looked at it, and exclaimed: "Your clock is surely wrong ? " igaething wrang wi't ava," answered the old farmer; "it you that doesna understand it. When the wee haun's straicht up, and the big haun's straicht doon, it strikes ten; but the richt time's five o'clock. After that," he continued, " ye've naething to dae but calculate." "See dat nice rich-lookin' old lady. over there on de 'awn, wid de gold specs?" said the tramp to his pal. Well, I goes up in front of her, and I on de ground an' begins to gnaw de short grass. 'Kan, man, what are you doing?' says she, just like dat. Madam: says I, in a weak and tremblin' yoke, I've got teF do it. I ain't had enuythink ter eat for four days: Poor feller; come wid me,' she says, jumping. up, an' lead in' me around to 'de back of de house. An, then, as I was gettin' ready for a good, square meal, she says,'Gib down au' try it here. pore man ; de grass is longer.'"</p>
        <p>FEMALE Al LMEZTTS Irregularities and Obstructions however obstinate quickly and surely relieved and removed in a few ...offs, after all e,ao fai!R, this ternedy acts as magic. Full particulars, testimonials and prc6fa will to sent</p>
        <p>on reeeliA of stamped envelope. ad m MAlt Y . o, Bishopegate Without, London. kAtablidlied ZO Years.</p>
