      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>ME DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>A pretty hat certainly a little difficult, but with due regard to covering the shape as to preserve the outline, with a well-wired tulle shape it might be carried out. The crown is first draped with double chiffon, over which is carelessly and not tightly dis- posed a layer of sequined white tulle with gold and black sequins. Any fancy sequined or tinselled net may be used in this fashion, and one and a quarter yards will be ample, with the same quan- tity of chiffon. The brim is covered with alter- nate folds of uncovered chiffon, and thirteen-inch</p>
        <p>straps of the sequined tulle laid over half a width of chiffon, these twisted and fixed in rouleau fashion to the brim. A few deft stitches with fine cotton and a long millinery needle will keep the twisted brim in position, and this sometimes sets closely up to the crown, or there is a folded band of the net round the crown. The left side and back are slightly raised by a little band of stiff canvas, covered with silk, which is sewn beneath the hat, and on this crushed roses or carnations are arranged, and the cluster of feathers and osprey are first firmly wired, and then sewn on to the crown. The general characteristics of gowns for the moors remain practically unaltered year after year, for such costumes are built with utility as the first consideration. Therefore, though coat and skirt are modified in cut, to accord with the style of the moment, the material of which they are composed is invariably homespun tweed or frieze, faced and finished with leather. Dark heather and natural fawn and brown tints are the sportswoman's favourite wear. Grey is a trifle too light and con- spicuous, while it is only the uninitiated who don the startling plaids and checks, sometimes ex- hibited as "shooting costumes," which are calcu- lated to scare any game half a mile away</p>
        <p>A very serviceable shooting costume is of brown tweed, the skirt well cut, measuring two and three-quarter-yards round the bottom, where it is finished with a three-inch band inside and out of tanned leather, with lines of machine-stitching in tan silk at top, bottom, and along the centre of the band. The skirt r.ache nearly to the ankles, but is quite short enough to enure comfort in walking. The coat is close-fitting and beautifully shaped, crossing over to the lett side, where it fastens with a single large horn button, and cut away near the waist, revealing a glimpse of the neat double-breasted waistcoat Of tan leather. The collar and cuffs are faced with leather, and there are leather shoulder-straps, terminating in a point back and front.</p>
        <p>Coat bodices differing from the skirt are new being worn, or the coat and skirt correspond, awl there are revers vest, and centre skirt pane: of different material. The cloth coat and plaid skirt makes an undoubtedly chic combination, and many serges and self-coloured skirts have Plaid linings, the plaid showing again as vest or yoke, and in the hat. A costume of this type, with cape lined plaid to. match, makes charming costume for country or seaside wear, and both cape and hat are useful with other gowns.</p>
        <p>The fashion of tightly-fitted upper parts and deep flounces on skirts admits of much variation, and one notab:e model has the yoke and upper por- tion of skirt Of blue silk, with a dainty little pattern in blue and black running all over, while the bodice and shaped flounce are of plain blue silk, trimmed with black ruching..</p>
        <p>A gown of soft woollen stuff or thin costnme cloth, if brightly and smartly trimmed, is admir- able for preeent we tr, and equally useful for home winter wear, or under a warm wrap, and each a gown comes in prettily for afternoon concerte and theatres, or for "At Homes," where a blazing fire and crowded rooms make actual winter gowns op. pressive. Any soft woollen fabric, either plain or faintly patterned, will make an effective gown, but smart pervenche blue or red-heliotrope is charming with black WA velvet, or, for brunettes, a sound grey, with cherry-coloured velvet, is most becom- ing. This model also works out well in black,with cerise or old-rose velvet, and the gttaged chemistite of old-rose surah. These bright touches of colour are just the thing for winter wear, and glorify a very ordinary black gown.</p>
