      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>JENN</head>
        <p>JENN</p>
        <p>We Guarantee Satisfaction! Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago, Piles. Pains in the Bark Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint, Diseases of Kidneys Bladder, Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism, and Gout will find a Positive Cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Price. is id., of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps. from id HOLDROYD'S, Medical Hall, Cheekbeaton, Yprks. Sol at 35, Bridge-street, Warrington. Don't be put Off. if you Can- not get them write to the Proprietor and ato will be seat next post.—Advt</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT." Thc trit;ic death of the Empresi Eltzabeth of Au4tria. has, of course; put au end to all Jubilee feativities planned or 1/ cember next. The Emcierer is nOlelikely to rt,ccive any foreign guests on the otcasion, and the event will tow only be celebra'el vervtquietly, through- out the country. The deep,st mourning isas been ordered for the fint two months, this to be lightened 6otnewhit for the neat to months, and ta'f Motirsing is to he worn for yet ato:her two months. It will therefore tr, six months belt re the Coln is o itint mourning.</p>
        <p>At one tit-no the late Entiv-ss was considered the best dressed woman in Eut op., rxeedin even the Emprss Eugenie in this particular. And until lately she remained a perfect vision of beauty and grace, slender, large-eyed, and with the clear brilliant t omplexion so seldom seen after early youth. Her azure was superb, and the general effect of her presenc.) elegant and imposing. Her Majesty was brought up very much like a boy, and even when atilt quite a child, toße ionz lonely expedi- tions in the wild Bavarian mountaics, only accompanitd by one trusted old attendant. It was then she learned to ride so splendidly,-and that she 'acquired that love of solitude, of which some of her husband's subje.its were wont to complain • i</p>
        <p>_ The Broyress was never a great advocats frr etiquette; and with bbe Austrians, for that reason, was never so popular as with the Huogapans. Her Majesty Was adored in Hungary. Tim sole intervention in politics she allowed herself was that in favour of the union of the Dynasty and Hungary. Frac lastly, it was her work and the happie4 of her life. The Hungarians were it ungrateful, and she was' vener ited among tern, like one of the national Saints._ She shard in their fiell slants, to, which she imnaited an inspuleo and .preet-ge of unprecedented force, and she wrote and spc Ic3 thoir lan- guage—the most d fficuit of Euus; cal tongues—to ahso- lute perfection. Hungarian 0/61n113 are, as a rule, wellegrown and pretty. , Their manners are courteous and r fined, aod the-e is a natural grace about tlyir simple and-easy way of cmvers:ng %vitt) foreigners whicn is charming. Bat these Hungarian women are as modest as they are clever,and w. 11-educated. They have pretty winning ways apd salt voices and hare the daintiest of manners at table. They drink wine but in wry moderate quantities. They do rot despise beer. But their chief delight is in fruit, and in sweatmests. Many strange delights come to us in this shape here, that are quite unkcow to us at home.</p>
        <p>The lower class wonarn are universally gentle and They are the bast of wives too, although they do not hold themselves to be inferior to the husband. They take past in hie business and occupation, thus qualifying them eves to be his pleasant and helpful comralion,thus qualifying themeeloss to be his plessant and helpful ccmpanion. A strikiog peculiarity of Buda- Pesth women is their predilection for pink. There is scarcely a woman f any standing wh 1 does not patron- ise this colour, both far morning and for evening drs Lace too they affect in large quantiles, skirts and dressm being trimmed with back and white examples of that tecture. Th ugh not so lell as their German sisters Flunearian women despite the intense heatore of fairer complexicn. This fact may perhaps be attributed to the softnest cf the watsr which is most agreeable to the skin.</p>
        <p>For some time past, disappointment has b en felt, by the many visitors to Windsor Castle awing to the tem- porary closing of the Albert Mtmorial Chapel. This was found necessary during the erection of the memorial of the late Duke of Clarenc-, wirch however is now nearly completed. Oaly the finishing t inches are wanted, and the Chapel is open to the public once more. The coffin rests in a sarcophagus of a sort of onyx or alabaster,and has a bronze cover, on which lies the recumbent figure of the Duke, in the unifcrm of the 10th Husears, and the Robes of the Garter, - For ths twelve Saints, Mr. Gilbert has used aluminum with exceilent effect, while wory is free'y used for the heads and hands. The stand on which each Saint is placed is the conventional rendering of a tomt,,%nd thus in turn is supported by two angels,lovely figures in flow- ing draperies of bronze and silver. Toe tomb is sur- rounded by a high grille or railMg, whith stands on a pedestal of marble. Mr. Gilbert has been assis'ed by the suggestions of the Princess of Wales,herself no mean sculptor, and the re-ult is n&amp;que. Madame Antoinette Serlin4's son Malcolm, has, it seems, given up all idea of going to the Bar. He has decided upon a musical career. Fec people, except those in Madame Sterling's own immediate circle, are aware of the fact that her husband Mr. Mackinley, was a perfect musician—though nrt a professional one.—At one time no more interesting d niers vr.are given in all London than tries, of Mr. and Mrs. Mackinley, where all that was clever and brilliant in London society was to he met. Madame Stering is an antidote to the "blues." It is almost impossible to feel tired, croeS, nervous. or "headachy," in her presence. She always sags that the way to be well and happy is to believe oneself so, and not let oneself be surrounded by a dark atmosphere of doubt and gloom. And all this with so charnaihg and kindly a smile that it is imoossible not to web c &gt;me and accept her theories, at least, until the magnetism of her strong personality has gone—with her presence—from one's room.</p>
        <p>Ireland has good cruse to b 3 prJud of the Brown Nurses,deven in number,who have been presented with the Decoration and Diploma of the Ancient Order of Sc. John of Jerusalem. During the dreadful outbreak of typhus fever in Inniskea, which lies of the col.sc of County Mayo, these clurageous women never flinched, and in spite of six of their number faring victims to the plague, they did splendid service. Nursing stilt attracts many vosaries from the upper classes. Among the latest recruits is Lady Hemline Blmkwood, daughter of Lord Dufferin, who is now engaged as a probationer at the Chelsea Workhouse Infirmary. Miss B. Cutler, who has just:been appointed Inspector of Female Pilgrims at El Tor, is the first British woman to hold this p-st.. It is at El Tor that the pilgrims re- turning from Mecca have to undergo a toren of twelve days quaranteen, f Alowed by a very strict inspection, and it is only when the result is shown to be quite satis- factory that yermission Is granted to the pilgrims to proceed to their various countries. Miss Cutler, who until recently was sur-director of the G:r:s M dical School at Cairo, will find her rxtensive knowledge of Arabic of graat service to her, in her new work. The revival of Scottish tweeds is an item upon which to congratulate ourselves. They seem at once to fall in with the spirit of the moment, to lend thentselves to the fitting skirt, the small sleeve, and the closely clinging bodice. These tweeds have many pleasing attributes,of which, first and foremost, is that fleck of brightness, perchance of red, blue, orange, parple or white, that crops up in a manner as irresistible as it is irresponsib:e. Repeated in the facings of the gown, a d in the head gear, the effect leaves nothing to be desired. There is only one thing to regret in a Scot tish tweed, that is—it refuses to wear out. Now there is something distinctly irritating about a fabric of this description. It is a reproach. a taunting rrminder of our fickleness, from which, getting rid of it, is the only escape. Many a generous gift of a sturdy costume must bp credited to this unworthy insi.ine t, and even to that rnethoi there may be some drawbacks. Fur years ago I gave a two d gown to an individual who has seen " tetter days." Never since have I seen that woman in anything else. She has turned ar d washed, washed and turned it, and it looks much the same as it did roily beginning. What will become cf my present Sontch.`tweed, when I can support its pattern no longer, I known not. To give, I am afraid. The Fates ass'st m .</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER</p>
        <p>There is no doubt that wAing is the best of all exercises, pr ofided, of course, that it is not over-done, or done in over quick style. Such preceedings are not gool for anybody. G )ed w alkiag means ne Vier the stride nor the hop, but to place the fo firmly and de- liberately on the ground, thu beel touching the earth very slightly before the rest of the foot. For,country walking, the boots sh9uld be vii.lta acrcei the tread, the heal perfectly square.</p>
        <p>Inie days of grand mothrra, a mincing step was considered an adtel beauty to its owner, but nowadays nous avons change tout cela. A aocifty women is drilled lrom her badyhood to vralk,dance,and carry herself with grace,and the result la usualy a very charming one. Moreover the active ex.rcise girls navy indulge in all helps to make them walk easily and gracefully if in, do not try to achieve the stride of a man, when all grace IS last.</p>
        <p>(00PYrigist-)</p>
        <p>Team= DELIGHT.—Take four tablespoonfuls of corn- our, mix to a smooth cream with a little cold water. and add tw) tab'e3poonfuls ef fine powered sugar. Boil one pint of water, pour it—while boiling—upon the mixture, stirring quickly all the time. Add a little good nsawater, and if preferred calour pink with cochineal. Cover a large di-.h with a little powered sugar, pour the mixture upon it,leava till quite set,and then cut it across and dust the remainder of the sugar on the top. "MADGE.</p>
