      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WARD OF THE KING.</head>
        <p>A WARD OF THE KING.</p>
        <p>BY KATHARINE S. MACQUOID, Author of "Maisie Derrick," The Story of Lois," "Patty," "Louisa," "At the Red Glove," "Miss Ryon of Eyoucourt." &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII. IN PERIL. A peremptory knock at her door startled Jeanne, and she rose and put across It a second bar which stood beside it. "Open, open, madame, in the King's name!" a stern voice said. "It is I, Laval; my business is urgent!" In his anger he violently shook the door, but the bars held it firm. Jeanne felt strangely calm. It was useless, she thought, to hold any speech with this angry man. She suspected that he had heard of the Constable's flight from St. Feriol, and guessed that she also knew of it. She said, with all the dignity she could muster: "You must excuse me, Monsieur le Comte; I cannot admit you at this hour. I beg you to send away your man Gaspard; he is intoxicated." "She-devil!" she heard him mutter; but he answered mildly enough: "Yes, madame; I will obey your wish, and return at daybreak; then I must see you, without fail." He went rapidly downstairs, his spurs jangling at every step. . Jeanne listened anxiously, but she could not tell whether he had quitted the house. At last came the sound of a heavy door, opened and then loudly closed ; and as she sat with strained ears she heard the sound of retreating footsteps. She breathed heavily, and leant back with a sense of relief in her hard wooden chair. Laval had not left the house. Going back to the eating-room, he found Gaspard sound asleep, with his face on the table. His heavy snoring filled the room, and made Laval furious; he gave the sleeper a fierce kick, but this only elicited a growl and muttered curses. Laval looked around him; he believed that the Constable was either in the house or not far off, and he knew that minutes were precious. On his first arrival he had not felt confident that Bourbon was so near, though he suspected he was not far off, but when he reached the village the constrained looks that greeted him had deepened his suspicions and added significance to the hostess's positive refusal to lodge anyone but the lady and her women. He had therefore hastened his return to the inn. He reflected, however, that if Bourbon were at La Verit6, De Pomperant was surely with him; and what could he, Laval, do without the help of Gaspard? "Pardieu ! I will in some way learn the Duke's whereabouts ; fraud may succeed where force fails." ' He went cautiously into the passage and peered through a half-open doorway; within it he saw a small empty room. He checked an exclamation of satisfaction; though the room was empty, he saw a rude desk, beside which lay a formidable bunch of keys ; he guessed that this was the hostess's sanctum. If he could conceal himself here, he might learn the secrets of the house. There was a closet beside the door by which he had come in, and above it, close to the ceiling, was a grating. The closet was evidently made in the wall, and was, like it, of dark wood. Was it open, he wondered? He moved very cautiously, lest the clink of his spurs should betray him, and found, to his joy, that the door was only fastened by a button. There was, however, a key in the lock ; he drew this out, tried it on the inner side, then slipping into the closet, which he found much larger than he expected, he locked himself in. The closet smelt horribly musty, and this shel,ved Laval that it was not much resorted to. As he groped about, he stumbled over what seemed to be a heap of horse wrappings on the floor. The room itself was but dimly lighted by a lamp in an iron cresset, which hung from the beam above ; there was total darkness in the closet, except a faint glimmer below the door. Laval raised one of the woollen wraps and spread it across his chest and shoulders, so that he .might be less visible in case the door should be by some chance opened from without. Then he stood listening. Very soon he heard the sound of an arrival, but as far as he could make out it was only a single horseman; next someone was ushered into the small room into which the closet led. A deep, strong voice asked whether a lady was staying at La Verite. The answer did not come at once; then the hostess said : "Yes, monsieur, a lady arrived not long ago, but I think she has retired to rest." "She will see me," the deep voice quickly an- swered. "You will say to the lady that her old friend, the Chevalier Rolland de la Touche, has travelled many miles on purpose to see her." "I will tell the lady, monsieur." The door opened and shut, and then steps could be heard mounting the creaking stairway to Jeanne's chamber. Laval ground his teeth in silence. He had morn to himself that D'Orbec and Jeanne should not meet again; and now he was powerless. But die girl would refuse to appear, She had refused to admit him on account of the late hour, and now it was later still. • He told himself he had been a fool to 'do his own errand; he ought instead to have sent the hostess to Jeanne's 'chamber. Yet, on the whole, it had happened los the best. Supposing that Jeanne came down tc see D'Orbec; he should overhear the talk of thess f two friends, and learn their intentions; and after- wards, when Gaspard had regained his senses, they two would be more than a match for this fool who had ventured single-handed to so lonely a spot as the hostel of St. Bonnet. And when hes friend was safely disposed of he would call Jeanne herself to account for her behaviour. He soon heard voices and footsteps ; the chambei door was opening. Then came an indescribable murmur of gladness. Laval raged and swore as he thought how this rustic's eyes were drinking ir the girl's beauty, and how he was sunning himself in her smiles. "He shall not have her, for all that!" He gave a malignant smile. "She is bound to marry Ins now, to save her reputation." Jeanne was speaking to D 'Orbec, and Laval put his ear to the door. "How good of you tc come! How did you find me? " "I ask myself that very question as I look at you." There was a laugh in the deep voice. "The description I have been giving of the lady I wished to find was not like yoit, madame; no more like you than a closed rosebud is to the opened flower. I understand now what the good hostess meant at Moulins when she said it must be long since I had seen the Countess of Laval." Jeanne blushed, and her little foot patted the floor with impatience. She said, with an arch glance that took Rolland back to Longuedec: "Fie, monsieur! you have, I fear, been keeping bad company! Since when have you learnt to call me madame, and to pay me compliments, as I hear they do at Court ? " "I have not been at Court, sweet friend; I have k,..en keeping watch in the Low Countries, and also fighting there, so that it was not till after you had quitted Laval that I received the sad news which told me you were an orphan." The listener in the closet noted a pause. Jeanne bent her head, while her eyes filled; the sight of Rolland—the very sosnd of his voice— bad conjured up that happy time when she used to tease him to her heart s content, and her loved mother used to sit near them, smiling at their disputes. Happy days!— gone, alas! from both their lives for ever. "I discovered at Laval that Gillonne, your woman, c•tad quitted you, and had gone to Mayenne. This puzzled me; I could not believe that the faithful creature would have forsaken you except for some good reason. My idea was that she had gone to Orbec or to Longuedec, so I went there. At Longuedec I learnt she had not been heard of, and I sent Lao —you remember him ?—back to Orbec, bidding Gillonne, if he found her there, follow me to Moulins, for at Laval I . learnt that your escort was on its way to seek the King." Jeanne nodded. "That was kind and clever of you; but how can you have tracked me from Moulins ? " She looked at him with such perfect trust in her lovely eyes that he sat silent, thinking what II blessed chance it was that he had found her. "Why do you not tell me? " she said with a touch of impatience, so like the naughty Jeanne of former days, when a wilful fit seized her, that instead of answering her question he laughed. The listener in the closet cursed them again. The flush deepened on Jeanne's sweet face. while Rolland wished lie were free to sit and look at her, instead of having to cudgel his memory for facts which the sight of her had completely blotted out.</p>
        <p>4You see, lam still as naughty as ever) Ana, after all, why need I tuk how yon ennie? S;nce you are here, that is enough." Tre felt thrilled by the look she gave him. "I only want to know if you heard of me at an inn called the Fleur de Lys, between this place and St. Etienne?" "Yes, I heard of you, but I learnt your route from the inn in the village close by; the soldiers who form your escort had been quartered there, and I heard enough about them to quicken my desire to find you without delay. I learnt that they are here in Auvergne for the express purpose of capturing the Constable de Bourbon, and that their leader is confident of success."</p>
        <p>Jeanne laughed gaily. "Do you not remember telling me that I must drop salt on a bird's tail before I could catch it? But I see you are on the right side; I can read your eyes just as I used to. Now lam going to tell you a secret. The Count of Laval has been kind, but as long as he keeps me with him he will not find it easy to capture my lord of Bourbon. You used to read me the tale of a caged lion, and how a poor little mouse set the noble beast free. Well, then, my friend, I did that to-night." The listener an the closet ground his teeth with fury. Rolland whistled. "To-night? Hist, my child, we must be care- ful! " he went on in so low a voice that Laval fr..atld hardly hear him. "How came it that you wased he was in the inn ? " hardly know; I suppose I came prepared. Did they tell you at the • Fleur de Lys that I was left alone there with my women?" "Yes, they told nae." "Well, then, one night I saw two horse- men ride away from the inn, and I felt sure that one of them was the Duke." "Did Laval suspect anything ? " "Yes, and his questions put me on my guard. When I arrived here, I saw alarm in Madame Tarbes' eyes, and I seemed to know the Duke was in hiding. I promised when we were at Moulins to give him warning if I could, and I felt that the chance was given me. You know the good Duke was always my hero, Rolland." The listener dug his nails into his palms; he could scarcely keep himself from bursting forth. "Alas! yes," Holland sighed ; "the hero of all the noblest hearts in France. You saw him, then? Did you speak with him ? " He spoke very low, and she answered in the same tone. "More than that "—her eyes shone with proud enthusiasm—" he kissed me, and thanked me, and said L was his fair cousin."</p>
        <p>_ Rolland took her hand and pressed it. "You have a noble heart, Jeanne! But now' about yourself. Tell me why I find you here in the Auvergne, when you took the long journey to Moulins to seek the Kin? It seems to me you qhould have gone direct fbrom Moulins to Lyons. ' "Have I not done so? Are you sure? Her cone was full of surprise. "Monsieur de Laval told me we were going towards the camp.'' Rolland bent his brows, but he did not at once express his indignation. He longed to know the relations that existed between Jeanne and her handsome young escort, for it seemed to him that she might side with the Duke, and yet be fascinated by Laval • but D'Orbec could not believe that she had brough't herself to love such a rascal as he considered him. Rolland rose, walked to the further end of the room, and then coming back, he placed himself before her.</p>
        <p>"Listen Jeanne, you are now your own mistress; you are at liberty to go where and when you will. Mathieu is in command of a dozen strong fellows, your own Longuedec men; old Leon raised and armed them to serve as your escort. I have joined to them my Coetfrec lances. My only doubt is—well, I will be frank with yolt. Are you in any way pledged to Laval? " It was said now ; the words had uttered them- selves almost without Rolland's consent, and he shrank from the indignant light in the girl's eyes. Meanwhile Laval was intently listening for her answer. It came at last, with the most freezing courtesy of tone. I pray you, monsieur, to say when you have known me keep willing company with a villain, when I know him to be such ? ' "Pardon me, Jeanne, but you did not say you were Laval's prisoner." Jeanne had risen, and, now, deeply wounded, she stood looking tit him. The pain in her eyes made his heart ache. He tried to speak, but Jeanne stretched out her hand, and put it before his mouth. "I see ; I know," she sobbed. "But till to- night I have gone on in blindness. Perhaps I was too careless to suspect a motive for the Count's kindness ; and then—Oh, Rolland, I can never forget it!—in the midst of his own sore trial, he—the Duke—told me the danger of my position, and warned me that Laval was a bad man."</p>
        <p>"Did he? Curse him!" the listener muttered. "Another item in the score I have against Charles de Bourbon!"</p>
        <p>Jeanne went on gratefully: "Can you not, than, imagine the joy your summons gave me to-night ? " Rolland sighed with relief. She might not care for him, but she did not love Laval ; she was free. "I will leave you now," he said, "to take the rest you must greatly need. I shall soon return; Igo but to find my men, and to learn whether they have news of Gillonne. I will say au revoir, dear Jeanne. Shall I bid the hostess attend you ? " "Thank you, I am safe here; but return as soon as you can, my true friend." The listener longed to see how they parted, but he feared to attract attention by a movement. He soon heard the Vicomte speaking to the hostess in he courtyard; then he heard him ride away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>COOL DISHES FOR HOT WEATHER.</head>
        <p>COOL DISHES FOR HOT WEATHER.</p>
        <p>In summer the lady of the house is ever on the look- out for sintab'e des 3rt dishes fir the family, especially wher 3 there are children. She will find that one of the coolest and most agreeable dishes, and one ever welcome to the children, and to their elders, is a bison-mange made with Browo ard Poison's Corn Flour. This may Le accompanied with pre erved fruit or stewed fruit of all kinds in season. A delicious jelly can be matte by Preparing Corn Floor with the clear joica of stewed • rhubarb, or any other juicy ftuit, instead of with milk. To produce thrs3 dainty dishes really good Cr s Floor is I essential. Brown and Poison's" Patent" Corn Flour has long been recegnized as the standar I article fcr these dishes. Though sometimes a trills d rer than ordinary makes, it goes much further than others, and the dishes made from it are always much finer in flavour. Ask 110! Bro-n and Poison's " Patent " C3rn Flour, best quality aril be sure that you get it.—Advt</p>
        <p>A Reading telegrAm reports that the man Reginald Brewer. who was remanded last week charged with being concerned with Ward and Smith, now awaiting trial. In the Ascot burglary, was discharged m Tuesday tnerning. the Treasury baying no evidence to offer against him. A Hull correspondent says a shocking tragedy was enacted on the Humber on Tuesday. near Hull Two men named Akester, father and son, were in charge of a sloop when, it is alleged, they began to quarrel, and the father fell into the Water. He wts rescued by some men in a tug, who failed however, to restore animation. Mr. George Ache of the Thames Rowing Club. made an attempt on Tuesday morning to cross the English Channel in a Thames river skiff weighing only 251 b. When about three or four miles out, however, the skiff sank, and Mr Ache had to be retcued by a boat sent from the tag which accompanied him.</p>
