      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CIGARETTE MAKER'S ROMANCE.</head>
        <p>A CIGARETTE MAKER'S ROMANCE.</p>
        <p>ONE of the wet striking features of the East End of London is the manner in which it has become peopled with the Jewish race : many trades and occupations are almost exclusively in their hand, and among these. tha business of bald-made eigar.ittis finds employment for hundreds of the Hebrew womankind. A reporter on the East London Advertiser, was recently deputed to find out a young Jewess who for manths had brim sick unto death ; she bad to give uy her work, and the doctor described her condition as almost hopeless. It was to find out the truth about this affair that the reporter caged on Miss Ethel Samuels, at 8, Beiumont Cottages, Beaumont-square, E. A young WOLTIVI in all the beauty and charm of perfect health met him ; it was difficult to blievo that her life bad twin to danger. But the story of her trial was a surprising one. " It was about Christmai, 1898," Ethel began. 66 that I began to fal a victim to Ancemia. Gradually 11Y conditices became worse and worEe,until at fait a doctor was called in. His treatmont.however, did not seem to do me any good,and at last I had to give up my busi• neis as a cizarette maker. Then I ha] to taks to my bed,and on one OCC4siOn I hid te remain there Bevel weeks, and on a second thole tire.) weeks ; at last the doctor sail the only chance I had was to go right away. It wee about this time that I called to mind the cures of Miss Da Costa and Miss Emanuel, who live near by, and I thought t'.iat if Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People cured them they might cure me. 8) I bought a box of the Pills. Before I had finished its cont Fort I felt a great improvement. I then gave up the doctor—as he had given me up—and persevered with the PUli.ln al I had three boxes, and now le3l fully recovered." She adicd, "I am now able to go to business again." "It is all true," Mrs. Samuels j eined in. "E- het was very ill ; she hai to give up work altogether and take to her bed. I shall never forget what we owe to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills." "No' shall I," said Miss Ssmnels. "I sha'l al•iiays remember how ill I was. I would be seized with f tint- ing fits, then have int:Jerable pains in the limbs and in the head, but now I have n pain of any sort t 3 com- plain of."</p>
        <p>- Thus concluded a most interesting interview, and though the circumstances may appear extracrdinixV, Miss Samuel's curb will h. understood when It is ex• plained that Dr, Williams' Pink Pills act directly on the , blood; thus it is that they are el farnoua for the cure of enmnlia, indigestlon, cunsumption,rneumatism„scrof ale, general weskneil, and to restore- pale-and sallow coin- plesions to the glow of health. They are also a splendid nerve and spinal tonic, and thus hays cured many casts of paralysis locomotlr ataxy. nearalgia, St. Vitus' dancP, and nervous headache. Dr. Wslliams' Pink Pills ate nbtainable of all chemists, and of Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 48, Hollyirn-viaduct, London, at 2a. 91. a box, or six bozo; for 13s. 91., but are fiertuine only with the full cams, Dr. Williams' Pink 'Pills for Pale People, printed in red on the pink wrapper. „</p>
