      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAPTER XXI.V. IN THE LITTLE WU.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.V. IN THE LITTLE WU.</p>
        <p>Jeanne sat still; one slender hand was pressed tightly over her eyes ; she wanted to shut in the picture which Rolland's frank, tender gaze had left. It seemed as if he still stood before her, as though the ring of that true, manly voice still sounded in her ears. Ah, how could she even for a moment have found pleasure in listening to the deceitful traitor, who had so long blinded her to the perfidy he practised? She started and looked around her, for a por- tentous yawn made itself heard. Then a bench grated over the floor of the eating-room; there was a stamping of feet, and Gaspard's voice shouted loudly for the drawer. This was the sound for which Laval had waited. He cursed his ill-luck that it had not come sooner. He unlocked and opened the door of the closet. Jeanne started violently when he came out ,of it and stood before her. "You may well look alarmed, madame; your double-dealing is discovered, you have been aiding and abetting traitors to escape from the just vengeance of the Ring. I should at once give you up to justice, but that I have too soft a heart; my tenderness for you makes me willing to screen ou from the fearful punishment dealt to those who connive at the escape of conspirators." He paused; then in a gentler voice: 'After all, I consider yours a youthful folly; you must be my wife, Jeanne, and that as soon as may be. I know no other way by which I can save you from the penalties of the law—such penalties "—he shud- dered—"f or so young and fair a criminal. It fills me with horror to think what might happen to on." Jeanne stood uprig)it, her eyes bright with scorn. "You coward! So you have been mean enough to listen! But you are too late; you cannot harm the noble Prince; he has escaped beyond your power to harm him." Laval put his arm round her; in spite of her struggles he held her fast, for with the other hand he had grasped both hers so tightly that she could not free them. "Do not cry out," he said quietly; "that will bring Gaspard to help me, for I must save you in spite of yourself. Be reasonable, dear girl; no harm shall happen to you." "Set me free! I will cry for help if you hold me an instant longer!" "No, you will not; if you do, I go away and leave you to Gaspard's tender mercies. You do not know him as I do; his first act would be to force a gag within thole pretty teeth, not to speak of worse indignities. Now listen." He paused, for, though still held her, he saw that his threat of Gaspard checked her cry for help. "You cannot deny, sweet one, that you have brought this on yourself. I can hardly believe that so fair and innocent a face has covered so much perfidy. In justice you deserve the punishment of a traitor, and if I leave you here, it must fall on you. But I will take Lou away with me, and place you safely with the -King. This iime we ride direct to Lyons. Your friend will not return ; my messenger will tell him that you and your women are on the way to Lyons to seek the King. You wish for the Royal pro- tection, and you shall have it, as soon as may be. "I can go to Lyons without you ; I have my own • wen to escort me, she said defiantly.</p>
        <p>lie shook his heact at her. tone:</p>
        <p>Men in a caressing</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>"What has caused fhis change, my dearest Jeanne? You have so long led me to hope that in heart and soul you were already mine that I have mid the matter before the Ring, ana ne has givell" his Royal consent to our marriage." She wrenched her hands so violently from his- clasp that she nearly freed herself. "You are lying!" she cried out ;"you know that you are lying!" "Strong words from such sweet lips ! They must pay the penalty." He tried to kiss her lips, but she twisted so deftly that he only kissed her ear. Passionate anger gave her strength; she tore one hand frota his grasp, and struck at him with all her force. "Fie for shame! That way is for a cat or a kitten not for a high-born damsel!" Jeanne still struggled with all her strength. "What! you will , not submit?" Then between his teeth he said fiercely: "By the Lord, you must be taught behaviour!"</p>
        <p>He seized her by the shoulders, dragged her to the closet, and so roughly flung her in that her head struck against the wall, and she sank in a heap on the floor. Laval instantly closed the door, locked it, and put the key in his pocket. "Pardieu ! what a wild cat ! So much the better; it saves trouble." He smiled unpleasantly. "If she recovers too quickly, she must be tied hand and foot, and we must for a while gag her pretty mouth. Gaspard well knows how to carry a fair lady like a bundle before him." He paused and then told himself it was a cursed business; but for that meddling fool D'Orbec, Jeanne would have followed wherever he chose to take her. He found Gaspard stretching and shaking him- self. Laval took him to the stables, bade him dip his head in a bucket of cold water, and then bring the horses out ready, with as little delay as possible. He re-entered the house by the back way, but as he turned to the room within which he had left Jeanne he heard some bustle before the house - door. Going forward, he saw a tall woman in colloquy with the hostess. He recognised Gillonne, and drew back, but the woman saw him. mon Dieu, there is Monsieur le Comte himself! Will Monsieur le Comte assure this good hostess that madame is my mistress, and that she may safely conduct me to my dear lady's chamber?" The reappearance of Gillonne, whom he thought safely disposed of, gave Laval a sudden shock. fie pulled himself together, and answered: Your mistress is not in her chamber, and doubt if Madame can tell you where to -find her." The hostess bated Laval and his errand; she considered his presence a blot on her house. She answered very stiffly: "I left the lady in my private room, with Monsieur le Vicomte d'Orbec." "You will not find her there now. Monsieur d'Orbec has ridden away, and the lady rode with him on the front of his saddle." Gillonne stared in surprise, hut Laval spar decidedly, and the hostess could not gainsay hig assertion.</p>
        <p>"Doubtless my lady's women are still here. Will you take me to them, madame?" Thb hostess nodded; she turned to shew Gillonne the way to the staircase, when Gaspard came in from the yard. "All is ready, my lord; I--" His eyes fell on Gillonne; he started and looked confused. Ho wondered what tale she had told about him. Gillonne left the hostess and hurried back to Laval. "That man is a villain, monsieur; he would have taken my life if I had not escaped from hito at Mayenne." As Laval turned on him in angry rebuke, Gos" . pard recollected himself. He knew that the Count could not do without him, and he also knew that nothing would be refused him if he helped to capture the Constable de Bourbon. "Pardon me, my lord Count, but the good dame' is in error. Come, come, Gillonne; you cannot say you were badly treated. , The truth is that you listened, and listeners hear things turned inside. out; they mistake lining for good stuff. As we rode along, my lord, I found Dame Gilloono pleasant and capable; I even fancied I had moat' progress in her good graces; when lo you there!' as ill-luck would have it, I must needs lodge nIY, convoy at the Croix d'Or. The hostess the'• Louise, is an old acquaintance, and the warmth of; her reception seems to have displeased my neer flame, for next morning, when I sought Daraer Gillonne in her chamber, I found her flown! Sh' went, I suppose, to her cousin, and I troubledPo head no more about her. Women are jealonsuit best of them." Then, with an unpleasant.. 44- Gillonne " Ventrebleu, dame, I advise yotl to Stitt to truth; you may else find this air unhealthy. A voice broke in: "A word with you, my lord, on the instant." Laval turned and saw the Moulins spy. Beckon- ing the man to follow him, he went into the noir vacant eating-room. The spy's face was full of excitement. "My lord has not a moment to lose; the Duke• is on his way to Vienne, where he means to ford • , the Rhone. Cross lower down, Monsieur le Comtet": and intercept him on the other side; he will not, expect to meet a foe in front, and capture will easy." Laval was almost frenzied. He dared not leave. Jeanne to be freed by -Gillonne, and yet evert) moment of delay risked his chance of seizing the fugitive constable. He stood gnawing Ilk°. moustache. .• - A knocking came from the inner room, then sudden outcry. "Help, helpl Let me out!" Laval dashed into the little room; Gillonne an the hostess ran there from the kitchen, the tire-: woman crying: "Great Heavens! that is my lady's voice! Where' . is she? Where ? "</p>
        <p>4 r;</p>
        <p>Laval pushed her out of his way. "Peace, foolish woman!" he said in a tone Of authority. "Do you not hear that the voice come° from above? Some ruffian is molesting your ladyl: women. See to it, hostess; women should tio'' come to harm under your roof." He was crazed with impatience to be rid of thou or he would not have uttered such foolish words:_, Vehement knocking in the closet was followed P 7 , another outcry from Jeanne. Gillonne was at the door in an instant.</p>
        <p>"Where is the key? " she cried, "I ant here dear lady. I dare swear that villain Count is e;t. the bottom of it! Ah, blessed saints be praised! Her voice rose into a joyful scream. "Look there —look there!" and she pointed to the window. (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>Of ill bP'</p>
