        <p>When a person has been saved from the agonies terrible and untimely death, or, indeed, when h( avoided intense suffering of any kind, it is only nit that he should be grateful, and desire that other should be benefited by his fortunate experien* Such has teen the inspiration of the tollowing lottery written June 28th, 1898, by Mr. James Wilson, Statiolv read, Hooton, near Chester : "In October, 1896. I taken ill, and at once consulted one of the best dooPr, in town, who told me I had inflammation of the kidne7 I went to bed, and remained under his tre.6 went, but endually got worse, and at end of six week., be tld my wife he not do anything more for me, it was Brigb_f disease I was suffering from, and only a questiollifj hours, just how long my strength would last„.. DroPfarr swelling extended from head to feet. I was blind.fl over a week. A friend who came in to See me 11dr,.7,8 my wife to try Warner's Safe Cure, which we did. .t6e. first few desespgave me relief, and at the end of tiPtil week; I was able to get up. I continued taking it.1_,13.44 am completely cured ; In fact, I have driven a cabiTsti lait August. and have been out many times until and three o'clock in the morning, yet lam stronger g , healthier-looking man than ever I have limn, incleeril never enilytd better health, and all who see MIL astonishel. I thank God for the day when first I Tot of Warner's Safe Cure, for I am certain that it saveu life. SO* "I feel Viat this wonderful medicine—Warner'S Cure—cannpt be too widely known."</p>
