      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>44 cAPIT t r." CHAT.</head>
        <p>44 cAPIT t r." CHAT.</p>
        <p>A Pretty story of thl dead Empress is told here Binde her visit to Cromer Her Majesty has eZhibited a close mild sympathetic interest in the lrelfare of two of its inhabitants. One of these Was an old fisherman, to whom she was first attrac- :ld by an act of d bring heroism which resulted in 4ne rescue of a child from what had seemed to be certain death. This deed, performed almost within the Empresi's observation, led Her Majesty to seek 45 acquaintance of the fisierman, an-1 thus , she Was brought into contact with his daughter, a pretty girl then in her teens. Her Majesty never lor.got the pair. Every Christmas the old man re- Ceived a very practical evidence of the Imperial re- gard, whilst the Empress not only dowered the daughter, but acted as sponsor to her first child. tibsequently she kept up communication with her Protige.</p>
        <p>Fora considerable number of months the London Papers have been abounding from day to day with ' accounts of street ruffianism which would justify be public in believing that acts of violence in the open streets are increasing both in number and, atrocity. The livelier journais, in their sprightly , !)tferts at picturesqueness, have even been attempt- to import a new name into the methods and lanais of crime. Gangs of ruffians certainly exist, SI:t1 some of them consist of mere boys, whose linuds are fed upon police-court literature and Leininiscences of Jack Sheppard and his class. The 'Lome Secretary, however, does not accept this view Of .the state of the streets, as he has refused to re- elve a deputation of the St. Pancras Vestry, which 'intruded to impress upon him the necessity of Strengthening the police force with the view of Patting down chronic rowdyism. Official police statistics do not point, he says, to any such increase rif acts of violence, at least not to the extent of requiring the police force to be strengthened. The I,leasures which would have been suggested to him °Y the vestry would have added enormously to the ,Cest of the force ; and at the financial effects of the lacrease Sir M. White Ridley stands probably both Paralysed and appalled.</p>
        <p>--- British subjects who think of sending arms and thZmunition to Spain,unless they are for the Govern- bent, would do well to be careful, for the Spanish Consul General in London has published an adver- tisement in several newspapers offering a liberal reward for reliable information regarding such con- lignments, and promising to treat communications on the subject as confidential. The Consul General interviewed with regard to this announcement, professes to know nothing about it, except that in raaking it he acts on orders from the Government. It may be that they wish to intercept arms and UMmunition intended for Carlists, but that is only Illnrmise. Of course this blankness of the offi-ial 14„itod will deceive nobody. There can be little doubt Zia the rulers of Spain are trying to prevent any "IlcAesale arming of th Carligs. .</p>
        <p>th!'ord Salisbury's pre,eace on the Contin3ra leaves 11° foreign Office without a head, and Mr. Curzm's h•eeregal appointment leaves it without a sub. 1,„•841,_ • Mr. Bagnur's engagements on the golfiing at North Berwick leave it without a ileum g,tlens, and with Mr. Chamberlain in the United 0" „Ittee, and the other Ministers on their holidays, "vernment decisioqs in sudden emergencies must „„ 4rrived at by a kind of round-robin. Mr. Curz ,n's ;Lecessor in the Foreign Office has not yet been ritvounced, and go sips would almost, appear to De forgotten that there is a vacancy there. It is erally assumed that the Under Secretaryship 41'1 remain vacant until Lord Salisbury's return. th Would be ra•her a trial to any new occupant ot ere Post to thad himself placed single-handed in the wrd of difficult lave 111 queßtions which are al- -`°(it day by day started upon the Department.</p>
        <p>The establishment of a technical college in Khar- b"."rli in memory 01 Goroon,instead of the establish- nt of Christian mission, meets with very general ii'v-213tance in influential quarters here, and a Zlonal fund will in a day or two be opened at the je,ttision House by the Lord Mayor to put the pro- On a financial footing. Already the whole 1.1."-1,000 which t-jr Herbert Kitchener indicated as E) minimum capital sum required to provide the atinual outlay, has been offered by a London gentle- ken, who in the meantime withholds his name but as a much larger sum will be necessary his liberal- ity will not preclude the many hundreds of thou- Sands of persons throughout the country who irg ould like to contribute something toward-, the 40.undation of a suitable memorial to the dead hero °E Khartoum, from subscribing their mite. The blemorial should be a thoroughly national one, aad an educational institution which would teach .tlle people European arts and sciences and enable ''aeto to settle down to industrial pursuits, and 'which would excite no religious animosites, must Commend itself to the good sense of the British Public.</p>
        <p>lie the dominions over which the murdered Era- Bress reigned the fierce anger of the people may i n- nence the Emperor,and induce him to ~pply to the ',2:ivlss Republic for the extradition of the culprit. • CtliCie. Joseph has never failed to show the in- -4tinctB of a refined and humane man, and he may rersonally be disinclined to satisfy a mere savage "ling of revenge; but even he could hardly with- rand a united demand on the part of his subjects s_Ot the blood of Luccheni. Curiously enough, un- nialactivity has prevailed for some time in that eeial department of Scotland Yard which devotes r• tention to the presence of Anarchists who arrive 1191 e metropolis from various continental centres, p. 14 not unlikely that the police authorities in rts gave London a hint of the conspiracy they had 00Vered,for the French colony here has witneswd aarkable increase of numbers this summer. They ill 'in the localities behind Piccadilly which centre frok)bo,a quarter schich receives more attention 41 the police than any part of London.</p>
        <p>be Commander of the Metropolitan Fire Brigade o' Man of inventive faculty, and has already made ler to the County Council some improyeraPnts of relit utility in connection with the brigade. Ills .!test idea is to utilise the telephone on the occa- ,,'lM of fires. He proposed that every engine should a telephone, which should be screwed to one tcif the street alarm pots in the neighbpurhold of 'lle arc He has invented a plan, also, fer attaching zue instrument to the post. The Post Office has eacked the idea up by undertaking to provide the telephones, and to adapt the posts to Captain Wells' Contrivance. By this means, instead of sounding mere electric alarm to summon engines from rationshe would be able to put himself in specific Icitantaneous communication with any or all the engine sta. ions in the metropolis, and to give rata the neces.ary instructions as to the assistance ja4 e requires. lI wiil thus have his whole force in 411 d, at almost a mo;nerit's notice.</p>
        <p>--- ,Ths Camberwell Vcstry is organising a crusaoe niainSt "condensed separated milk," and according to the Clerk (Mr. Tagg) nearly all the public health Illithorities of the kingdom are willing t.) join in 4PPeal to Parliament on the subject. The great Ostrict of Camberwell itself,wbere infant mortality ;IS for some time been mysteriously high, is being Vaded by the Vestry with warnings against this delthetto popular food. To feed babies on" con- separated milk,, the Vestry says, is to kill it-e!ln by starvation, in proof of which a,sertion the u"lsority points to the testimony of Mr. F. L. Teed, riblic analyst. That gentleman, having tested ealaPles taken by the Vestry, came to the startling ellaclusion that ten half-pound tins contained only let ranch fat as a pint of new milk. Dr. Frank strs)medical officer of health, makes a still more a --'ag statement—namely, that a child three or niantlis old would need %lb of the best" con- e —sed seParated milk " and 211 b of the cheapest ttiv)eorrY d ay ; and yet 20 per cent of the Camberwell „Is 118 e it. What the Vestry proposes is that on shall be a label saying how much of the ~.lents would be equivalent to a pint of new mill . Eze ---- the aPt, for a slight decline in the year's incom.3 .annual report of the Church Pastoral Aid Society tlegettlat is being sent out this week is a satisfactory tk ent.Though the income is less,the work done bte„'"3,_ society is greater, as regards some of its : yee'eues. The society has not, during the last eight zs of its existence, maintained as many lay trzits as it did last year. Their number amount ed to 1,.,,,z) fewer than 143. It alz-o granted assis!ance the s'l Curates and 70 women-workers throughout iheocanntry. About a fourth part of the society's jEI spent in Dincashire. The income for the cn,et came to £42,600 and of this, Lancashire ' Live,.",lay agents,and women-workers got £lO,OOO. , yeeei4 i )001 contributed to the society £1,222, but t pre.ed grants amounting to .C 3332. The society 11011 keen elainently a Low Church organisation, and Lew alably the bulk of its income goes to support Clizzrak principles.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>GUNEPAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Over 80 per cent of the shipping of the world is built on the British Isles. -</p>
        <p>The heaviest man whose weight is recorded authentically weighed a little less than 8001bs. Bees are among the most difficult vegetables to be di- gested, requiring nearly four hours. • •</p>
        <p>„ The Island of Tobtgo was named ly.cause of its resemblance in shape to the tobago or pipe used by the naives. At Hereford on Monday Rdward Parker. an Innkeeper, was fined £25 and £5 17s costs for keeping a common gambling house.</p>
        <p>Tennyson " Lancelot" has been transforme I into a four- act lyric drama for the Paris opera by M. Victoria Soncieres.</p>
        <p>The largest known sapphire is the property of the Royal family of Germany. Is weighs 6 ounces and its value is in calculable.</p>
        <p>The tobacco supplied by the French Government factories to the consumers is said to be the worst known in the civil- ised world.</p>
        <p>On Monday the wife of a boiler maksr at Barry Dock de• liberately strangled herself by tying seine blind cord tightly round her neck.</p>
        <p>A youth named George Brown died at Loughborough on Monday night through colliding with a trap while cycling on the Leicester-road.</p>
        <p>The town of Jerome, Arizona, was destroyed by fire early on Monday m orning. Eleven persons are known to have lost th-ir lives ,and 30 are missing.</p>
        <p>Frederick George Nodal, died in Roshester Hospital on Monday, having had his sku'l fractured whilst fighting with a young man who knocked him down. The body of John T. Taylor, engineer, of Melville-street, Coventry, was found in a cave at Onchan, near Douglas, on Monday afternoon. The decetsed had cut his throat with a razor.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening the young eon of Mr. Alfred Powell, farm bailiff of Bilsbotrow,near Garstang, was filling a bladder with water at a big tank when ha overbalanced himself and was drowned.</p>
        <p>A terrible fire is reported from New Westminster, British Columbia. destroying. alcording to one account, over 300 buildings and rendering 10,033 people homeless. The mone- tary damage is very great. ()Sandler on Monday fell fro athe top stoA hirecyolfd named Chandler Buildings, In the Borough, London, a dis- tance of 100 feet, and was killed. This is the second fatality of the kind in the same buildings within a fortnight. gliThe body ef an unknown man was found on Monday on the railway near South Shields. It is believed he was a pas- senger from Newcastle to Sunderland on Sunday, and that failing to change at Tyne Dock ha left the train while in Action:ling to mail advices received at San Francisco from Honolulu a revolt has broken out in the Caroline Islands. Two of the leading chiefs united their forces and attacked 200 Spanish in Ponape. It is believed the latter were annihilated - • • --</p>
        <p>The official " Kallsruher Zietung " announces that Prince Max of Baden is betrothed to the Grand Duchess Helene Vladimirovna daughter of the Grand Duke Vladimir. The bethrothal took place at the Castle of Krasuoje Selo. near St. Petersburg.</p>
        <p>- _ Frederick Inwood, an ex-member of the Town Council, was fined 10s. and costs at Northampton on Monday for stealing meat from a butcher's stall. The defendant, who was at one time president of the Northampton Shoe Trade Council, pleaded drunkenness During the small hours of Monday morning the premises of Messrs. S. and G.Glase,who carry on busin-ss as jewellers, silversmiths, do., at Cathedral-street, Fennel-street, Man- chester were broken into, and silverware and watches taken to the value of £2OO. - -</p>
        <p>Mies Lily yenning was cycling at Bromley on Monday when her machine ran away with her. She was seriously injured, end now lies in a precarious condition. She was thrown violently against a lamppost, cutting her head and face and knocking out her teeth. The body of John Clarke, moulder, Crewe, was on Mon- dy under a bridge on the North-Western Liverpool :Meat Church Coppenhall. The body had e,idently been run over by several trains, and was terribly mutilated, both legs being cut off and the neck broken. A terrible explosion occurred at Philadelphia on Sunday by which three houses were blown up and completely des- troyed. There is no explanation at present fortha ming of the cause of the disaster. Four of the inmates of the houses were killed outright, 18 others were missing.</p>
        <p>Early on Monday afternoon a woman who carried a child in her arms and who was standing on the Liverpool Landing Stage, suddenly jumped into the river, taking with her the baby. Fortunately some workmen were in a boat a few yards away, and at once rescued th i woman and child. At an inquest held at Crewe on Monday, on the body of William Steen. of Bushbury, engine driver, who died from scalds re^eived through a collision on the London and North- western Railway between two goods trains on Thursday night last, the jury returned a verdict of death from misadventure. The" History of Modern Times," which Lord Acton is editing for the Cambridge University Press, will extend to twelve volumes. The first is expected to be randy in the early months of the new century; thereafter a volume will he issued each succeeding spring until the series is com- pleted. A hurricane was blowing over the Caribbean Sea on Sun- day, and was reported to be severe at Barbadoes and St. Vin- cent, the cable being interrupted to both islands. There was a terrible thunderstorm, with continuous lightning all night but no damage was done beyond the sinking of a few small craft in the harbour of Port of Spain.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held a Northampton on Monday on the body of a young man named Frederick Coles, aged 24, who when bathing in the rit•er near his home on Suradsy was seized with cramp and was drowned in the presence of several boys, who were unable to render assistance. The jury found a verdist of ac 'Mentally drowned. Mr. Price, the county coroner, held an inquest on Monday at Patricroft on the body of William Taylor. a railway brakeman, who was fatally injured in the course of his employment last Wednesday. He was engaged marshalling trains, when he slipped, and a waggon passed over him. The jury returned a verdict of accidental death. 1</p>
        <p>- - On Monday morning, William Woodcock, aged 55, whole- sale fish dealer, of Blackburn committed suicide in a most determined manner. He got up very early, and when his son came downstairs he found his father lying in the back yard with his throat cut. A penknife was by his side, while another knife in the kitchen was covered with blood. Mr. Duncan M'Gillivray, a young man of 24, who was ern_ ployed as season telephonist at the Observatory on the sum- mit of Ben Nevis, went to Fort William on Saturday to take part in avolunteer shooting competition. In the evening he started for the summit of the mountain, and on Monday morning his body was found by some tourists lying near the bridle path. A shocking accident occurred at Bridlington Quay, on the Yorkshire coast, on Saturday. A party of townspeople had gone out in a boat shooting sea birds, when the gun of a young man named George Matthew Shaw (whose father, Francis Shaw, had lent them the boat) exploded accidentally, and the charge. salking the owner, blew th 3 front part of his head off. Death washastantaneous. At Manchester Police Court, George Partridge, of Harpurhey, was charged with stealing a number of postal orders, the property of his employers, Messrs, Abel Heywood and Sons, publishers, Oldham-street. When apprehended the prisoner said." I thought it would come to this • I am very sorry, and have been very foo ish. I have been drink- and betting." Thl prisoner was remanded. Henry Eagles, a private in the 6th Inniskilling Dragoons stationed at Dundalk and Belfast, has been sentenced to three years' penal servitude and afterwards to be discharged with ignominy from the service. He was found guilty by court-martial of striking his superior officer, using threaten- ing language to him, and off ering him violence. The sentence was confirmed by the Commander-in-Chief Messrs. BrOckbank and Dorrington have succeeded in imparting a great variety of beautiful hues and tints to various flowers by simply immersing their cut stalks in aniline solutions. With a microscope the coloured water can be watched flowing through the vein tubes in the leaves, petals and other parts of the flowers. If aniline dyes are placed in the soil they do not find their way through the roots. Mrs. Frances Hodgson Burnett's divorce has had an enex- pected result, a writer in the" Publishers' Weekly "tells us. She wishes to be known as Frances Hodgson, but her pub- lishers say that under any new name she would have to win fame anew, as the reading public knows not Mrs. Frances Hodgson. The name of a well-known writer Carries with it a real money value, hence her publishers insist that she must retain in some way that under which she won her reputation. A gossip says that Miss Olive Schreiner was once upon a time" unconsciously in possession of a small fortune." Among the playthings of her childhood wa a bright stone, about the size of a walnut, which she and her brothers named the candle-stone, on account of the beautiful rays it emitted when held to the light. As time went by, the candle-stone was lost sight of, and not until she had grown up did she realise that this plaything was a diamond of immense value. Quite recently a member of an old-established and very reputable firm of discount brokers retired. A few nights ago this gentleman invited his late staff to dine with him (Says the "Estates Gazette "), and after handsomely regaling them presented each one with a sealed envelope with the request that the opening of the same would be deferred until each left the house When the packets were opened the equivalent of £l,OOO was found in each. Such an instance of generosity eleserves to be recorded. The Russian Minister of War has ordered aluminium horse- shoes for the cavalry. The first trial is now being made by the Finland dragoons, and the horses are shod as follows : A shoe of the new aluminium model is placed on one foot, the three remaining shoes being of the kind now in use. The trial, which has already lasted six weeks, seems to be satisfactory, as the aluminium wears out less rapidly arid is less affected by mud and moisture than the ordinary shoe. The difference In weight is about seventy grams (21 ounces) on each shoe. The cost is not greater at present, and will be less RS soon as the system of manufacturing the new shoes shall have been perfected. A boating accident, resulting in two deaths, occurred on the Ouse at Bedford on Saturday night. Two London asphalters, named James Murphy and William Hunt, accompanied by Robert Clifford, a Bedford friend. went for a row on the river. An attempt to chiNge seats upset the boat. Murphy sank at once, and Clifford was rescued little the worse for the immersion. but Hunt was unconscious when got out. Artificial respiration was resorted to with success,and he was able to stand and thank his rescuers. Ha declined the use of the ambulance, and was allowed to walk to the police station,half a mile distance. Outside the office he showed signs of collapse, however, and he became uncon- scious on being got inside,and expired within a few minutes</p>
      </div>
