      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WARRINGTON PARENTS CHARGED</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARENTS CHARGED</p>
        <p>wirir CRUELTY.</p>
        <p>AN INCORRIGIBLE SON. SINGULAR CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday morning, before Mr. Monks (in the Chair) and other magistrates, John Caldwell and Martha Caldwell, 44, Back Dallam-lane, were summoned for neglecting their son, George Alfred Caldwell, in a manner likely to cause him unnecessary suffering.—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., and the defendants were repre- sented by Mr. Grainger. Mr. Browne mid the boy was the son of the male de- fendant and the stepson of the female. He was twelve years of age, and for seine months or years had been grossly neglected. He was suffering from two wounds on the leg, which Dr. Bowden would describe, and tell the bench that in consecoence of neglect the wounds had festered. They also complained that the lad not been properly fed. His parents had made charges against him of having stolen things out of the house, and had turned him out. A witness named Thorley would say that he bad given the lad food. He could else give evidence WS to the lad's Serious condttion. Inspector McAteer's attention was drawn to the lad's condition, and he called in Dr. Bow- den, who examined the boy and sent him to the work- house infirmary. The mother afterwards went there and took him out. The male defendant was a moulder, earning 38s a Week: They did not complain very much against him, but they did say that he should have seen the lad was attended to by his wife. George Alfred Caldwell said he had lived at 44, Back Dallam-lane, about a month. He had teen working at Cunningham's, but in consequence of the pain in his leg he could not stand it and came away. He did not go home, because if he had done he would have been "laid on." He went to live with his cousin Mary Ellen Gosling, and remained at her house two weeks. He got his food from a shoemaker named Thorley. Mr. Thorley had been very gelid to him, and had been giving him " meat" for a long time. Mrs. Russell had a'so been goed to him, having given him a jacket and vest. The Inspector got Dr. Bowden to examine his leg, and he was sent to the workhouse infirmary lest Thursday ' but one. His stepmother came there and took him out. I He had scarlet fever about four years ago, and it left dropsy. Be had had the wounds on his leg since that ' time.—Cross-examined : His mother had supplied oint- ment and bandages, and he had bandaged the wounds himself. One time his mother gave him some money to buy ointment,and he spent the money and stayed out all night. He stayed one night at the house of a Mr.Meff at, and in the morning took has watch. A Mrs. Douglas re- primanded him for doing so, and he returned the watch to her. He had pawned things to get" meat." He had stayed out at nights sometimes, and on some occasions had stayed away from home for weeks at a time. Inspector McAteer said he visited the shop of Mr. Thorley, and there saw the boy. He found the wounds on his leg to be very bad. He called in Dr. Bowden,and the lad was removed to the workhouse infirmary. Wit- nese afterwards went to the parents' house. The father said that the boy was beyond control, and both parents said he was a Thief and a liar. Mr. Grainger: Is it not your duty when undertaking a prosecution on b helf of your society to investigate the case on both sides ? Witness': Yes.—Mr. Grainger: Did you not consider when Mrs. Caldwell asked you to go upstairs and leek a the boy's bedroom that it was your duty to do so ? Witness: The bedroom had nothing te do with Monks : That is not an answer to the qnestion.—Wit- nes% : I did not consider it necessary.—Mr. Grainger : Did not Mrs Caldwell tell you the lad was incorrigable ? Witness : Yes. The lad told me he took the shirt and pawned it because he had not had any food for two days. Mary Ellen Gosling, Back Oliver-street, sail the lad was brought into her house on the night of the 18th of August. Re had been found asleep on a doorstep. She gave him shelterond he stayed at the house a week. He said his parents bad turned him out. Dr. Bowden said he was called in on the let Sep- tember to examine the boy,and found two large inflamed ulcers on his leg. The wounds were covered with some rags, and were in a very foul neglected condition. The wounds would cause him a great deal of pain,and would injure his health. They could be cured, but the lad would need rest. The boy was very ill-clad. Mr. Grainger submitted that the lad was entireiy incorrigible. His parents had dole everything they could for him, and had given him food and attended to his wounds. He was, however, continually running awap from home. and on some occasions stayed away for weeks together. When at home he stayed out until twelve o'clock. His father had tried kindness with him, and had administered chastisement, but it had proved of no avail. As he said, the lad was incorrigible. On one occasion he had stolen a watch, and he had taken things out of his parents' house and had pawned them. The parents were more sinned against than sinning. Martha Caldwell gave an account of the visit of the Inspector. She asked him to go upstairs to look at the boy's bed, and he said it was not required. She asked</p>
        <p>asked him to see the boy's father,and be said he did not want to. She said he must. The father then came into the room,and in answer to the Inspector's questions he said he could do no good with the lid. He could not keep him in at night,though he had done his best. Wit. nem; told the officer that the lad had taken things out of the house and pawned them. He said he did not want to listen to that. Subsequently witness went to the ww ao workhouse hs niorouets e t going oo go po borne. i t a t, aon d tboeol Whent;ba side the tl eaodnuet he said he out. He expressed ad and ran u sway. His sister afterwards brought him home.— Mr. Monks: Why did you take him out of the hospital? —Witness : Because I could nurse him myself. Theresa Morris, 48, Back Dallam-lane,said the parents food. dalways treated the boy kindly, an I had given him d. John Caldwell, the father, said he had done every- thing he could for the lad, but he was incorrigible. Eizabeth Mains said she had heard Mrs. Caldwell try to persuade the lad to be good, and not to run away. The boy had been treated very kindly by both his father and mother. _ _</p>
        <p>Jane Douglas, 13, Earl-street, said when she accused the lad of baying stolen the watch belonging to Mr. Mof- fat heslenied the theft. She threatened to call a police- man to him, and he then gave her the watch. That was two years ago. The Chairman mid the law did not allow the magic- trateS to deal with the case as they should like to, but the father could do so. They suggested to him that he should allow the lad to be taken back to the workhouse hospital and arrange for his maintenance. When he was cured—and it would require more attention than defendants had given him —they must bring him back to the court, and he would be sent to the training ship Clio. Re (the Chairman) Was quite satisfied that that was the best thing to d). The magistrates believed thas the lad was not entirely bad, and they were anxious that he should be rescued from drifting into crime, Or rather that condition in which crime was made easy..</p>
        <p>- Caldwell promised to comply with the wishes of the Bench, and on that underst%nding the cafe was dis- missed.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week Ending 3rd September 1898. During the week the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington Registration District 44 33 The excess of births over deaths was U. The Birth Rate was 36.4 per 1,000 of population. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 273 per 1,000, against 202 the average of the previous 4 weeks, and against 23.5 the corresponding period of last year. The death. rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 182 per I,ooo,against 6.5 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks, and against 9.2 the corresponding period of 1897. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 23 3 1 5 1 33 The deaths included-1 from scarlet fever. 1 from whooping cough, 20 from diarrhcea, 1 from lung dis- ease, 1 from heart disease, 1 from injuries, 8 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week:—Enteric fever, 2 eases. Total number of patients in hospital for week ending, September 3rd, 8. METEOROLOGY—The mesa temperature was 571 Fah., and therefore 3 deg. lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 711?., was recorded on the 3rd inst., and the minimum temperature 46F., on the 2nd inst. The rainfall measured '37 inch. The general direction was S.W. the reading of the anemometer denoting 960 miles. _ _</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>Fever Hospital, Atkin Street, Warrington Report for the month ending 31st August, 1898 In Hospital on the Ist of August 4 Admitted during the month Cases of Scarlet Fever (Warrington) ... 8 Other Diseases</p>
        <p>, Total l3</p>
        <p>Discharged Cured 2 Dead In Hospital on the Ist of September lO</p>
        <p>Total l3</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Superintendent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 16, 1.898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>BEVISING THE LISTS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BEVISING THE LISTS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Thomas Foard, barrister-at-law, held an evening sitting at the Town Hall, Warrington, on Thursday, for the purpose of revising the lists of Parliamentary electors, of burgesses, of county elec- tors And of paro.thill electors--Hr. Clayton repro salted the Liberals, and Mr. Holbrook was present on behalf of the Conservatives. There were also present :—Mt. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. J. L, Tunstall (assistant overseer), Mr. Joseph -Smith (Clerk to the assistant overseer), Mr. T. J. Garnett and Mr. F. H. Aukland (relieving officers), and Ser- geant Chantler. A LIBERAL OBJECTION. The first claim heard was that made by the Conserva- tives on behalf of Catherine Sherratt, of 53, Fox-street, and formerly of 38, Green-street.—ln answer to ques- tions put by Mr. Clayton the applicant stated that when she resided in Green-street she was in the employ of Mr. Boardman, chip potato dealer, and she carried on the business there for him.—Mr. Clayton, addressing the revising barrister said his objection to the claim was that she was in a service occupation at Green-street an:. was therefore not entitl.rl to the vote. She was not the tenant.—The Revising Barrister held that there was a tenancy.—Mr. Clayton: Will you reserve your decision on the point until to-mo,raw's sitting ?—Thn Revising Barrister : I don't think there is any difficulty. It appears to be good enough,but:l will mention the case at a future sitting.</p>
        <p>HE WANTED THE VOTE. The Liberals made a claim on behalf of Michael Hyland, 91, Cherley-street.—ln answer to Mr. Holbrook the applicant admitted that he had not sent in the claim ; personally. If be had done so he would not have given the name as " Michael Ireland."—Mr. Holbrook con- tended that under the eircuinstances the applicant was not entitled to suceeed.—The Revising Barrister ; Do you want the vote ; that is the question ?—Applicant Yes ; I want to have a vote.—The Revising Barrister : You sanction the claim that has been sent in for you a'though your name have been spelled wrong ?—Yes— The Revising Barrister : The claim is allowed. "NOT GOOD ENOUGH." The Liberals objected to the vote of Walter Brown, 7, ; Prince-street.—Mr. Clayton: When did you go to live at Prince.street?—Applicant : In April.—And where did ' you remove from ?—Marbury-street P—Where did you ' live prior to being at 16, Marbury-street 2-4, New Road, Letchford.—Yes and who was the tenant ?—I was.—Wasn't Mr. Speakman the tenant ?—No.—Mr. Clayton (to the Barrister): Mr. Speakman is on the lists for that address. The applicant was only lodging there.— The Revising Barrister (to applicant) : Who was the tenant at 4, Old-road P—Witness : Mr. Speakman turned the house over to me in September, 1896 —The Revising Barrister : Have you any evidence P—Witness: Here is the rent book.—The Revising Barrister (after examining the rent book sad the voters list). This claim is not good enough. The :marce of the tenant in the rent book is written in pencil whilst that of Speakman is in ink. Walter Brown comes off the list. THE VOTE GOES TO THE LICENSEE. James Rushworth applied for a vote in respect of the Rose and Crown Inn, Bridge-street.--In reply to Mr. Clayton's questions, Mr. Rushworth admitted that Ms wife was the liceneee:—Mr. Clayton said that was his objection, and contended that it had been ruled where the wife was the licensee the husband could not claim the vote.—The Revising Barrister said that was so.—Mr. Holbrook reminded the Revisinglßarrister that when the late Mr. Kiernan of the Patten ArM3 Hotel applied for a vote it was granted. and yet the license of the hotel was in his wife's name.—The Revising Barrister : Wasn't there a case afterwards arising suggesting that I had gone beyond the length of my tether P—Mr. Hol- brook : I suggest that the claim be allowed to stand over.—The Revising Barrister : I will make a note of the case, and if you can show me a decision in support of the claim I will reconsider the matter. I must strike this out for the present. A LIBERAL OBJECTION UPHELD. Albert Edward Longshaw. 89, Dallam-lane, applied for a vote in respect of a workshop in Dallam-lane.--In reply to the Revising Barrister. Mr. Tunstall said the as- sessable value of the property in question was £6 103.— Mr. Clayton said there were already two voters on the lists for this workshop,one was a brother to the present claimant, and the other his stepfather—Mr. Wilbam J. Shaw. The stepfather was the occupier of the premises adjoining the vearkshop.—Mr. Clayton (to witness) : Is not this workshop immediately behind the beerhouse ? It is close to it but it is under a different landlord.—The Revising Barrister : This claim is not good enough ap- parently. Mr. Holbrook: He is not applying for a Par- liamentary vote.—To applicant : Are you joint partners in this business 2—Yes we are joint partners in the painting business.—By Mr. Clayton : Are you an equal Partner ?—Yes.--Have you any deed of agreement ? No.—You cannot p-oduce any evidence to show that you participate in the profits P—No. I work in the business. —I suppose what you get fr in your stepfather is in the shape of pocket money 2-- Yes. Mr. Clay- ton (to the Barrister): It is simply a case of the son working for the father.— The Revising Barrister : Does your brother Frank stand in the same position as yourself in connection with the business P—Applicant : He looks after the boeks.—The Revising Barrister : He acts for the stepfather who carries on the business. The c'aim is disallowed. LODGER CLAIMS. The Liberals supported the lodger claim made by Percy Jones, Frognall-lane.--By Mr. Holbrook: What rooms do you occupy ?—A sitting-room and bedroom,— Doee any person occupy them with you ?—No.—Does your brother Frank use them P—No ;heis in Liverpool. —How many rooms ate there in the house ?—Nine.—Do you swear that no one sleeps with you in the bedroom ? —Yes.—The Revising Barrister : You have the entire and exclusive use of this sitting-room ?—Yes. It is open during the day when lam out.—By Mr: Clayton : But you have the sole use of toe room ?—Yes.—The claim was allowed.—The Conservatives made a eimilar claim for a lodger vote on behalf of John Thomas Robinson, 3, Hanover-street.—The claim was allowed. THE TESTAMENT BROUGHT INTO USE. John Emery claimed a vote as tenant of a dwelling. house at Knowles-yaid.—By Mr. Clayton ; What age are you ?-22.—ViThert d d you go to Ktiowlese-yard ? On the sth of August. 1897,—Wasn't it in December ? No.—Where did you live before you went there ?—At 3, Silver-square.—Didn't you live in lodgings for some time before: you went to Knowles-yard 2—No.— Mr Clayton (to the Revising Barrister) : My information is that the applicant was in lodgings before ' going to Knowles'-yard. I have .an aversion to putting the man on his oath.—The Revising Barrister : This is a case in which he had better be Swum. (To applicant :) Take the testament. After having been sworn the Barrister said : Will you swear that you didn't go into lodgings between your oceu- pency of Silver-square and Knowles'S yard?—Yes.—You didn't go into lodgings ?—No.--The Barrister : All right the vote is allowed.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. On Friday morning Mr. Foard continued his work of revision at the Town Hall. THE TENANT OF P. FURNISHED DWELLING HOUSE. The Lib :rats made a claim on behalf of James Liddell, of Glass-house Square.—ln answer to Mr. Holbrook' Mrs Liddell stated that the landlord of the house was Mr. Daley, and it was let to them furnished.—The Revising Barrister : Is there an entrance from the landlord's house to yoors?—Witness : No ; it is a self-contained cottage.—The Revising Barrister : Aim ! yes ; that is what you have been told to say (Laughter). I want to know whether the landlord can get in without going outside his house P—Witness No, no one can get in except through ths front and back doors.—Mr. Holbrook : These are really furnished apart- ments.—The Revising Barrister: Are they furnished ready for the tenants ?—Mr. Holbrook : Yes.—The Revising Barrister : This is a very nice point. (To the Assistant Overseer) : Is there anyone on the list for this house ?—No.—Mr. Holbrook : The landlord lives close to. They are complete houses but they are let to the tenants in a ftrnishecl condition.—The Revising Barrister : That doesn't Make it any the less a dwelling. house.—Mr. Holbrook : I lo ok upon these persona as being in lodgings when the landlord supplies the fur- niture.—The Revising Barrister : It makes no differ ence as far as I can see. It 19 a separate dwelling- house. Still it is is an unusual case, but in my opinion the mere fact that the landlord supplies the furniture does not alter the conditi, no.—Mr.Holbrook(to svitnws): What do you pay ?-4s. Eid. a week.—The Revioing Barrister : No one also lives with the family The vote is allowed. EXPENSES &amp;MED FOR AND REFUSED. The Liberals objected to the vote of Samuel Smith, 4, Tinsley Terrace.—Mr. Smith was represented by hii stepson, Mr. Lingard.—By Mr. Clayton ; Where is Mr. Smith now ?—Re is at Liverpool ?—Has he given you any authority to appear for him ?—Yes he was at home at the week-end, and asked me to come and look after his vote.—When did he leave this house and go to Liverpool P—He has not left.—Are not you, Mr. Lingard the tenant P—No.—Whose furniture is it at Tinsley's Terrace P—lt is mine.—When doss he go to Liverpool ? —He is away during the week and comes there at the week end. He is the tenant of the house.—The Revising Barrister : The objection cannot be alio wed.—Mr. Lin- gard: I must ask for my expenses.—The Revising Barris- ter : This doesn't appear to b 3 a frivolous objection. I think we were bound to make these enquiries in a case t where a man has gone away.—Mr. Clayton : I made enquiries personally, and was told that Mr. Smith hod gone to Liverpool.—The Revising Barrister : 1 quite admit you had reason to aet. I cannot allow expenses in this case.</p>
        <p>THE _RENT-BOOT MISSING.</p>
        <p>-- The claim of Edward Gill, Ileathside, which was made the previous night and postponed so that the rent book might be produced in order to prove the date of the tenancy, was re-introduced.—Mr. Gill said they had lost the rent b3ok and he had been unable to find it.—Mr. Clayton said the case was thoroughly thrashed out the previous night and the Revising Bar- rister would not allow the applicant to go on the lists</p>
        <p>unless he produced his rent le eok.—The Revising Barris. tar said ne must disallow the claim. DISALLOWED. The Conservatives supported the claim of George Edward Mackie for avote in respect of a dwelling-house at 37, Froghall-lane.—ln answer to the questions of Mr. Clayton the witness said he was chiefly the support of the house,but admitted that his mother had independent means and was interest sd in cottage property.—The Barrister having satisfied himself that Mrs. Mackie had a vote for certain property disallowed the claim. ems LIBERAL AGENT AND CAPTAIN HAWES. Mr. Holbrook asked permission for Captain Hawes, of Orford Barracks, to appear at the next sitting of the Court, as he was unable to be present that dey.—Mr. Clayton said he could not understand ahy Captain Hawes, who was an officer appointed by the War Office to furnish a list of those at Orford Barracks entitled to vote, did not do so. Their lists were incomplete aed they had no means of testing them as neither overseers nor agents were allowed to visit the Barracks.—The Revising Barrister said that could not ba considered an objection to the attendance of Captain Hawes on the following day. Mr. Clayton would be entitled to cross- examine the Captain when he appeared respecting the list which should be fureished.—Mr. Clayton : I had occasion to raise the same objection last year. SATURDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. At the Town Elsie Werrington, on Saturday morning, Mr. J. T. Fdar 1, barrister-at-law, continued his work of revision of the voters lists. The Liberals were again re- presented by Mr. Clayton, the Liberal agent, and Mr. Holbrook appeared on behelf of the Conservatives. AN INSURANCE SUPERINTENDENT'S VOTE. Thomas R. Ashcroft claimed a vote in respect of pre- mises at 135, Sankey-street. In answer to Mr. Clayton, applicant said he was local superintendent of the London Frieedly Society,ani the premises were occupied by him as a tenant—Mr. Clayton: Were not the offices taken by the society ? They have a lease of them and lam the tenant.—You are a servant,are you slot? I am the tenant.—You are a servant and you have to live on the premises to perform your duties ? I can please myself ; I could go somewhere else if I pleased.—The Revising Barrister said Mr. Ashcroft appeared to be the tenant. —The vote was allowed. THE REVISING BARRISTER AND A "MANUFACTURED VOTE." Herbert Hayes, Leigh Villas, Grappenhallsroad, an assistant to Mr. Holbrook, the Conservative avant, claimed a vote in respect af a room rented by him at the Conservative Club.—The Revising Barrister (to Mr. Clayton) : Is not your point in this case that the room is not worth £lO a year P—Mr. Clayton : It is not worth that.—Mr. Hayes: lam only claiming a vote under Division 3; not a Parl am:eatery vote.—Mr. Clay- ton (to applicant) : As a matter of fact do you pay any rent for the room 2—Yes.—What de you occupy the room with ?—I have occupied the room for four years and ute it as a storeroom.—Mr. Clayton (to the Revising Barrister) : He simply uses the room as a storeroom for decorative materiel. It is not an office. Mr. Clayton (to witness) : You don't do anything in it? Ido plenty of thin es In it.—How much do you pay for it ?—A sovereign a year (the applicant here produced the receipt,—Mr. Clayton (to the Revising Barrister) : That is less than sixpence a week,—(to Mr. Tunstall, the assistant overseer) : Is this storeroom rated ?—Mr. Tunstall: No.—( o applicant) : In order to get into this room have you a private entrance ; can you get in without going through the main entrance? I go through the usual club entrances.—What time does the club close ? Eleven o'clock—Have you a key to the front door ? I have a key to the side door.—Have you a right of admittance at aay hour of the night ? I have ; I can enter when I lik a—The Revising Barrister : He can if he has a key,—Mr. Clayton: He keeps that for the sole purpose of assisting in their registration work.— The Revising Barrister : It is clear that this is a vote of Mr. Holbrook's manufacture. (Laughter). He has, , however, made it out.—Mr. Clayton: If you allow this you open a very wide door.—The Revising Barrister Unf ertunately whatever laws you make some one will be found to cresp out or througn them, That would be found the whole country through, but in this particular case I do not think it will disturb the English constitution very much. There is no doubt it is a manufactured vote, and there is no doubt it has been manufactured properly. —Mr. Clayton: And you intend to allow it?—Toe Re- vising Barrister : How can Ido otherwise ? He pro• duces a receipt for the rent and says he has this room , for his sole occupation and exclusive use. You say thatl he has no right to enter the premises after the club is closed and he establishes that fact by producing the key which enables him t enter the building at any time.— Mr. Clayton: I say the key is for the purpose of getting into the office fsr r -gestration purposes, and not for this room.—The Revising Barrister : If he has get a key for one purpose you cannot prevent him using it for the second purpose.—He has no continuous occupation.—The Revising Barrister He says no hes the key.—Mr. Clay. ton Yes for another purpose.—The Revising Barrister: I sheul I not be inclined to allow the vote in the smallest degree if I could help it but I cannot do anything else than allow it in this case. MONDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. The revision was resumod in Ni. 1 committee room of the Town Hall this morning, at ten o'clock, by Mr. Fcard. The overseers were represented by Mr. J. L. Tunstall, tbe Liberals by Mr. Clayton, and tne Conser- vatives by Mr. William Holbrook. SOLDIERS STRUM( OFF THE LISTS. At Saturday's sitting of the Court the Revising:Bar- rister struck off the lists a number of officers who occupy rooms at Orford Barracks owing to the evidence of the length of occupation being inadsquats.--Mr. Clayton said there were already others officers on the lists for the same rooms.—Colour-Sergeant Edridge said he was not aware that the date of occu- pation was necessary or he would have come with full partic liars. With the permission of the Revising Barrister he would furnish them at the next Bitting of the Court.—The Revising Barrister said he would allow him the privilege—Accordingly at the final revision on Monday the officer attended and stated that one of the officers struck off had bout in occupation of a room at the Barracks so far back as IBM—Mr. Clayton pointed out that there was another officer on the list for the same ro ,m,and who should therefore have been struck off at the Revision Caurt in 1897. He com- plained of the lax manner in which the information from the Barracks was given.—The Revising Barrister: Some- body is irregular in furnishing the lists, (To the Officer) : Who is the officer who is supposed to have the duty cast upon him to give these names ? It is not a duty but Captain Howes has been kind enough to supply tha information.—Mr. Clayton : The Assistant Overseer bas a note from the War Office stating fleet the authori- ties at the Barracks would supply the information.—Mr. Tunstall : We have received a letter from the officer in charge stating that in future if they would call at the Barracks an officer woull supply the information.—The Revising Barrietni : If it was deputed to an officer he would see the necessity of getting the lists correctly.— Mr. Clayton : Unfortunately this happens year after year.—The Revising Barrister : No daunt the matter will be brought before the notice of the officer in charge by the officer present. —The Revising Barrister then altered the lists with respect to several officers, but the majority of those struck off on Saturday were found to be disqualified by shortness of occupation. Mr. Clayton, at the conclusion of the revision at 12.30 on behalf of the Libnal party,said he wished to express his thanks t) the Revising Barrister for the great kind- ness and courtesy he had shown to all who had appeared before him, an] for the attentive manner in which he had lietened to the various claims and objections which ha] been made. He hoped that the good feeling which had existed for So many years between Mr. Foard and themselves would continue. Mr. Holbrook also thanked the Barrister on behalf of , the Conservatives. Mr. Foard, in reply, remarked that the lists were very carefully preparel,and seemed to give him the minimum of trouble. Of course that state of things could not ex- ist without the co-operation of all persons connected with the revision. Had it not have been for the hearty co-operation of the overseers his trouble would have been greater. His duties had been very light, and the revision bad been a very pleasant one. Mr. Tunstall said he thanked the Revising Barrister for his kind expressions. The overseers certainly devoted every possible attention to their work, and endeavoured to make the lists a: correct as possible. If they attained any measure of success they were glad to receive the approval of the Barrister. The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLILFE P.S.A. SOCIETY. Not only was Sunday's meeting an " open "one but it was also the half-yearly prize distribution when several hundreds of valuable books were handed to these mem- 13,rs who had fulalled the conditions which entitled them to become possessed of one. There was a large attendance, the church being filled: Mr. T. Birchall, J.P., occopied the chair, and in the course of his address, he referred to two recent cases which had CDMB uuder his notice during his magisterial duties, and said that when he saw so many just entering upon life he felt anxious to speak a few words of caution to them of the dangers that lay be- fore them. A great many of the mistakes and downfalls in life canoe thrhugh starting life on a wrong basis. There was a false idea prevalent amongst some people that their enly object in life was to live for their own gratification and pleasure. He pro- ceeded to refer to the example of Christ, who lived not for Himself but for otirrs,and asked his hearers not only to get the best out of life for themselves but to assist others.—The Rev. F. G. West, of Liverpool, was the speaker, and bascel an exceedingly able and interesting address upon some of the lessons " Taught in xeßelulennytOn enderimz's Holy War." Mrs. R. H. Pearson gave an of the difficult solo "I will exalt excellent Thee 0 God" and Mr. R. H. Pearson contributed a violin solo. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ: The Rev. John Yonge announced that the membership for the new half-year had now reached 710. The service was also taken part in by Mr. Healey and Mr. William Beswick, jun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SUGGESITD CENTRAL CLASSES FOR PUPIL TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>SUGGESITD CENTRAL CLASSES FOR PUPIL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the instigation of the Committee of the Pupil Teachers Central Classes, a general meeting of managers and the head eachers of day schools was held in No. 1 Committee Rom at the Town Hall, on Friday night. The circular convening the meeting stated that the pupil teachers central classes held at the Training College, had been in existence nearly five years, and had done goo 1 work, but as there had been such a large lir:Tease in the number attending, now over 60, tt was necessary either to increase the staff, or make some fulther arrange- ment f3r these clisses which might be of a perma- nent nature, There was a representative attend- ance. The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) was voted to the chair. _ .</p>
        <p>In opening the proceedings, the Chairman said they were called together to consider a matter of very great importance. Lie was strongly in favour of central day classes at Warrington. He knew there were great difficulties but the a 'vantages were very great. It was heir bounden duty to try and prepare teachers for the future, and provide instruction for them to the best of their power. He had come to the conclusion that they ought to have central classes for several reasons. First of all, he was in favour of them in the interests of the pupil teachers themselves, particularly the girl, who at a critical time were sadly in danger of being over- worked. He was very anxious that their p,esent hard lot should be lightened as much as polsible. His second reason was that central classes were in the interests of the head teachers. Anything they could do as an economy of time and labour for the head teachers they ought to do. Head teachers, with a large staff of pupil teachers had a great deal of work to do, and when they had to provide for a year's course for pupil teachers in addition to their other work it was very hard upon them. If man- agers of schools could strengthen their staffs so as to enable the pupil teachers to attend central classes it ought to be done. He would rather do that than have a double staff of pup I teachers. At Wigan there were 200 pupil teachers at the central classes and the cost was about £5OO a year. He thought they might raise that amount in Warring- ton. If 100 pupil teachers paid £2 each that would bring'in £2OO, and then they might count upon £2OO from the pupil teachers' grant. Then if they were to have £5O in subscriptions and .£5O for books that would be £5OO which would enable them to provide very well so far as the money was con- cerned With regard to the place he was not sure whether it would be well to have one or two centres. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON princ'pal of the Warrington Training College, referred to the in- crease in the number of pupils attending the central classes which had been held at the College and stated that a difficulty had arisen in con- ducting such large classes at the Training College. He thought the time had come when they should seriously consider whether they ought not to follow the lead of other towns and or- ganize a regular pupil teachers' centre for the pupil teachers of tlie town. The rev, gentleman proceeded to refer to the pressing necessity of such a centre so that the pupil teachers of the town might enjoy the same advantages, the same helps and the same privileges as those in other towns. Mr. CHARLES Twrss, headmaster of People's College, said a difficulty had been ex- perienced in recent years in getting. their pupil teachers at Warrington placed an the Training Colleges. He believed it was due to a want of teaching which was given in other towns at the central classes which had been organised. Their pupil teachers in Warrington used to do splendidly but they were now getting behind .hand, and he thought school managers cright to do every- thing po;sible to put them on a right footing. Mr. Joule Dewtrunsr, headmaster of the National Schools, spoke of the disadvantages to the pupil teachers which might arise by taking them from und-r the influence of the head teachers, especially in the art of teaching. He would rather have a good teacher who was a good disciplinarian and very fair scholar than a clever scholar and a poor disciplinarian. He thought £2 a year would be too large a sum for pupil teachers to pay for their instruction at the central clases, Mr. UPSON said that where Central Classes were formed for pupil teachers, the head teacher was made responsible for imparting knowledge in the art of teaching. Central Ciasses did not take the re-. sponsibility from the head teacher in that respect He noticed that at Be If ord Leigh where Central Classes for pupil teachers were being formed that , they were in connection with the Technical Com- mittee of the district. The Rev. H V. Pider spoke in favour of the for- mation of Central Classes. ' On the proposition of the Rev. MORLEY STEVEN-. ' SON se eoaded by Mr. CHARLES Twiss it was de- cided that a small sub-committee be appointed to , draw up a scheme for the formation of pupil , teachers Central Classes and submit it to a future meeting of managers and teachers.—lt was also agreed that the following gentlemen compose. the ' committee : Revs. Morley Stevenson,Canon John Yonge, Father Lucan, 11. V. Pigot, Miss Shaw, • Messrs. F. W. Monks, J. Dswhurst, C. TWiSS, J. D. Buckton, A. J. Jolley, and E. J. Guest.</p>
      </div>
