        <head>rrli EX A "Ail iNER.</head>
        <p>rrli EX A "Ail iNER.</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER Mb, 1893.</p>
        <p>THERE must be moments in Does Anarch'.sin the lives of individual Anar- PaY ? chists, and in the career of the organisations to which they ;:ro attached, when the question will arise as to whether the game is worth the candle. There must also be some pecun- iary inducements to reinforce the fanaticism they exhibit, and to maintain the directing authority intact. They need not be very considerable, but it is quite ;conceivable that some persons imagine that it is a paying game to be connected with or- ganisations which send out men to various capitals from time to time, not always on dangerous missions, except in so far as communications between revolutionary bodies always involve police surveillance and possible imprisonment. The practical effects of Anarchism as a whole can- not, we should think, very greatly impress the cool-headed leaders of the movements which make so much stir. There may be a feeling of intense excitement in entering into connection with An- archical associations. Probably it is as keen as anything human can be. The objects are vague, the opportunities are large, and the dangers are great. There are pass-words to learn, mysterious signs for identification, aud journeys of adventure of a kind that interest many young minds. The crimes to be committed involve months of steady preparation in some cases, and then they fail. A change in the notified route, a betrayal, and an arrest may destroy the work of many weeks and many minds. Then another plot is hatched, and the same thing goes on. During the past few years there have been three assassinations, but they by no means represent anything like a , fair percentage of successes in regard to attempts. Dozens of men are in prison who have been concerued in these abortive attempts. They have found anarchism to be any- thing but a paying game. Others who remain have to live the lives of hunted animals. They must get money from some source or other, but probably the amounts are small. One of the cap- tured:men, a short time ago, seemed to have been I sent forth on his errand with about £l5 in his possession. He lived at decent hotels, he was a gentleman at large, and he had to risk his life to commit a crime. He did the deed, and he was executed for it. In China there are said to be men who will submit to be imprisoned and even exe- cuted for a previous monetary payment, which they can spend in free living and enjoy- ment. Anarchism cannot be accounted for wholly in this way. But it is hardly possible to omit all references to the influ- ence of money. The Russian Government employs women to worm out the secrets of these gay An- archists, and they are planted out in the different capitals. But, on the broader view that the men are wholly inspired by fanaticism and a kind of madness, which makes them regard the mselves as heroes, there are still leaders, directors, arch- conspirators, who must sometimes doubt whether the time, money, trouble, and human lives they expend are worth the game. They achieve no- thing to which any effects can be ascribed. They have:undermined nothing. They have not converted the world. They have not accomplished anything which has bettered society. Society is as much against them to-day as it was when Anarchism began to orgnaise itself. Dagger and dynamite and pistol have conspicuously failed. Anarchism, in fact, is not a paying game.</p>
        <p>WE shall all agree that a mem- A Gorden orm . , i to GORDON at Khartoum Memorial. is absolutely imperative. With- out dwelling on the revenge theory in an un- pleasant way, we must remember that GORDON ruled the Soudan at one time, and ruled it wisely and well and that his second appearance at Khar- toum was in the nature of a desperate expedition in which the risk was excessive, and from which</p>
        <p>••••■6</p>
        <p>he might have prudently escaped had not fastidious notions of honour kept him at Khartoum after his mission had failed. A memorial to his memory at ' 1 Khartoum will be certain -No receive warm national support. It is proposed to found a college there, in which young men can be trained in practical arts. We should be inclined to favour the idea if there was as much common sense in it as some of its supporters seem to think there is. But we re- gard it as rather quixotic, because we do not think it meets any known want or will have any value as a memorial of the man. Where are •ti youths in Khartoum who want to be educated i technical arts ..? What will become of them whe they are so trained ? How will their subsequei careers benefit the Soudanese or contribute keep alive the memory of GoanoN? These are t questions that will arise, and have to be answel before the English people subscribe money for doubtful a project. It is surely not impossible to lay down one two lines upon which a Gordon , Memorial Khartoum should be established. It should be accord with the principles on which Gonnox hi' self acted. That is, it should be humane,it shoi relieve suffering, it should benefit the Soudar and it should lead them to cherish his memory that of a good man. The college, unless it be something of a missionary establishment, Ins appears to meet these points. A Gordon Hospii and Mission seems to be more suitable. It wol be necessary for it to treat Mussulmans and Cl tians alike, but not in the same building, and . by the same staff. The Mohammedans could ha their own buildings, nurses, and staff. Ti Christians should be treated in the same wa: whether they be Protostad or Catholic. Semi thing of this kind, absolutely gratuitous in its re. lief, and absolutely humane in regard to all creeds and races, would seem to be more befitting the man and the occasion than any educational establishment ideas. inuchas any generalitaappealsto appealourWestern Possibly, before w , made for money to establish a memorial to GORDON in Khartoum some representative body will fully discuss projects, and endeavour to select one that will be in every way suitable and satis- factory. A Soldiers EL-Aue in Cairo is one of O. projects which have been suggested, but it is gOO4 enough only so far as Cairo is concerned. At Khartoum something else is wanted, true to 60 lofty and comprehensive ideas and feelings which were the characteristics of the man we delight to honour. It will be no credit to us to rush forward with any scheme which may be suggested, even though high names may be attached to it.</p>
