        <p>Last week we made casual reference to the in- crease of insanity. Since then the annual report of the Commissioners in Lunacy has been issued and in it are contained some very important facts. It may be some comfort to those wno have felt 3 not unnatural alarm at the tendency of the age to mental instability, to learn that the increase of lunatics during the year 1897 has not been so large as it was in the preceding year, Id fact there CO been a decrease of something like 300 in the num- ber of people who have become insane during the twelve months reported upon, as compared with the year 1896. Tins is a matter for considerable satisfaction, inasmuch as the tendency is to send to the asylums eases which at one time would have been retained in the home or the workhouse. But, though the increase is not quite so great, it j 3 formidable enough to be disquieting. Between January Ist, 1897 and January Ist, 1898 the ranks of officially known lunatics have had 2,607 ' additions to them, which brings the total army of the insane to a maxinnun stthriesnfiggtulire 50tf00d1a0t13907,726. F 2, so Forty yiat with the janigc:. manse development in all adjuncts to civilisation during the past half-century,there has been a cor- responding increase of insanity, which, if raain- tained bids fair to overthrow the mental equili- brium of the nation at no very remote date. We have hopes,however, that the tide will turn in an other and more consolatory direction. Intemper- ance in anything is sufficient of itself to cause insanity, while added to this one source of the trouble is the tcint of heredity. The latter we can't hope to eliminate—it may be, and often is unsur pected—but surely with the ever growing intelleo' tuality of the people there will come a keeneF, appreciation of the ne'ed for moderation in ail things than exists at present. To-day there is little moderation in anything. Some persons work ire: moderately, others play immoderately, more es" innmoderately,while sadly too many drink immod' eratGly. It is to intemperance in drinking that the greatest proportion of insanity is due, and whett the drink bill of the country begins to decrease, then may we expect, if not a corresponding 0! least considerable decrease in the number 01 people condemned to incarceration in the mad" man's home. Moderation must characterize all we do, and especially must it apply t° what we drink, if our nation has to maintain its ' sanity and prestige.</p>
        <p>'4</p>
        <p>We do not profess to know exactly what the "laws of medicine" are, but from the particulars of a case just disclosed we should be inclined t° doubt their reliability. The North East London Coroner received information from a medical ins° cf the sudden death of a child in Bethnal Gress; and sent his officer to the home of the parents el the child. On arrivallthis functionary was veri much taken aback to discover that the child was far from being a fit subject for a coron9r's enquirft inasmuch as it was "alive and laughing." As soon as possible,therefore, the doctor was interro" gated, and in explanation of the extraordinary` state of affairs, said the child was alive when last he saw it, but" by all the laws of medicine it must die soon," and to save time he reported it dead, Alas for the doctor's diagnosis and the "laws medicine." The child could afford to laugh,thc. whether at him or not, we are uninforme6 There is good reason for Speculating here whether the doctor would, on the authority of the " la; of medicine," similarly decide that a person in S, critical state of illness was bound to recover, an° forthwith leave him to his fate.</p>
        <p>One of those brilliant geniuses whose talent nosc and again illuminates the dark places of the earth has come forward with a solution of the earif closing difficulty. It is simplicity itself. And We believe that one sex at least would hail its ado?' tion with perfect content. The real difficultY the way of early closing, as Mr. Stacey, of Or Early Closing Association, delicately hints, le woman. Woman has her own views about shot': ping. Sometimes a rampant fever for the posses, sion of cheap drapery will seize upon her shoo; closing time, and the anemic shop assistants silt in proportion. Then too, many women cannot n° brought to believe that girl shop assistant's especially if they are comely in appearance,desery any consideration whatever. The secretary of ' Early Closing Association—hopeful soul !--bjo , lieves that all this could be changed if husbann ' would exercise a little wholesome tyranny home, and compel their wives to shop at reascelt able hours; but probably he is a bachelor, and the, explains much. The heaven-sent genius to Wh?!.." casual reference has boon made knows a plan. It is that men should do women's shoi.„ and that women should shop for men. We n1r,,,. no comment on the vistas opened up this suggestion, for comment is superfluous 'v77- before the mind there rises up a vision of ti man whispering across the counter for nov. . ments, the mere existence of which masculine I. ture delicately refuses to contemplate.</p>
        <p>_ The annual revision of the voters' lists for °IA° Borough of Warrington has just been conducte i -A" It s interesting, if not amusing, to witn'; the two political agents splitting hairs about.trefs length of tenancies and raisino•little technicallti</p>
