        <p>gains t the qualifications of those who are desk- °us of obtaining a vote and thus becoming pos- eBBolsl of the power to express their opinion on 13iblic questions through the ballot box. The re- gistration laws of this country are of a oomph- e,ated description, and bristle with innumerable 'lle points which even a barrister-at-law:finds ,cli—cgrolt to interpret aright. The lodger,especially, 'area very badly under the present system, and 114Iess he can prove that he has two rooms for his ezclusive use his chances of getting a vote are very 'e indeed. We believe it was the late Mr. oies who once said that it ought to be as a man to get a vote as to be placed on the ir. Even in that case the lodger would anxious time of it, and the only, way out resent unsatisfactory state of affairs seems anhoed suffrage. This reform has been a the Liberal platform for some censuler- le, but at present it stands in the dim and future.</p>
