        <head>RIINOORN AND WioNEs.</head>
        <p>RIINOORN AND WioNEs.</p>
        <p>HARVEST HOMB SPIRVICES. MIMS: home services were held at S. Michael's Church and Rolv Trinity Charch.Runcorn, on Thursday evening, when the re3pective pulpits were occupied by the Rev. W. L. Cogs well, D D, (mall. of Wallasey),and the Rev J. W. Williams, (vicar of Far worth), Both places were attended by large congregations, and the services were of a hearty character.</p>
        <p>Sr: EDWARD'S CATHOLIC SUNDAY SCHOOL. On Wednesday the above Sotools ha I their annual trip and tea party, which event was participated in by about 300 seholais and congregation combined. The place visited was Frodstiam Hire, and a very, entoyable day was spent. The Rev. Father Gore accompanied the party, and WiS instramontal in providing for the com- fort and entertainment of all. DROWNING FATALITY AT GARSTON. - About nine o'clook on Tuesday night a sailor named Thomas PrOann was drowned in the river Mersey near the Old Dock, Gaston. He belonzed to the steamer Rosabella,lying in the Old Dock. He was returning to his vessel when he fell into the river from the dock wall. Search was made and the body was reeave:ed at live o'clock on Wednesday morning. Firm AT A. VVIDNES FA.RM.-1 DOMESTIC SERVANT ARRESTED.</p>
        <p>At Widnes en Thursday afternoon a young domestic named Florence Price; was alleged with setting fire to a storeroom at Yew Tree 'Farm, Halewood, occupied by Robert Owen Alawdsley. A fire broke out on &amp;inlay afternoon, and raged with violence, damage being done £2,000. The place was left in snaelavralge corps,and the fire broke out again cahmaortignet o amounting gthte y on Tuesday,just after prisoner had been seen leaving the barn, and an officer found matches there. Tne prisoner was remanded for a week. • the wife of P.C. Worsfold FLINEE4LL Or A WIDNES CONSTABLE'S WIFE. 's churohyard,Widnei. The tahfetesrtn.omana,ry wasO On nTt eh rur relic? la: funeral 10rb/0 incluled a company of twenty Con- stables, under the charge of Sergiant Bennett. The Rev. J. Adams read the burial service. A large number of the Wines church people were present at the last rites, to show their respect for deceased. She leaves a family of eight children, the youngest of whom is two years old.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>MILTON P. S. A.</head>
        <p>MILTON P. S. A.</p>
        <p>- The usual service in connection with the above took place on Sunday afternoon, in Milton Church, Widnes. whcn the hair was taken by Mr. T. Tr avers, one of the members. The singing was taken by members of the choir, Misses Hulme and Harrison giving a duet and Mr. W. J. Bagger a solo. Misses Thomas and Harrison and Messrs. Wilkinson and Biggeridge sang " Rock of Ara" as a quartette, the whole giving an excellent ren- dering of their respective nieces. The speaker,Mr. Salt- house. of 'Soutlopor, gave a moit instruc'ive address on " Silver." CHARGE AGAINST A WIDNES WOMAN. At Widws on Minday, a woman named Emma Platt was charged with receiving the following articles, well knowing them to have been stolen :—Two gold rings, four tablecloths, two sheets, one apron, and one pair so! boots.—Superinteident Reighley said the prisoner was she mother of a young woman convicted at the court the previous Thursday for having stolen the articles enumerated above.—Evidence having ben given the ptioner, who p`ca led goilty, was fined 405., including cot, or in default one month's imprisonment. HARVEST ROHE FESTIVAL AT WIDSIES PARISH CHIIRCH.</p>
        <p>_ The harvest thmkigiving service in connection with St. Mary's Widnes, commenced last Thur ;day, and were ertinued cn Sunday. Supplies of cora. thwers. and fruit had been received horn Mr. 3, S. GiniAtt. M.P. ; Mr. T. Sutton Timmis, !Alai Htrgreaves, (Clorwen), Mrs falcrOsa (Ditton), Mt. Gedde (Warrington), the M9M- bers of the coneregat,on and other Weals, The decor- ations had been most tastefully thought out and execu- ted, for which praise is due to the many helping hands. Apormoriee music was sung at the serviles. In the morning the preacher was the Rev. W. Carey Wood, M.A., of Australia. In the afternoon there was a chil- dren's service, when the Rev. J. Adams, M.A., delivereel an address. The evening preacher was the Rev. H. W. Campbelß Baugh, MA., vicar of Holy Trinity, Rich- mini Park. The servic ts were well attende I, anl the offertories re tlized a substantial Wm. RUNCORN PHILHARMONIC Swim. - On Wednesday next this society will resume rehearsals for th, sexiion,under the conductorship of Mr. Granville Bantoek, at the Bethesda schoolroom. The musical repertoire is a good one and includes Sir Arthur Sul- livetn's " On Shore and Sea," and the part songs ‘1 The Lovely Vale of Hresms," "The Miller's Wooing "and the " Solliera' chorus" from Faust. It is iatended that ! the annual concert should take niece on the 23rd November. The proj 4cted baz tar in ad of Abeffoeietyts Funds, which, thanks to the lady friends of the society is timely to prove successful. is to be to two days affair and will take place on the 9% and I.oth'Of November.' Further particulars will appear in due course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT] The finding of the boiy of An unknown chili in the Ship Canal at Old Quay Dock, by a couple of school boys, on Monday, threw the town into some excitement, as it was conjectured that the least possible conclusion to be arrived at was that it could be nothing more nor less than a barefaced case of infanticide. Happily. such a conclusion has now been dispelled ; but still there remains a serioas charge hanging °ye* tla3 heal of some one or other which is no less than that of birth conceal- ment. For the honour of the town and district, and in the came of its basted high morality, the clearing up of this mystery is anxiously looked for- ward to.</p>
        <p>Wednesday afternoon was c,lebratei by the Wes- leyan Cycle Club in a " blackberrvinz " picnic to Helsbv. About 12 of the members and their friends turned out, and pursued their self-imposed task so successfully that it was found exosdient by some of the members to:send home the lusciDll3 fruit by train.</p>
        <p>At the Rural District Council and Board of Guar- dians meetings on Tuesday, the eQtimates for the coming half year were submitted and passed. The Guardians eltimate a rate of 614 in the and the District Council are issuing a precept upon the overseers for 5i in the£ making a is. in the .£ altogether, which is exactly similiar to the call for the half year just closed. This is very satisfactory. as owing to several additional require- ' ments, in the way of water supplies, etc., it was generally believed that a greater demand would be made by the District Council.</p>
        <p>-- The deadlock betweem the Football Club and some of the old players still exists, and the Run corn team will take the field against Salford, to- morrow (Saturday), as they did at Wirran some- what the worse for " the rift in the lute." It is my belief, however, that Runcorn will suffer nothing, and personally I feel the result will be satisfactory to all concerned. f)r the team that takes the field does so with feelings of. unanimity, and can be de- pended upon to do their level best—which is a factor in the success of any football team.</p>
        <p>-- In the team selected by the committee, there figures "A. N. Other " a personage generally sup- posed to represent some great gun. This I can only suppose to be the "incog" for Faulkner. whom I am (unofficially of course) informed has decidod for the " green." If this is true,and to-morrow will show whether it is or not, the defeat of Runcorn by Salford or indeed any other team will be " A. N,. Other" stage removed.</p>
        <p>The Central Ward of Rencorn will, in a few days, or possibly a f-w hours,be c tiled upon to set about selecting a milli-late to represent their interests on the Board of Guardians, owing to the fact that Mr.SamuelYarwood is obliged to representtiewtown Ward. Already several candi lates are mentioned as likely to put un for the honour, amongst whom figure : the Rev. Canon Mnitland Wood,Rev. Tether Gore, and Mr. William Handley. What the private feelings of those gentlemen in the matter are, how- ever, has not vet been ascertained. All that can be said is that any one of these would rnsk a capital Guardian, as they are thoroughly enversant with the requirements of the Ward.</p>
        <p>Si. JAMES'. LATCHFORD. The treasnrer of the Schools has rec9ived a cheque from Sir Gilbert Greenrill, to defray thn expense laying out and enclosing with a. wall a large addi- tion to the pfitvgrolind in connectim with the S. .ss.mos' Scho-,14. 09 land haring hem given by Mr. Thomas H. Lyon.</p>
        <p>CYCIASTS USE PILLAR BRAND. labriaating and Burning 011 e and grela Pummel, MlZlVellette,and Brunswier Black. From all Dealers. AGRNTS : AIchEAN AND CO., 13! SBEL-5111.131iT, LIVBRFODL.</p>
      </div>
