        <p>'PAUPER LIINAWICS.</p>
        <p>The list of the lunatics in the County Asylum, Upton, chsrvable to the Union, was sub rated, showing there were 63 at present in the asylunt,seven of whom hal re- cently been traasf iind from Warrington. .laT INCORRIGIBLE GIRL. The Mastery eported that toe girl Ei:z Antrobus, who had been prasecoted on several occasions for re- fractory conduct, ha i br Aten 17 windows, and the Com- mittee recorara-ind id that she shoyld he prosecuted. A girl named Godley, who had appafently been influenced by Aatrabus had also brek sn one pace of glass. The Committee recommended that she be sent to an indus- trial school. It was stated that Antrobus seemed to like to go to goal bemuse she hid a ride to Kautsf ird. (Laughter.) The Master said sh 3 was as refractory as, ever that. morn Mg. The Committee's reclaim miations were adopted. VAAMSTEELD V. FISHZRY. Wien considering tie rein nendatians of the Vislt- lag Committee, some discussion to place sic to tie ad oisabilitv of sending a couple of • the boys t the fisheries at Grimahy.—Mr.flaxter thought that the bops ought rather io he encouraged to go as farm lab ourers.as at present there was a d mrth of labourers.—Several of the other guardians cone irred.—Mr. Norman said that there was no coercion at all in the matter, as the b sys expressed a deeir it° go there; The guardians siinpiy pa d a sum of £5 for outfit that was all. Ile was also pleased to testify that BONI, of the boys who had been commenced in chat way were doing well.—The man expressed the hope that the Press would take notice of the fact that the: Guardians were always glad to consider applications for boys, and especially from f armero.—The matter drop pe I. —Mr. Mead 3wero f t dre attention to the fact that tnere were at preseit only 4 girls under the Industrial trainer ; the Master said it was so.— l'hfarecorrimendations of the Visiting Commit- tee were then confirmed on the motion of Mr. W. Norman.</p>
        <p>INSPECTION COIimITTEE. Mr.' WOO I Era! Wad that tho following g"lf!narla blappointpd as to o)mo:tit:ea to visit the chi]. irsn at St. Clare's Orphanage. Pantasaph, and the Ow:Wei' Hill Orphinage, B ichiale,who are chargeable to this Union: —AL sere. Wright, Darlingtoo, and Lewis.—The motion was unaohnonsly agreed to. SATUFAcTORY STATE OF AFFAIRS. I Mr. Normen r marted that the Cammittee appointed to visit the Upton Asvium. Chester, were convinced that ' everything that could b 3 done was done for the inmates lof the place. An unfortunate feature was that lunacy was on thz increase, and as a result large exteesions were taking place at the asylum. After what they saw being dose they wara ROC surprised at the ad cane ein I the Con ity Rite.</p>
