      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>F RODS HAIL</head>
        <p>F RODS HAIL</p>
        <p>- - SALE OF BOOBS AT FRODSHAM.-011 ThUMW' and Friday M sere. Glutton, Elphick, and Co., disposed by public auction of tee library of Mr. John Simpson, at Donsiale, Frodsham. The library consisted of some 8,000 volumes, and comprised many valuable historical, biograohical, illustrated, scientific, poetical, aed ms • cellanems works. Good prices were realised. RATEPAYERS' MEETING.—At a meeting of the rate- payers within the district of the Fro ishatn and Helsby Drainage Board, held in the Town Hall, Frodsham, on Thursday evening, Mr. Ralph Bate preslding, the follow- ing gontls men were elected as members of the Drainage Board for the coming session :—Owners—Captsin W FligErn, 0 akmere Hall; the Rev. G. D. Wray, Halton ; Messrs. A. H. Talbot, J P. Aston Hall ; St. John Charl- ton, Cholmondeley : and lealph Bate, Netherton. Occu- piers—Messrs. J. William Andrews. James Andrews, Thomas Andrews, William Booth, Frodsham ; Thomas Elam% Netherton ; Thomas Littler, Wood houses; James Percival, Godserdt; John Priestner, Frodsham ; William Oakes, Netherton ; and Stanley White, Flelsby. Panocaue COMMITTEE.—At the meeting of the above committee hell in the Courtroom, Froleham, oi Friday evening, Mr. Charles E. Linaker presiding, a letter was read from Dr. Burton with reference to the manhole opposite to his residence, inqairing whether the com- mittee intended to ignore his complaint altogether. If such was the case, he w add have no alternative but to call the attention of the Local Government Board to the matter. It was resolved that the Clerk acknow- ledge receipt of such letter, and state that the complaint had repeatedly been under discussion and inspections made, but without any result. The clerk produced a number of fieures which he had worked our, fi era which it appeared that a fair all-round profit could he made, providing Frodsham Lordship was snpplis d with water on an assessment being made on all properties of 10 per cent. on rrsidences and 3d. per we k for cottage property. The Chairman did not think it was a wise thing to seriously consider the proposal arisiag as it did nit of the application of one gentleman who had purchased a house and that of another who pro- posed to built houses in the Lordship. Re should have thought if a general desire to have water in the Lordship existed that a further approach %you'd have been made on their behalf He was as friendly disposed to the Lordship as any mein her of the committee, hut thought that th• proper course would be for the Lordship to purchase the water at a reasonable price per thowand gallons, pump it and dis- tribute it themselves, instead of suggesting that we should increase our public debt to the extent of £3,000 Mr. Kydd was of an opinion quite adverse to that of the Cisairmal, and he proposed that the Frodsham Lordship Committoe be asked to take the water on a 10 per cent. bssis with cottages a, 31. per week,and if there was ally' deficiency in the working that the Lordship Commit-el should bear a proportionate part thereof, the township carrying nut the whole of the work and being responsible for the repayment of the principal and interest. This was seconded hr Mr. Hulse, and on its being put to the meeting the following voted in favour : —Messrs. Kyddi Badger, Illilee, Booth, Hulse. Wilkinson, Spencer, and Barrow, and Messrs. Esrlom,ltothwell, Shors,and Gleave against, whereupon the same was declared carried. The Parish Clerk was instructed to call an early meeting for the consideration of the following matters --The reduction in railway fares,a new Post Office,and electric OPENINO OF A NEW FOOTBALL GROUND.—On Saturday last the Frodsham club opened their new ground with a match in the Chester District Junior League, ani on wlich occasion they had Rossett (who are the holders of the Cheeer Charity Cup and the runners up of the Chester Junior Shield Competition) as opponents. 9t the outset the hems &amp;even played in a brilliantly dash- ing manner and scored three goals in the first half against their opponents' nil. After the interval the boot was on the other ter; to use a familiar phrase, and Ros- in-et put on four goals to Frodsharn's additional one goal. the final being a draw of four goals each. The fini,h was very exciting as Frodsham had a lead of four to two goals five minutes from time. The following com- posed the home team : (loal, JPacock ; backs,?. Horrors end F. Evans ; halvi9, F. .Aahton, F. Rogers aeff P. Andrew's ; for war 's. Thoraley an 1 Timperher (right wine). R. Selby (centre), a. Elillidge and C. Burrows (left wing).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>itIJNOOR.N FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
