      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ANOTHER C VIAL MYSTERY AT BUNCO RN.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER C VIAL MYSTERY AT BUNCO RN.</p>
        <p>About two o'c7ock,on Mondar afteraom,as some boys were playing near to the old Quay D .ck, now part of the Mancheater Ship Goa', they discovered the body of a fully thaellpod male child, in the water. The police were at once communicated with and inquiries insti- tuted. From th 3 appearanco of t'vi holy, it is qvidmt that it has not been long in the wat ;it is that of a now-bra cliilri,but has no clothing or anything at;ached tO it by which it can hA iciantifiod. CEIARGE OF CONOEALNIENr OF BIRTH.</p>
        <p>At J. i. wste npoliocrecs7-,, Barr:ern o mr. n WAvin-escia-Y—M—or. ulna. coromr, he d at invest on the body of an unknown male child, fowi.l as stated above on Monday last, at the old Q la? D )ck, when ths following evitence waa adduced : Dr. Ormsby said he had made a post-mortem examina- tion of the body, which wis that of a six monti's chill, and had probably lived only a coopla of hours. Death was due,in his opinion. t ) ov,alrn?is e )nacqueut up„ premattire birth, and not to drowning By a juror : Did you ever know of a six months' child having a sonar ite existence after having been born? .....sach cases are rare; and ia m 7 experience I have never coins act. us °ie. The Coroner: It is clear that the child did not live. Inspector Hicks, who watched the proceedings on 103- y oto school, with half of the police, expressed himself satisfied with the ojna doctor'sdn,asyni a evidence. lrnßutter,i 3, 74svrtlansinlv,:st;irseewta. stated that n the li another boy, when he notio A something in the water, by Old Qaay Dock, that looked !ik) a rnon,To7. on going closer to it however, he disaoverd it was the body of a child. He than went and gave information to the police, P.C. George Carrington testifie I that the last witness gave him information about the child's b7de, which he went and got out of the water. Sunseqa ‘ml," he handed it over to the doctor for the purp)se of a post-mortesn examination. , The Coroner, in reviewing the °victories, said, on first finding the body the case had the appearance of that fin,anticide, Mr he was glad that there was no longer any anxiety onthat point, which had been removed by the doctor's evidence, that the child had diet owing to premature birrh. He would alvce teem to bring in a verdict accordingly. Of course there would be a question of the concealment of birth, hut of that they natvd take no notice as it was a matter for the p dice, whom ne hoped would be able to trace to its proper quer er. The penalty for the concealment of birth was a vory seven one, and he hmerlrhat the police would los su icesstu' so as to show that people coul I not do es they had lore in thid case ; when it was evident that the chill had been premituraly trrn and cola :Ai in the Qa Cal by its mother. l'he Coroner further commentel upon the frse- I dom oft le county f )r......_........._0r in , 700 in. A BOGUS ALARM OF FIRE.</p>
        <p>On Monday considerable excitemsw was caused in the town by the soun lin r of t:e fire "buzzer " at Cockhedge Cettnn Factory. at ten minutes to twelve (noon). A rumour quickly spread tiat afire had broken out there, and many plople flocked to the place. There was no sign ef fire, however, anti it was ascertained that the Wire connectinz the "buzzer" had broken, whin c tused the steam to escape, and Rave the nee Iless alarm. It was some time betore the crowd could bf assured that no fire had broken out. Firemen, on hearing the alarm, I presented themselves at the Heath-side Stntion, but as no information of a fire had reached them they did not proceed to the Works. ENGINEERS' PICNIC.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the employee in the engineering de- partment of Messrs. Rylands Bros held their annual picnic. Chester w%s the place slectel, alid the party, which numbered 2,0, arive.d at the ancient e city soon et-r noon. Many of the places of interest ansteamersertehres plaertyEejeoluetn rsuen Ferry and d hy on of Eaten Hall. Returning to Chester a knife and fork tea was partaken of at the Bear and Billet,the cater- ing of Mrs. Hewitt giving ev:lry satieaction. A social evening was afterwards held, under the pre• sidency of Mr. J.Berry. Oppattunity was taken to celebrate the coming of age of Mr. J. ailafon, and suitable references wore made by the Chairman, Mr.</p>
        <p>Savory, and others. The return journey was made a 9.30, and the outing was one of the mast, sor.:- ensful euer held. _ .</p>
