        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was hell in the Board Room at the Workbouse on Wednesday morning. Mr. G. pin ling presided, and there were also preens —Mrs. Clay, Revs. W. H. Drummond, J. H. Rid tette, Dr. Patterson,' Messrs. A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley, %VlDiane Hickman, B. Davies, T. B. 5d Okla, T.H.Ash:roft, William Houghton, T. Hatton, A. Brookes, J. Clarke, J. W. Knowles, J. Johnson, with Mr. J. C. Sutton (clerk), Mr. S. Sutton (deputy-clerk), Mr. Ward ( workhouse marker), and Mr. Wright (superintendent Padgale Scheele). THE NEW CLASSIFICATION SCHEMB. The House Committee recommended that under the general order of March 8, 1894, and the special order of August 25, 1898, the c nnrnittee be empowered to grant iametes as provided by the new scheme, two ounces of dry tea, and eight ounces of sugar with mirk weekly, and facilita s for the use of the same be provided. The tender of Mr. Davies ler tin ware was accepted. The classification sub-comnaittee's minutes were also read. ABSENCE OF THE MASTER AND MATRON. The House Committee reco nmended that during the temp nary absence for four days of the Master and Marcia, Miss Ward be empowered to act as chief officer of the Workhouse. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY in moving the adoption of the minutes of the H else Co nmitcee said the recommendations in conneatioa with the clasifieat'on scheme gave practi- ce! exuression to the resolution a ereed to by the Beard nearly two months ago. He would f ormally move that they be acc mted, and that the minutes ineluding those of tee se-committee be agreed to, and that the m tit.r be instructed to get the necessary priating of instructions as agreed to. Mr. A. JOLLEY seconded. Mr fircemere: Will the printing be carried out under the coo rain. Mr. A. J. Jot LEY : It is only a small matter ; the printing of a few circulars. The minutes were then passed. TRE IT FOR THE CHILDREN. The minutes of the &amp;nieces Co'nfltlLGOEl stated that Mr. Ohm,' had invited the childrei to witness tee perform- area et his Circus om Saturday afternoon neat, the invi- tetem being accepe 1 with 'hanky. Toe rniaut se were c weed, on the motion of Mr. A. Jolley, se:on led by Mr. Davies. THE RELIEF FROM FRIENDLY SOCIETIES. To, CLERK read a lettea he heel received from the Welv:reamptem B earl of Guardians to the effect that all recipieens of out-relief elonging to frienily societies shoal I riot be diefrenceired, an calling lapel all other Board of Guerdians to pass a similar resolution, With a view to petitioning Pare tment to so alter the law that the above might be carrel out. The Cresaannes : What will you do with it P Mr. ['ICEMAN : I propose teat dee letter lie on the Mr. DAVIES seconded,end the motion wee carried. ' raz ESTIMATES—STATEMENT BY THE CLERK. TilE CRI [lcisu OF THE ASSESSMENT COMMIT TEE. The CLERK made a statement with regard to the climates for the ensuing year. He said the qainquennial revision by tit: County and County Rating Committee of ths Aesessmeet of properties sutjct to C runty Rates reaultei as (renal in large additions to rateatee value all over the county. The effect upoe this Union, judging from the roam at to tee county eou make to-day, is apparently unimportant if not slightly favourable. The estimated outlay is £8,246 and is charged upon the tansies in proportou t rateable value. The total of £3,991 is charged upon parishes as fixed in the County Raze wareant. Nenierate additions to the sums name] veal fad upon ths raral parishes within the Fs riot or the School at endance Committee, such additions being lecluied in a beprate estimate to be ap- proved by your Board. The urban to xn of Warrington (whica new includes Letchbrd) and ant of Newton will be charged with the r svctive slater es paid to tee Assistant Overseers as collectors of poor rates. An equal Call of sel. in the £ would ordiearily suffice to meet your Bo ad's estinaat ed out'ae of £8,246. The continuous growth of expense uude: several important heads,and of new charges upon your Loan and Interest Account, will prevent any large balances Aanding in favour of parishes at the close of the present half year. It may, hewever, reasonably be calculated that balances of the kind at the close of the half year for which provision is being made will result from a present equal call of 61. in the £ and that rate is advised. Tioirty years ago three times as much peer rate wee raim I for the relief of the poor as for every other 'Jur. pose combined. As teceatly as ten years ago rn ore than half the poor rate went to the actual relief ofthe poor. The expenditure of your Board out of monies received from Poor Rats, and from all o her sources, tor all the puro see include] in the p trtieulars of common charges was £18,051. The call for cam non charges let the new , expiring half year was gi in the £, making with the proposed call of 61 a total or 111 i for the year 1893-9 anl with prospeet of balances at t-idy Day next sufficient to as the rate for the first halt of yours mond year ia office. Cre lit must also be taken for theadvantagesgeined by an effec- five revision of Assessments of rateable property under the direction of the appointed C mmaittee. An increase of £4,500 rateable value easing union and local burdens by several hundred pounds yesrlyen respect of one class of property periodically re-valued by the Clerk,wileirres- pective of additions derived from new properties be broueht into force prior to issue of your Board's precepts at your next Interim Board meetine, It may be remarked in connection with the subject of Assessment that Kane local dis- satisfaction has been expressed with the eating of licensed hooves. The criticism had it; origin with a dialogue published in a local newspaper, follo xed by elionial comment thereon, the matter thereof being ad- mittedly fairly one for public comment. Nothine, how- ever could be clearer to any readers of the crtieple, famdiar with the law of rating or of assesimenewhether ef licensed houses, or of other classes of eroperty, tha that the two perties to the discourse had entered upon a way they knew not, and -hence were alike at sea upon law and fact. Whatever the degree of excuse due to the two, neither of whom probably had personal experience of a 'ministration be local government, and both et whom were at least oblivious of practice. it iv diffieult to excuse members of an elected co:perate body for their expre"sion of erroneous views upon a subject it was their plain duty to have better understood. Yet these same pO-sons are ap- parently aegrieved that be the wis lom of the leeistature the very important work of fixing and raeula'ine aesessnente 01 premerty for rating purposes is entrusted to Boercla of Guardians and not to Bereueh Ceuncile. Tae criticism', anonymous and official, did not at the time seem to merit further atten- ti en than it r 'calved in the form of a brief explanation thereon to your Board. It will he satisfactory to the Guardians to learn that the principle of assessment of licensed houses obtaining in this Union received the approval of the Lancashire listing Committee, enforce a maximum basis of assessments all over the county ; &amp;Id who revised, for County Rating purpeaea, tee assessments in force for local rating within this Union, at nearly the close of a revision of such atS2SS• ments that had extendel all over the county. Since thee the head of the r4tes Department of a erat rail way Company, who are by far the heaviest centribu- tors to the r tt,s of this Union, and upon whose obj?ction on grounds of irregularity of assessment, the rates of the tevanahine of a large Cheshire Union were not long aeo held bad and quashed. and the aesesaments of whole rateable property in this Union are p,riodically. sloe tan. tially raised, visited the Union without communication with the Committee or their Clerk, upon receiving netice of an iuteedee further revision of the railway A55.,451 Men% to investigate the gravels upon which objection had in the manner above explained been made to the aseesameet of licenaed houses with the result that he exprearied the setisfantion of directors a ith tee precis- dere of the ASsesament Committee. Mr. Willie n BOUGHTON moved the adoption of the report and also that it he printed anl a copy circulated amongst the members. Mr. THORNTON SsnOn led • The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said there were certain exereeiens of opinion and c srtaio things which had been said in peblic i in the newsoepers which were impel-- telt. ffs thlught it unfortun ste that the Board eheull be asked to give it sanction to the individual eepree- sines of opinion of the Clerk. They might or might not agree with the Clerk and he did not think it was desr sole that the Bard sheuld be asked to adopt the report .1 it steed and allow it to go forth to ,the public that they as a Beard had adopted the Clerk's individual ex- • nreaalons of opinion. Personally he did not wish to criticism or say whether he agreed or disagreed with the expreesiens which ha I been made,but he thought it was not desirable to castigate people who took part its public discussion. Mr. KNOWLES •. Did the person who criticised put his na t ) ? Mr. A. Jourr : No. Mr. KNOWLES I ShOlfd like to kuow that. I think these anonymous writers are ths worst kind of back- biters, (Hear, hear.) lam not a member of the Assess- ment Committee but would Llee to ask that question. Tee CHAIRMAN : The statement just read is not the statement of the Guardians but the Clerk's statement. The reneutioa beore the meeting is that the Clerk's statement be adopted. The CLERK: The resolution should be that the Clerk's Statement he printel and circulated and that the esti- mates be approvel and ed opted. He albeit venture to sae that he certainly did not intend his statement to be a cestleation ef any person as had been ;suggested. Pen- plc outsi le had a pea-6; right to criticise the proceedings of the Assessment Committee but he (the Clerk) had en- delyoured to poitit out