      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>the matter, and he did not know whether the Clerk was referring to him or to somebody else. But from the Olerk's romarks he now gathered that he was not allud- ing to h;na but to seineone outside the Beard. On the general question of public house assessments he (Mr. Jolley) did not know whether the Clerk bad seen the reports of a case recently decided which bore on the question Although when the matter was brought before tee public he (Mr. Jolley) took a decided stand on the lines just laid down by the Clerk, still the question was not absolutely and finally disposed of for he found in the case be bad alluded to that two judges of the Queen's Bench gave a decision which undoubtedly mods- fied the previous attitude adopted in the assessment of tied houses. These juoces tad laid it down pretty clearly that the competition which exists between tied houses was an element to be taken into consideration when assessing tied houses. He (Mr. Jolley) was sure prised when he saw it bocause it went against the deal- sines in the Stocktcnand South Shields cases. One ot e judges in the recent C 893 was Justice Ridley, and he could not say for certain who the other judge was, but , their decision rather modified the previous views taken by judges on the matter. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE did not und.retand that they I were asked to endorse the statements of the Clerk. They wet e simply asked to confirm the fiaancial part of the statement. The analysis of the aecounts of the Union was extremely valuable and he wished that it could be printed and circulated throughout the constituency. There were thirty unions in the County of Laneaster,and ,he found that tea of them showed a mach larger p o- peition of paupeis to population than Warrington. N 3 fewer than eight of the (Mons showed a rising average and he thought if these facts were made known it would do something to allay the alarm which the unwarraet- able criticisms on that Beard caused a few moelitis ago The resolution as amended was then a treed to. The CLERK said as the Board had disposed of the question he sought perhaps be allowed to make a remark ith reference to the assessment of public-houses which was a very interesting acid important subject and also refer to the remarks wh ch bed fallen from the lips of Mr. A.J Jolley. It was a very difficut thing to follow the courts of law in their decisions. One court settled it in one way and another in an entirely different way, whilst in tee Court of Appeal tooth decisione were undone. It was not an easy motto: to interpret and put into practice the apparent deciaione of superior courts. He (the Clerk) beppened to be familiar wi:h the case Mr. Jolley refer- red co, and La I drawn other conclusions to those which he had arrived at in that case. It meant Vele : that, the goodwill of a licoused house or the great sum a brewer woull pay in order to have the molopely of the beer trade was not the sum ii respect of which the tenant, who was sitnp'y the occupier of the house and liable to pay rates. 47f s liable for assessment on. He was liable for the ass-set/lent or the house j ist as a p Tson would be liable fir any other kind of a house,except that it was fir the Assessment Committee to take into considera- tion it being a licemsel house, an I being so, they were privileged te give it an enhenced value. That was just what the Assessment Committee at Warringten had done. As a place grew in value the commitreo r 'cog- nised the change and the benefits to be derived from it. THE DELEHITES' TO THE POOR L CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK allude ito the forheorning 24th annual Poor Law Calferanoe, t bo held at Live:pool (St. George's 114), aud mentioned be subjl.:ts to ba dis- co It was deeidnd, afer s )1133 discuasion, t at the fol;ow• m,inbers of tha B trd b pp Anted a deou-at.on to attln I tie C mferenee on the 30th :.-eir.-al'ae;• and 0 ..;tober let, viz., the o.ta,rman of the Board,Dr. Par- and Mr. A. J. Jolley. THE VICCINATION QUESTION. THE NEW MIRRIAGE LAW.</p>
        <p>M. CLARKE asked if the Ctork hid received the cir. cular retitive to the new VaC3initi3ti Tha Re?. J. Ef. RIDDETTE: Hive you had a letter nlatiy? to the new mirriale law ? Wo Noncortiorts hive been ponhillocl for oir Nmconformity by h tying ihs notices of our marriages suipmd-id within tha pre- cin'ts of the Workhouse, kLaughter.)</p>
        <p>TIP CLERK: NlAtie WurkillUSe. 1113 R 3. J. H. RIDDETTE : It raw not !sive bela d 1) here, bqt the Workho lie pretnisia- The CLERK : At 1113 Clerk's °Mee. _ _ .</p>
        <p>, The R v. J. a. RIDDErT : That is part of it. (Laugh- ter.) The CLERK : is a li:`.le diiocien. There is no doubt that a strong feeling exists that the registrition office—Uh. qup,rintend r gistrar's office—iho ild for n U) part of tha union ofil les. In many Unions he S3p?r- intendlet re ir's nffi !es are perfeclv distinct from the w rk bui!dings. I have r-c-nved a long circular on the Vaccination Act, 1898, but Or.. ch logs only comas into operation on the Ist January. It would, ! ti-e ref ue, b h Ater to defer y3ur consid 'ration of tha matter till nearer the day wnea the At cones into op^ r an. Mr. Cmaturg : What concerns us carol into ,m on th 12th August. I more that the circalar be printed and ojr tul,ted amongst the Gaardians. Tee CILVIRMAV The copy ol Ih3l3Jarl's ()riot Mr. CLI.EKK Yes. Mr. ASBCROFTSCSadCd, and the motion was carried. The CLERK observed that it was for those parents who did not bel'eve in vaccination to go before the magis- trates. With regard to tse whole Act, it would be material that the Board ssiould come to some conclusions upon it as from the let January next. CONTRACTS. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The f Mowing contracts wore accepted : Coal, Messrs, Richard Evans and Company, Limited ; drapery re- ferred to the House Committee ; butcher's meat, Messrs. Singleton and Stephenson ; bread for schools, Messrs. James Strange and S /[l3 ; Penm teumawr stone, the Warrington Slate Company, Limited ; cord, twill and canvas, Mr. Lee, Bridge-street ; potatoes for the Work- house, executors of M. James Warburton, Hallam ; cof fins. Mr. linsworth ; milk for the schools,Mr. Houghton, Woolston ; milk for the Workhouse, the Farmers' Stores, Bewaev-street ; brushes, Mr. Radcliff., PrieXe7-street ; flour, Mr. Carter ; leather, Mr. Pickavarre ; straw, executors of Mr. Warburton, Hallam ; clogs, Mr. Peter Sudl ; snoss, Messrs, R. Roberts and Son ;gr ocerirs, referred to the House Committ'e ; Printing and Itatilnery, Mr. Jtarps Wood; druzs, Mr. Whitby, Bridge street; wines and spirits, refeir ad to the House Coin- mittee. rho Board then rcre.</p>
