      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. WALLACE, OF WILDERSPnOL.-01.1F readers will hear with regret of the death of Mr. George John Wallace, of Willerspool, Warrington, which oc- curred at his residencr, Fern jore, Wdelorspool, last Friday evening. Toe decoased gentleman hell a res. ponsiole position w:th M Fors. Greenall, Whitley and pr sdeeessors for up A ards Company, Limited, and their of a guar er of a century. Do to Tuesday last he was able to perform his duties at th 3 brewery, when, not- withstanding his brave attempts to still carry on his work, he was reluctantly compiled to relinquish the same. For somo months his health had boen somewhat precarious. and in spite of the most skilful medical aid I his death took place on Friday night. An affection of 1 the heart;was succee led by an acute attn.' of Bright's disease, which proved fatal. The deceased, who was ' 48 Years of aae, avos a Freemason, arid a msmber of the Lodge of Lights, No. 148, Warrington. He leaves behind a wife and seven children, to whom many expressions of sympathy have been sent, including one from the men employed ha his department at Willerspool. The funeral took place on Monday at St. Thomas' Church, &amp;nekton Heath The popularity of Mr. Wallace was demonstrated in a remarkable manner. Along the tine of route from the house to the church every hlin I was dr wn, and marks of regard were observable on every hand. The cortege left Fern Cottage shortly before three, the hearse being preceded by the emplo7e from Messrs. Greenall, Whit- ley and Co. On arriving at the church gate the members of the deceased's lodge assembled, at d hey, with the Rev. S. P. Gray sod the choir of the church, joined the Procession. The service was impressively rendered by Mr. Gray, as also the musical portion by the choir. The Dead March in " Saul " was played by Mr. F. Rose, organist and choirmaster. Tho chief mourners were : Master Percy Wallace, Master Bruce Wallace (eons) Mr Joseph Wallace (broth, r), Ed war 1, Mr Herbert: Mr • T R Westbrook, Mr A J Page, Mr W Hadfield (brothers- irriaw), Mr John Payne (cousin). Master John Harris (nephew). Mr Winiarn 8 Robsrts (the deceased's col- league). Amongst the numerous wreaths and other floral tributes of Iwe aed esteem, in addi, ion to those from the family and r datives, were those from the officials rid staff and from the workmen at WildPr!potal Brewery, and from Mrs Wakefield, Mr and Mrs C Higginboitom. Mr John Preston. Mr A J Harvey (Worksop), Mr E R Parry (Liverpool), Mr and Mrs Bolton, Mr and Mrs Girlies, Mr and Mrs F Howard, Mr and Mrs J Henry, Mr W B Roberts, Mrs W H Young, and Mr and Mrs C W Haveneoe. The funeral arrangements were in the hands of Mr. C. W. Daveneort, of Steckten Heath. All the carriages were supplied by Mr H F Gresmoug,h, of War- rington.</p>
