      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>LATEST NEWS. TERRIBLE CYCLONE IN THE WEST</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS. TERRIBLE CYCLONE IN THE WEST</p>
        <p>INDIES. KINGSTOWN DESTROYED. Sr. THOMAS, 'THURSDAY. A. fearful hurricane has swept over the West Indiee, 13:inging death and ruin. All the reports agree that it is one of the most des-ructive storms in the history of the islands. No place has escaped. The greatest lois of LIT: has occurred at Kingstovrn,St. Vincent, where it is rep nted that three hundred per- sons have [saris ed.Tne town has ben totally destr3yed. Twenty thousand inhabitants are homeless. Thousands of people are belt g f d at the public expense, but many are starving. The dead are being buried in trenches. , The first news received from St. Lucia describes the storm as having been of almost unprecedented violence. , It was accompanied by a tidal wave and a tremendous rainfall. Thera were several lan 'slips Many houses and bridges were destroyed, ani the cocoa estates were devastated. Guadaloupe suffeced equally. Nameraus destructive .landElldes occurred, and nine.-xen persons were killed. BARBADOES, THURSDAY. A hurricane has pas ird over here ding considerable damage to property and lives. British man-cif-war Alert steamed oat, Loenda, Lapland, " E," Grans Lynwood,. Lovdahl blown am ay. For aser losses are repo:ted, principally of ,coasting craft. PORT OF SPAIN, WEDNESDAY. Ths stoamPr Yucatan reports that immense destruc- tion ha 3 b.en c arsed at Bar 'lades b 7 a hurricane, f let'? th Ind people being homeless and tam hundred killed. The damage is estimated at a quarter of a million. THE ATTEMPTED TRAIN WRECKING</p>
        <p>IN THE MIDLA.NDS. DEPECTIVES SEIIVAING FOR AN ESCAPED LUNATIC. .</p>
        <p>A seartling turn has been ':-We-n- to the train wrecking mystery in the nA zhbourhood of Northampton (says the tt Daily mail "). A day or two before the first of the four &amp;Tecate outrages was committed a male lunatic esc aped from Huntingdon Asylum, and he has not yet been cauF.tht. The rai'way and police detectives, assisted by an officer specially sent from the asylum, are making every effort to effect his c tpture, as it is quite possible that he is the author of the crime. For some reason best known to themselves the detectives have kept this im- portant fact a profound secret. Why, it is cl.fficult to , conceive, because if an accur ate dcscr:otion of tie mis- sing ma dman had been publish° 1 without delay it is , just p issible he would not now be at large. The public, as well as the rail way officials, would leave been placed in p ssession of valuable infoemation, and thereby , enabled to give material as ,ist ince to the detectives ingaged in solving the present mystery. ANOTHER RAILWA.Y MUSH. I NARROW ESCAPE OF THE PRINCE OF WALES. A WIDNES TRAIN WRECKED. .</p>
        <p>A few minutes after the Royal train conveying the Prince of Wales had passed through Crewe station, en route to Scotland yesterday miming, anr.ther disastrous erasion occurred near the North Jooction. A mixed gods train from Widnes came off the North inc. and as it neared th 3 junction a ligut engiae, which was going to take the Bangor and liolyhead express, ran into the side of it obliquely, cutting it in two. Six tracks and tire engine were derailed, three being thrown over on their side, the goods being scattered up and down the line. The rolling stock and the to'r nanent way were muc'd damaged, and the lines blooked. The Prince of Wal9s had passed through only ten minutes before the smash.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>POISONING OF THE EMPEROR OF COREA.</head>
        <p>THE POISONING OF THE EMPEROR OF COREA.</p>
        <p>YortAllAMk, THIIRSDLY.</p>
        <p>The E nperor of C wea h recove:inz, but th Crown Prince is still ill. One of the la lies of the h eusehold is also sick. It is believed the poison was given by another lady. but there are doubts whether the attempted crime was due to jealousy or to political motives. FATAL COLLIERY ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>----- A rorthcawl correspondent t-legraphs that a shocking accident has occurred at Park Slip, one ot the pits of the Nuth Navigation Company. One man named Rice Morgan has been killed and nine others are injured. It is sttted that the injured have been removel to their homes. The nature of the accident has not yet trans- pired. A later message states that the accident was caused by some trucks running away. The colliery is one at which upwards of one hunei lives were lost six years ago by an explosion. TIIE AXE RICA.N AILLBA.SSA.DOR- f SHIP.</p>
        <p>WASHINGTON, THIJItSDLY.</p>
