      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SYMBOL OF STRENGTH.</head>
        <p>THE SYMBOL OF STRENGTH.</p>
        <p>Britannia is the symbol of strength at home and abroad. and whether up Jn the ocean wave or ia the serried ranks of her brave song in Runt. India,or China the Came of our country is always hientined with power of Will and strength of nerve—the highest state of cour- age that makca heroes. Science is the handmaid that helps ti maintain this standard of supremacy, and the great gifts of nature are concentrated for the use of men In Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, a tonic to keep every faculty at its brightest and its bet. Mighty is the power of the spade and those who wield it are the pioneer 4 of our greatness. The fearless miner burrowing in the earth and the strong navvy removing mountains are the very sinews of the nation. These men must always keep their strength at full stretch. Lib )ur of the severest is their lot and full health is necessary in accomplishing their daily tasks, and if they are attacked with Indiges- tion, Liver Disorders, or Pains in the Back, they know they can find relief by taking Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. The hard working toilers, generally, of the United Kingdom, should remember the old saying, ',Prevention is better than cure," and that just a 5. it is nece,sary to call in a medical man, when brain and body are overtaxed, so S it desirable to do all that is possible to keep the system thoroughly up to the matk, for every rush of aompetition and extra labour. You have a regular doctor, have you a regular preservative of health to save you from the doctor and the doctor's bills? If not, try Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Great Tonic Pre- ventative against Serious Illness. The distressing worries of a family household lead to the greatest suffer- , jog—the nervous system being specially strained in the ' turmoil, and Neuralgia, Nervousness, and Debility, with their attendant miseries are the most frequent torments that women in particular have to endure. Low spirits and Depression often render housewives quite unfit for their onerous duties and to prevent this condition Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is strongly recom- mended. Don't delay if you feel out of sorts, but get Gwilym Evans' Quinine liitters,a purely vegetable tonic, which contains the strengthening qualities of Sarsa- parilla, Saffron, Burdock, Gentian, Lavender. and Dandelion, as well as Quinine. The exact remedy you want for Palpitation, Sleeplessness, Biliousness, or Rheu- matism. It not only gives you freedom from pain but strength, and zest for your work, that makes it a pleasure. This perfection of medicinal preparations is advocated as a first aid towards quickening commercial instincts for the counting-house or the market-place, it is a Tonic that is sure to be wanted. This world- renowned remedy is sold in Bottles at 2s. 9d. and 4s. 61. each. Beware ef Imitations. See that you get "Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters " with the name " Gwilym Evans' on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. This is important. Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Manufactriring Com- pany, Limited, Llanplly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SINGULAR CONDUCT.-BREAKING WINDOWS OUT OF SPITZ.</p>
        <p>At the Borouigh Police Court on Tuesday morning, be- fore Mr. Garnett (in the chair), and Mr. Birchall, Michael Coleman, 63, Dallatn-lane, was charged with wilfully breaking two window panes at the bakehouse of James Lee, 13, Pincers-brow, late on Monday night, and doing damage be the amount of 94. Defendant pleaded guilty.—P.C. Clarke said that at 11-25 on Mon- day night he was on duty with Constable Fish in the neiglabourhnod cf Pinner's-brow, when they heard a crash of glass falling. They stood for a moment or two, and then saw defendant deliberately rut his fiat through a second pane. He then put his hands into his pocaets, and walked towards them. They were in plain clothes. They arrested him and took him to the Police Station. Defendant told them he had broken the window panes out of spite.—James Lee, sad the damage was 91. He had not seen defendant before aod did not know why he should have any spite against hirn.—Defendant : I have' got no spite against him.—ln answer to the Chairman, P.C.Clarke said the defendant was sober at the time.—Defeniant Said he was in drink, or he should net have broken the window.—The Chief Constable: You had not a sign of drink in you. It was pure naughtiness.—The Chairman : Where did you get your drink 2—Defendant : On Town. bill. I will be a teetotaler if you will allew me to go.— 'The Chief Constable: That's an old song. We have lots of this window-breaking at night. It is supposed by some people to be rather a humorous thing to do.— Defendant was fined 135., including 9e. for the damage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUCIII POLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. Fred Monks (in the chair), and Messrs, Councillor Shaw fareen, T. Birchen, and Samuel Wright. ASSAULT ON THE FOOTBALL FIELD.--John William Mather, 43, Raw-road, Folly-lane was charged with assaulting P.O. Mdwards on the _football ground on Saturday afternoon.— P.C. Rdwards about ten minutes past five on the day in question he saw Prisoner creating a distn-bance. He wanted to strike another man. Witness interfered and prisoner naught hold of him by the throat. Witness kept hold of him and took him into custody. He believed the disturb ance arose out of some betting transaction. The Chief Con- stoble said a bet had beeen made and prisoner had been made stake holder and there was a disnute as to whiah had won. Prisoner was excited and resented the interference of the police. He would not press the case, as he did not con- sider the assault a blackguardly one, but thought it had been done on impulse.—The prisoner was fined 53. DESERTING HIS Two CHILDREN.—George Plumpton, 42. , Warburton-strett WAS charged on a warrant with running I away from the borough on 22nd August, and leaving his two ' child,-en chargeable to the Union —Prisoner pleaded guilty. —Mr. B. Shearer said pdsoner left the town on the 22nd August, leaving his two children aged 7 years and 4 years in lodgings. Witness ton's them into the Workhouse. Ile came back on Saturday. Prisoner was up in March last for the same thing The children at times were very mush Meted through him drinking —The bench sent him to prison for one month with hard labour. A FlltfioNigß'S Molt Foe LIRESTY.—John Foy was charged with being found in a dwelling-house in Buckley-street on Sunday night with Intent to commit a felony —Prisoner pleaded guilty. —The occupant of the house 32. Buckley-st., said he we ,t to bed leaving the house secure. A square of glass had been broken f om a window at the back and a board substituted. About 5.30 a in. on going downstairs he found prisoner astride the window. Witness did not know whether he was going out or coming in. He caught hold of him and detained him until the arrival of the police. The Chief Constable said that when the officer and his mangot outside the prisoner made a dash for liberty but was caught by the policeman The man appaared to be woer the in. donne of drink. He had been about six time before the eourt.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month with hard</p>
        <p>labour. THE ADJOURNED CASE 0E NEGLECT —lnspector McAteer Said in the case of James Graham, 25 Chorley-street, who on the 15th of last month was charged with neglect of four children, he wished the case withdrawn as an Improvement had taken place since the adjournment. —The case Was ac- cordingly withdrawn. AN UNWILLING PRISECITTOR —Samuel England warj charged with beating his wife Mary England on the 2nd Septerober.—The Chief Constable said a free summons had been granted td the woman, but when the case came up on Satnrday last she wished to withdraw it but the. Chairman would not allow it.—Mary Graham said her husband did not Come home at the usual time on the day in question and she went to Inak for him, but he got home whilst she was out. He had had some drink and struck her, knocking her against the slopetone He had been teetotal for 12 months before that. He was a good husband when not in drink.—The Chairman : Well as he Is such a good husband we will only fine hlin 3s. 6.1. MlsosmtlNEntre CASM—John Wilfred Deloaze, 62. Lyth- goes-lane. for being drunk in Horsemarket-street at 7.33 on Saturday night was fined 55 and costs or seven days. Edward Ashton,ll,New Burv-street,Salfdrd.and William Allen 15, Lombard-street, Salford, were each fined 2s. 64 , for being drunk and disorderly in Wilson Patten-street at 7.33 on Saturday night Theresa Virgo for the same offence in Aroley Walk. at 11 minnres to eleven on Saturday ni •ht was fined 55.. and coats William Henry Oman. drunk and disorderly, at 10 minutes to six on Sunday night, was fined ss. and costs. Thomas Marmire, cab driver. 8, Bowes-street. , for being drunk in Colborne street on Sunday the 4th Sep- tember whilst in charge aif a horse and carriage was fined ss. and costs —P.C. Baxendale proved the case. William Salt, greengrocer, 28. Clartrvrighi,-street, drunk in charge of a pony and cart on the sth Sepiember in Wildersnool. WAS fined 10s. and costs. John Thomas Finchett, 17. Perch-street. for keening a dog without a license on the 13th August was fined 7a. 64., and for havinT it at large unnvizzled on the same day Was fined ls,—P.C. Clough proved the ease. John Thomas Garner, a newsled fonnd sleeping out in Dallam-lane at twenty minutes past twelve that morning was handed over to Mr. B. Shearer who said he would obtain his admission Into the Workhonie. The Chief Coestabie said he would withdraw the charge under the circumstances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>_ TITIESDAT.—Before Mr. R..flarnett (in the chair) and Mr.</head>
        <p>_ TITIESDAT.—Before Mr. R..flarnett (in the chair) and Mr.</p>
        <p>- _ Birchen. SCHOOL ATTEND&amp;NCE Clo37:B.—The following par.mts were dealt with for not sending their children regularly to school : —Mary Barlow, 10 Howley-lane. boy sent te Hightown Truant Feb() ; Ralph Jeeyons Bird. 176, Mancheefer-road, school attendance order and fine 3 2e 6d • Martln Brett, 3. Shepherd's-row (two children), dismissed on promising to send children regularly; Petrick Collins. 9, Barry street. school attendance order ; end Richard Tranter, 2, Warburton- street, fined se. Mr. Jones, school attendance officer. prose- cuted. TEmpennor Aternorayg.—Vary Ann Wooletencr• ft applied for teniporary authority to sell at the Griffin Hotel, Horse- market-street in lieu of Annie Pritchard. There was no Objection from the pollee. and the application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY sEssioNS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY sEssioNS.</p>
        <p>_ MONDA.Y.—Before Messrs. G. S. Davies (presiding), and A. R. Norman. DETAtrt.TING TIATEPAYEAS. —ln respect of failure to meet Proceeded against 96 by Mr. J the demands for the poor rite. persons were proce-uied against by Mr. W. Sittton (assistant overseer) ; 4. In respect of &amp;strict rate, and 12 Of water rate. were . D. Jones (Urban District Council collector).—The orders lsked for were granted in almost all the cases, as Well its eostg. Dattyg AND DisOßDEßTX.—Tilinbeth Wilkinson. of Robert.. stre was summoner-LEO the instance of the police, for being drunk and disorderly in High-street, on the 29th ultimo.— Digendant pleaded guilty. and WAS fined ss. including coats.— The ease was stated by P.C. Bdwards. QUARIVELSomm NEIGHBOURS.—SamneI Atherton, of Cooper- street, was summoned by Sarah Alice Crank a neighbour, for assaniting her on the previous Wednesday. night.—ComPlain- ant. who anpeared in court with her eye terribl discoloured, said that the assault w s ^onnnith.l asp wits l°° fIP the</p>
