      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>street, when the defendant cams up to her and said that he would "knock herb— n-ck out." The assault was entirely unprovoked —Cross-examined : She did not run after the defendant and say "Here I am. what are you going to do at me," nor did she try to strike him.—Mrs. Eliza Dunning, testified to seeing the assault.—Cross-examined witness said she knew nothing more about the affair; only she saw the defendant strike her sister-In-law—The husband of the com- plainant stated, on the previous evening he met the defend- ant who told him to "do something to stop that wife of his," or he would "knock her b— neck out." To which he (witness) replied "Sammy don't do anything you will be sorry for ; and if you will take a fool's advice you will let the matter rest." He could not say what led up to the assault (which he did not see), but he saw his wife had been badly bruised about the eye and chin. She had been to the doctor.—The delendant stated that he only put up his arms in self defence, and caught the woman awkwardly without intention,of hurting her, when she aimed a blow at him with a jug she had in her hand.—James Koayon said, on the Wednesday night, he called for Atherton, for the purpose of going with him to the swimming gala. On the way up the street they heard someone running after them, and noticed it was Mrs. Crank. She called out to Atherton " I am here, now what are you going to do at me." Defendant turned suddenly, and caught the woman in the face with his elbow, and she fell to the floor.—Cross-examined He was sure of what he said, as he saw everything that took place Inspector Hicks, called for the defence, said he could only explain to the magistrates that it was six of one, and half- a-dozen of the other." What he would call the result of a street squabble —Atherton then made a leng•hy statement to the Bench, in which he stated that he had been subject to tbe annoyanc -s of the complainant for a long time past, for no accountable reason. He hid also been oit of work for some tinae,and the woman was continually reproaching him for neg- lecting his clailiren.—The Bench considered that defendant had po right to strike a woman,and snould have sought some Other remedy, he would be fined 10s. and 12s. 63. costs; or 14 days in default.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDlY.—Before Messrs. J. Ocklestoa Oa the chair) and A. Norman. _ . .</p>
        <p>DISORDERLY SCENE I7.6A—SIS—T.II—RE. T.—Joseph Yates and Joseph Cliffe were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Gas-street on Thursday night.—P C. Fry stated that he heard a row in Gas-street, slid on going there found the prisoners drunk and creating a disturbance, and he ordered them away. At twelve o'clock he was oalled to another dis- turbance in a house, and on going there he found the prisoners fighting, and the house was like a slaughter house. Witness and Sergeant Cooper put them out of the house, and in twenty minutes afterwards found them k eking at the door and trying to get into the place again.—Sergeant Cooper gave corroborat ye evidence. They were obliged to lock the prisoners up in consequence of their disorderly behaviour. —Mr. Ockleston said the men were taking the law into their own hands, which they had no right to do. and as that sort of thing must be put down, prisoners would be sent to gaol for a month's hard labour. A DETERMINED PIUS •NEB RESOLVES TO DO NO WORK.— Freierick Chell, was brought RD on remand charged with , stealing Dos of bacon, value is.. the property of Edwin Gregory. on the let September.—Prosecutor stated that he was a provision dealer,and resided in Bridge-street, Runcorn. , On the morning of the Ist September witness was star:ding outside his shop when the prisoner walked into the premises and picked up a piece of bacon from the counter. Witness asked him to put it down, and the prisoner swore at him and said he could thrash a dozen or 20 men like him. He then went out of the shop with the bacon and walked down Bridge- street. The prisoner offered to gave the bacon to witness's lad, but he would not take it. The police were afterwards informed of the theft.--Prisoner : I Only took it for a bit of nonsense.- Sergeant Cooper said he apprehended the prisoner , and brought him to the Police Station. He found the bacon in his possession. Prisoner said he had been sent to gaol for two months, and he had lost his business only through getting souls drink. Ile had got really dissatisfisd about being in prison. He alleged that this was all through the police swearing a lot of lies, :tergeant Lee swore a lot of lies and got him two months when he was in his own house. He never stole anything in his life. He gave a lot more away in Runcorn than ever he stoie.—Prisoner was further charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street. on the Ist September.—Sergeant Cooper proved the case, and said the prisoner was so violent that it took three of them to look him up.—Prisoner : I think it was time to be " obstropulous " when you get abused and knocked about the same as I have been in these cells. When I have come as quiet al a lamb they have knocked me about shameful. They have not taken fines They have sent me to prison, and I have lost my business.— Prosecutor: I don't wish to press ths charge against him. The fact that he offered to give it to the lad showed that be did not intend stealing it.—Questioned by Mr. Norman, com- plainant said he did not know whether the prisoner knew It was his lad.—Mr. Johnson (clerk) said there was a certificate from toe prison doctor to the effect that during the time the prisoner had been in gaol he had been under careful observe- tion, and nothing strange had been noticed in his manner. Mr. Oeklestan (to prisoner) : You promised to sign teetotal the last time you were here ?—Prisoner : I found a glass of tser would do me good sometimes.—The Chairman : It does not do you good.—Prisoner : My wife makes me a deal worse. It is other people who set her on to it, I believe.— The Chairman : You bring it on yourself.—Prisener : I don't bring it on myselt at all. It is scamps.—The Chairman: Bet you were drunk.—Prisoner: They don't care what they really do to me, because I have been punished scaodalously. —The Chairman said it was no use fining the prisoner, it was the drink that was the cause of his present position. He would havetn go to prison for a month with hs.rd labour, and the other case would be dismissed. He hoped it would be warning to him—Prisoner: I shan't do a stroke whrn get there ; not one stroke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RECOGNITION OF ERE NEW WESLEYAN MINISTER.</head>
        <p>RECOGNITION OF ERE NEW WESLEYAN MINISTER.</p>
        <p>A meeting was hold in the Bold-street Wesleyan sem, on Monday evening far the purpose of welcoming to the circuit the Rev. EL G. Roberta, who has been appointed to succeed the Rev. W. G. Triggs Friend 4 were present from nearly all parts of the circui7. For about an hour an "At home" was held, clurin4 which time the rev, gentleman was introduced to those present. Light refreshments were handed round, followed by a meeting during which several speeches of welcome were de- livered. The rneelog was opened by the superintendent of the circuit (the Rev. W. Potts). Mr. John Taylor ; was voted to the chair, and he was supported on tits I platform by ha co-circuit steward, Mr, James D Ads. Speeches wee delivrred representing the van-ms 6oljp. ties in the Warrington circuit hy Mr. Taylor for Roll. street Chapel, by Mr. William Piesieson, for fi,w,ey. road Chapel, bv Mr. R. G. lienshall for Latchford, and by Mice Bessie Dutton, for the Moore friends. The Rev. GBORGE HARBOTTLE said a few words in favour of itinerancy, and observed that they would not have been able to hold that meeting but for that Pine,- ancy. A great deal had been said against it in the PS., but most things John Wesley had a hard imninblogrtervmwl:krso of wisdom and stood the test of tim on e. It was wderful ho smoothly chang in the Wesleytn brought about. One man went and another came to fill hes place. There was no ffelon, no diffic slty, and everything went off smoothly, and that was certainly a great advantage for the Church. Tha itinerancy was 4 for the benefit of the pPopie ; it tended to keen amongst them a young ministry. Of necessi-y the work of the mini6try was hard and difficult, and when a man ierst to 60 years of age circuits ceased to invite him, and the effect was to preserve yeung men wh e were in vigOrri le health and in full possession of their powers, That also was an advantage. It doubtlPv pressed hard on the ministers' wiyes,but they could not have a great advan- tage without a great disadvantage. He assured Mr. Roberts from his knowlelge of the circuit, gained by two years' residence in it, teat he had nian, amonizat a warm-hearted and kindly-disposed people. The 011thYlk of the circuit at the present time was particularly hope- ful, and generally the pevle were in an optimistic mood. He would find the present superintendent rf the circuit one of the bsst men in Methodism, and with such a people and with such loyal colleagues Mr. Rcherts ought to have a very happy time here. (Applause.) , The Rev. IL G. ROBERTS, on rising to respond to the warm welcome extended to him, said he had not come to Warrington with the object of making any changes in the order of public service. The need at the present time was for increased spirituality in all their churches. He knew of no new gospel ;he knew of noth- ing they needed but the Gospel of the Lord Jesus Christ. He was gal to be assured of loyal cclleagurs in the ministry and the circuit. The Metiodist ministry he regarded as the most wonderful brothrnood in the world, and he was equally gratified saiih the account that had been given of the friends in the Warrington circuit generally. It was not often in a man's life that he had betore him such a gathering as that which it Wie his privilege to address that night, and he felt very greatly impressed by it. A remark ha I been mide by a preceding speaker hs fully aer ere]. with, and that was he thought that the wants of the yonng people of the middle classes in Method;sm hid not beun fully met in the past, and something should, therefore, be done to meet their requirements and satisfy their needs. He had always taken, and would continue to take, a great interest in the young. He could net Fay that ho was very much enamoured of the itinerancy ix itself. The incessant changes were a great trial in some respects. They seemed to be a hindrance ti a man's usefulness, bat he was prepared loyally to accept Methodism as it stood, and to do his best as a Methodist preacher with the machinery as it existed. He thanked them all very heartily for the warmth of their reception, and trust .d that the ministry upon which he had just entered would be one of happiness to himself and of usefulness to the circuit. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LIBERAL WARD MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WARD MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday eveninz a large and enthusiastic meeting of the St. Austin's Liberal Ward Committee was held in the Working Men's Mission Schoo' room. Liverpool-road. Mr. William Roberts, chairman of the ward, occupied the chair. Councillor Tinnion moved,and Mr. Samuel Dunbobbin seconded a reso- lution thanking Councillor Thomas Barton for his past services in the Town Council. The resolution was supported by Messrs. Dunn, Corradine, Dixon, and many others, and was unanimouzly and enthusi asticallV adooted. Councillor Burton thanked the meeting for the cor fiat appreciation of his services, and consented to offer himself for re- election on November Ist.</p>
