      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>TtIE EXAMINEE, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 16, 1898.</head>
        <p>TtIE EXAMINEE, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 16, 1898.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. Tx view of the approaching Football Season, it may be in terestiug to state that the world-famed manufacturers GEORGE G Busiwir lz Co., of London, are preparing a stoc I Baas, which for quality, uniformity and durahility, will see unique. These balls will be distinguished by the appear- thereon of their well-known trade mark .4(CCO(C. Ask he local Sports Dealer for their Catalogue. If not pro airable apply to 36 &amp; 33, Queen Victoria-street. London. Factories Feckharn, London ; and Elmswell, Suffolk.</p>
        <p>LA.NCA.SHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. THE CHIMPIONS AT WARPINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE HOHESTERS CRUiHED.</p>
        <p>It was a great day at Warrington on Saturday. , The visit of Oldham always rouses football enthu- siasm in the town to fever heat, with the natural consequence that a huge crowd finds its way to t he Wilderspoel ground. About 8,000 people resoiited there en Satirelay, including about 1,500 from Old- ham. Most clubs have a wholesome respect for Farrington on their own ground,aed Oldham threw no chances away by bringing a weak team. They had, in fact, the strongest available fifteen. T. Martin resumed on the right wing, and in addition the three-quarter line contained Tom Davie, late Treherbert,a Welsh player for whom there has been much angling. Warrington were in the unfortu- nate predicament of being without Boscow, Mason, and Barrett, the two former of whom were on the sick list, and the latter 'reemployed. Jones was promoted from the "A "team to Boscow's place, , while Su How and Swift replaced the two absent forwards. Excellent weather fig lin prevailed, a fairly strong breeze making the conditions pleasan- ter than the previous week. Mr. Priestley,Brough- ton, was the referee, and the following were the players composing the two teams : Oldham : Thomas, full back; I. Taylor, T. Davies,S. Lees, and T. Martin, three-quarters: A. Lees and J. Lawton, half backs ; Bonsor,Moffatt, Merrell, J. Lees, F Davies. Freter, Porter, and Edwards. forwards. Warrington ' • Jones, full-back ; Moores, Isherwood, I Hallam and Rolcroft, three-quarters ; J. T. Bate, and I 11. B ate, half backs ' • Taylor, Naylor, Deakin, Swift, I Fairhurst, Boardman, Eden, and Sudlow, forwards- I Warrington had the good fortune to win the toss, and I tiros secure the advantages to be derived from: both wind and sun in the first half. Moffatt stetted for Oldham, Isherwood securing andi putting in a dashing run to centre. Fast play I was the order from the commencement. Old- ham soon had the ball out, but Hallam checked their passing and punted into safe quarters. The War- rington halves opened a ;natty passing movement,which had just got going nicely when Hallam mulled his chance. Oldham's handling of the ball was superb, and from some loose play they secured the ball, and ahnost the whole team joined in a combined series of passes which entered the home half. Warrington's determined I tackling counted a lot in their favour, and Oldham had I many chances nipped in the bud. " Smack " and h s brother were in rtrumental in causieg an invasion of the Oldham citadel, Fairhurst and R. Bata rnakihg a com- bined race t 3 the Oldham line. Excitement ran high when Warrington got so near success, and superhuman efforts were made to break through. A clever bit of passing on the h •me side rounded the Oldham backs and Elelcroft should have scored from the pass given him, but he fumbled the ball. Davies got away only te begrassed promptly by Isherwood, who was playing a greed game. For a spell it really seemed as if Warrington m ist score, but finally Old- ham replied with a beautiful movement which enabled Martin to break away, and relieve the pressure on his line. A penalty against A,. Lees was well utilised by Elo!croft, and Werringten again attacked. A punt from touch, finely supported by the borne forwards, looked cart do to bring ahout Oldham's downfall, but Thomas marked with marvellous cooleess. Hie kick failed to relieve however, and Oldham were k trd put to it to hold their own. Lawton, Davis, and Taylor, made a speedy burst to centre, but a forward pass spoiled any forther chance. Then the other wing got going, and Martin safely passed Holcreft, only to step in touch later on. These flying attempts by Oldham needed a lot of check- ing, and some relief was Afforded Warrington when " Smack " judiciously punted down the field. and Thomas was downed by Isherwoel and Mseres. A for ward rush by Warrington crossed the visitors' line, and a try was almost gained, when Thomas saved his side with great dexterity. Nice passing by Oldham cleared the danger and Ike Taylor was run in touch by Jones after a speedy rush down the field. Good defensive play by Hallam eased the home forwards c meiderablv, Moores coming in for a hearty cheer for a tricky run and useful kicir. Warrington were again within an ace of scoring, "Smack" dropping at goal ineffectually. Moores made another gallant effort, but was winded and had to retire Fairhurst tak- ing his place. Though thes handicapped the hornes•ers continued their onslaught and " Smack" got a beautiful opening but chose to pass and Lawton intercepted and quickly transferred play to the other end. The ball WAS punted past Jones, and Martin sprinted up, seized the ball and was over between the posts in a twinkling. A knock on caused the try to be disallowed, and half-time then arrive I with a blank scoring board. On resuming, Meores folk his place and received a welcoming cheer,for his services looked like being badly needed. With al the advantages in their favour Old- ham began to show what they could do. Martin was only just tripped up by Bate in time, or he would have SC -mei after brilliant combination. " Smack" was to the fore again wile a plucky save, and Moores changed the aspect of affairs by another daring run. Oldham utilised a tee ch•kick with effect,Fairhurst coming to the rescue only in the nick of time. First one wing and then the other menaced the home line. Lawton and Lees kmt the ball rolling, and from a clever movemeet by the former Ton Davies notched his first try in Northern Union football and practically settled the issue of the game. nam Lees failed at goal. Warrington rallied momentarily but their lack of speed was painfully evi- dent. Oldham appeared to be getting fresher and fairly demorelised their opponents. After paving right along the line, Taylor bounded over, but Moores manage] to shove him in touch. Then Prater was pulled down elenost under the cross-bar. SO rapidly did Oldham vary the attack that they were almost irresistible. At last l'Avlr was rewarded for his previous failures and secured the second try after fine passing, Frator being unsucceesfol with the goal-kick. Martin crossed the line again after a knock-on and Was re-called, but five minutes before time he reoeated the effort in fine style eni scored an unmietakeeble try. Tnere was no eel. To the last minute Warriegton had to defend hard, T. Davies having the hardest of luck in losing the hall when over the line. Warrington were net sorry weea the whistle at lait sounded finally, and Oldham bore Off the honoues by 3 tries (9 point;) to nil. WIDNES V. WIGAN. ••• -</p>
        <p>Plavol at Wieran--ora. ...91;t71;d-ay. Efoitititle oponed with Wigan playing fourteen men' aid the visitors soon found their way in'o the home' quarters. Martin and Nevitt succeeded in taking play ta the centre, whence Widaes were forced back, ani Bit 3V was held Close to his own line. Here Haslatn and Halli wet both made attempts to cross, but Batev was too smart for them. Timoney having come to the r,scul : Nevitt, was instrumental in avering danger and tran.f3rring opercions to the borne half. Wigan were reinforced at this stage by Winstaolay, their back, and then f3r several minutes the Widnes men found them- selvei having the worst of matters. Timon3y subse- quently opened the scoring by kicking a goal from a liffie.ult position. At the inter vat Wigan were thus lead- ing by two paints to nothing. But having now lost the servics of Pet, one of tho forwards, and having the disadvantages of facing a strong wind, it was evident early in this half that the visitors would make it exceed- ingly warm for the local men. Nevitt initiated a srroog attalr which followed, but Winstanley and Timoney displayed fine form, and the visitors were checked on several occasions when their prospects looked promising. 'Mainly owing to the opposition of these two playere, Wigan managed to maintain their lead until close Moon thre,quarter time, but ones Widnes had equalised through Batey, the home reoresentatives, no doubt feel- ing the strain of fighting with fourteen men, fell all to pieces, and ere the referee announced f. time," the Wid- nesiana had forged ahead to the extent of ten points to</p>
        <p>two. ST. HELENS v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. Most inauspicious was the first appearance of St. Helens on their own ground on Saturday. They had to meet the strong team from Broughton. In the opening movements the Saints hal two or three nice chances,but unfortunately for them the decisions of the referee were againet them. In each instance, too, the spectator; thought the official in error. The Rangers got into attacking position, and Messenger iand Nelson demon- erated how making A mere pretence of putting the ball into the scrummage could make try-getting easy. Fro -n the two halves the ball was sent to Chapman, and Han- son galloped in at the corner. This was bad enous,h, but when Messenger claimed and got a fair catch by taking the ball close to the ground from the feet of a wheeling pack, the Saints were paralysed. They were under their own posts, and the goal by Nelson was a rec,nalar gift. Dnck was stopped on the touch line inside the St. Helens quartera, hut no next scrummage saw MPS ongrr run r3und and, " hutting" his would-be-tacklers,scrambled over the line. The goal was again missed, but Duck's oppoatuoity arrived Ehnrtly afterwards, and his try at the same spot was cleverly improved by Chapman. When ends were changed with the Rangers leading by thirtcen points it was seen that the sun had departed, and with it the hopes of the " Saints' " supporters. Sum, dashing play found the home team under the Rangers' posts. and Unice in succession the ball was Ilim.••••••</p>
        <p>—Tor—I,IoTTIERS.—Mrs. Wilmslow's Soothing Syrup has been for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their chil- dren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste : it pro- duces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes 4. as bright as a_button," Of all chemists Is. 14d. per bottle.-441.</p>
        <p>snatched from the hands of a St. Helens forward when the coveted try was being completed. Thera was some desperate tackling, and tie play was fas: and furious. To say the game degenerated would be a mild descrip- tion. Whether the Rangers heavy weights were the ag- gressors or not is a mitt ir of opinion, bat they were in- vsriably seefi to b uppermost. The referee had never seemed to hive proper control, bin when, after twice warning Robinson of the Ringirs,he saw ODD deliberately punch Glad win whilst the official was within a yard of him he had no opi Jn. Unforunately the S:. Helens man—who wao, as uaualoinderneath—pushed him off in retalialon,and Ala the mei were ordered off the field Then came the worst feature of the affair. Glad wiu evidently f ,It aggrieved and want d satisf action from Rotinsoa,and as tile latter declined to leave the touch- line to oblige him, Glad Nin gave him a smart knock on the face as an inducement. There was an uproar at Once. The referee's atter .ion was called to the mttter, but though the game was rosum d the excitement at the dressing tent did not sub iide till Robinson was sent away in a conveyance by the St Helens officials. During the remainder of the game the Sain's were pressing severely, but only managed to score once. Same clever passing by Boyle. Traynor, and Barnes,ending in Traynor again coming into po session, and jamping over Damson to score. Ths final result was Broughton Rangers two goals, three tries ; St. Helens one try. SALFORD V. LEIGH. . .</p>
        <p>At S Alford, beFor-e-6,66T,P;c7a-t-;r-s: Rh ip?4 started, and after ten minutes, play Jam's Coleman dropped a gial and at half-time Leigh led by two points. Subse• quently S Word played up strongly, and Jack Williams 41311 Giedhill scorA tee; in quick succession, Griffiths f Ailing at the place kick. R- salt—Salford two tries (six points); Leigh, goal (two points). TYLDESLEILIT.. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>- --- At Tyldesley, betore 1,500 spectat n.s. The teams were very evenly matched, though neither side showed brilliant form. Wersley intercopie -I a pass, and F c 'red a fine try for Stockport, and in the earns half Trow kicked a podalry goal. In the second half, France wored spleadid try fir Tvldesley after grand passing. Just before time Hope scored. lirsult—Tyldesley,eight points; Stockport, three points. MORECAMBE V. SIVINTON.</p>
        <p>At M)r3cambs, -in fine weather. The passing move. melts of the visitors' team were smart. Bevan scored 4 tries and Valentine one. Half-time—Swinton, 15 points; Morecambe, nil. In the second half Morecambe, showed to greater advantage, but could not keep Bevan frun securing his fifth try. Result Swincon 6 tries, More3amise nil.</p>
        <p>had to be conceded, which Hampson headed out, Mid- field play followed, and Lee and Hughes getting pos- session the former shot an easy goal. Give and take play followed and the game ended in favour of Earlestown by 4 goals to LB. BRUNSWICK GUILD (RUNCORN) V. WIDNES COMBINATION Played at Runcorn, on Saturday, in splendid weather After the preliminary exchanges, the Guild took play up the field, and after some neat passing amongst the fur- wards, of whom Weston and Aokers were prominent, Weston scored a grand goal for the homesters. From the centre kick off,the Guild again :nada nearly all tie run- ning, but could not score on account of the grand defen- sive play of the.visitors. Through erratic shooting when near the goal, the ball was frequently shct over tie bar. From a burst away, the visitors had a spell of at:acking, and after being partly repulsed they returoed to the at- tack, and owing to a misunderstanding between the backs and goalkeepers, scored a rather easy goal. From now until the interval, play was chiefly at the other end, but no further alteration resulted, and half time was called with the scores 1 goal each. On resuming ( lown hill) the Guild kept up a continual attack and only on two occasions was the home goalke Ter troubled, and then they were easily routed. The Guild putting on 3 more goals (Weston two and Ackers one) won easily by 4 goals to one: If the form displayed thus far is kept up, the Guild will take a lot of heating, the com- bination of the forwards being splendid, and the backs are also very safe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION..</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION..</p>
        <p>WILIMEN V. LAN-CI-WE-1i ---;ii-iiGra-111..,12. Result :—Lanca.ter, elevea points ; Walkden, BIACKTOOL v. MILLOI4I.—A.t Blackpool, R,sult : Millen, eight pals twelve tries (52 ',Ants) ; Blackpool nil.</p>
        <p>BARTON T. BARROW.—At Blew], Result :—Barrow, one goal and two tries (eight points) ; Barton, one goat (two points).</p>
        <p>" A " TEAM CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON 47A,"-;. OL-D-HAI,/ " A."—The match be- tween three teams at the Watershedlings enclosure on Saturday was witnessed by a large number of specta- tors. The home team included Woodhead of Carestou Moor, full back ; Morgan Rees, of Wales ; and Williams, Sellers. and Ellis of the first team. Oldham won the toes and Warrington kicked eff against a strong wind. Old- ham attacked strongly but Duckw'rth relieved to half way, where some sharp work took place. The game became very fast, and a grand passing movement by the home team ended in Rennie scoring a fine try ;Ellis failing. Soortly after Warringten were penalised and Ellis dropped a goal. The half time score Was 1 goal, 1 try. WArrineon attacked repeatedly in the second halt but had hard lines. Crank and Radcliffe made grand rem Vat were brought down on the line. No further score WAS made, the homeaterg winning by five points. YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPEFITION,</p>
        <p>BRAMLEY V. WAKEFIELD TRINIVE.—At Brantley. Re- sult—Bromley, 8 points ; Trinity, 5. Huta, V. BATLEY' —Lit Hull, before 10,000 spectators Plull won a stubb-irn game by a dripped goal to nil. HECKHONDWIRE V. HUDDER9FIED.—&amp;t Heckmond wike Result—Huddersfield, one goat and four tries (14 points)l Heckmond w;k 4, LIVEKSEDGE V. MAYNINGHAIK.—At Liversedge.Result-1 Manningham, one goal and three tries (11 points) Liversedze, two goals (four points). HUNSLET V. HALIFAX.—At Hun4let, before about 7.000 spectators. Result—Flunslet, 4 goals 4 tries (20 points) ; Half Ix. 1 goal (2 points), BRIGHOUSE V. CASTLEFORD.—At Brizhouse before 2,000 spectators. Rasult—Brighouse,two goals one try (seven points) ; Castleford, one try (3 points). BRADFORD V. LEEDS PARISH CHURCH.— .kt Bradford, before four thou.and spectators Result—Bradford, 3 goals 5 tries (21 points) ; Leeds Parish Courch, nil. LEEDS V. HOLBEEK.—A crowd of 8,000 witnessed this team at Leis. 13.9sult—Holbeck, two goals one try (s,y,n points) ; Leads, one godl and one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>A.SSOCIATioA Ili ALL.</head>
        <p>A.SSOCIATioA Ili ALL.</p>
        <p>LAIC HFORD BRITTANI V. WARRINGTON POST</p>
        <p>OFFICE.</p>
        <p>This enestgement came off on Saturday, on the ground of the former,- the visi*ors bringing a weak team. Bibby for the Brittania team kicked off, an i after a few minutes of the game Told scared the first point. Brittania scored I. befere an 1 4 aft Sr the interval. Final: Brittania 8 goal. ; Post Office, nil. NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS v. WARRINGTON TRINITY. Pais match opened the season at Newton on Saturday. 0 wing to counter attractions that° was only a poor attendance. The previous week the Trinitarians bee.' Newton Athletic by 5 goals to 3, which of course made the match under notice all the inure interesting. G. Houghton, the Newton skipper, wen the ties, and the visiting centre kicked off. Tne game WAS Very keenly contested, the Warrington men being the first to score, the referee allowing the point although a strong app sal Was made for offside. This made play all the more lively, and Butler with a grand shot caused the goal- keeper to give a corner. The visitors came ' again and boat Irving a second time. Try as they would Newton could not get through,their efforts being att ended by the viiess luck, Frankland was hurt, but aeon resumed. C. Hill at last got one thr nigh for Newton from a passfrom the right and when half time arrived the visitors were leading. The visitors were outplayed in the second halt but they possessed a custodian whom they may well be proud of. He kept out shots innumerable, bat Rhuelbotham with as goad a shot as one could wish t see, put the teams on an equality. Now it was a case of who was going to win, and the Dane was very warm. Just before tee close, however, Hill amid much entihn- si ism gave the victory to Newton with a good shot which made the game stand when the whistle blew Newton 3, Trinity 2. Even the most ardent Warrington supporter present could not say but that the best team had won,as taking the play all through they were the superior party. The backs were however not as safe as usual, and of the halves, Hamilton wa the bast, he playing as substitute for Bell, Galloway was also very good especially where head work was con- cerned, but Morri soy should keee in his position instead of rambling all over the field. The forwards combined well at times but if they shot a little more Instead of passing so much when directly in front,the goal average would without doubt benefit thereby. It w.s certainly a surprise to many the way the visitars played and it will take the St Elphins all their time to keep the premier position in Warrington Association football. In fact some of the Trinitariaes quietly believe that they are the best team. The Derby day is Saturday next wine. Hudsons the champions of the combination visit Newton and there should be a record gate. NEWTON ATFILE riG v. sr HELENS Y.M.C.A. Played on the Mesnes ground on Saturday, the home team showing their superiority throughout and winning by 3 enals to 1. NEWTON RESERVE v. ECCLES ELLESMERE. The newly formed reserve team of Newton journey to Eccles on Satorday, and displayed their scoring abilities bv defeating Eccles by 7 goals to nil. The score at the interval was 6 goals to nil, but in the second half the hour team showed up better and it was mainly due to the sterling defence of Ellarn in goal that the home eleven were prevented fram scoring. EIRLESTOWN V. BIMFURLONG ROVERS. Earlestown opened their season at home on Saturday toy an Allianee fixture with Bamfurlong Rovers. The horns team Yoked off up hill and Bamfurlong returned. By a series of three's in. Earlestown got up and became dangerous, a free kick in front of goal being headed over. A continued pressure was relieved after a throw in, but a foul against Bamfurlong again placed the hems team in a dangerous position, the visitors worked the ball away and essayed a shot at the home goal, but it went outside. A foul in front of goal gave Bamfurlong a chance, but the defence was too good. Mid-field exchanges resulted in Earlestown getting in front of the visitors goal. a rare opportunity to score being mulled by " Cap "Lee missing his kick. Finally R. Lee gothis toe to the ball and shot through. After the kick from centre, Parr placed the ball, and " Cap " Lee getting hold by some clever dribbling ran through the whole of the Bamfurlong men and scored. Bamfurlong raced down, but were driven back by the half backs. Again Earlestown got possession and "Cap "Lee running up passed to Marsh who shot across and Lee missed scoring by inches. Bamfurlong pot down and after a series of , exchanges they troubled Chew with a long shot forcing him to concede a corner. Half time arrived with no further addition to the score. With the change of ends it was expected Earlestown would make things lively, but the visitors apneared to play much stronger up hill than in the first half of the came. Ultimately Earlestowa got away and Lee shot in; it was returned and in the scrimmage it was put through by one of the visitors backs, but for some reason the point was disallowed. From the kick off Hampaon made a clever run, but his final shot went wide. Bamfurlong paid a viait to the top goal, but they were driven back and Aldridge re- lieving Hampson got the ball and steadying himself centred beautifully to "Cap" Lee which notched another Point. The same thing happened a few minutes later, but Lee shot wiete. Bamfurlong raced away and got dangerously near the top goal, but the backs relieved, and a visit was paid to the bottom end where a corner</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>TOO MUCH SALL</head>
        <p>TOO MUCH SALL</p>
        <p>IN the western part of central North America—well over towards the Pacific Coast, yet not within some 500 miles of it —is the great Salt Lake, one of the most re- markable bodies of water on the globe. Comparing equal volumes of w tt2r it contains about twice as much salt as the ocean. Of course it is not available for drink- ing. You can bath in it if you wish. first making sure thateyour skin is sound and tight all over; other wise you will have the sensation of being. washed with brine. Neither must any of the water get into your eyes or down your throat, inasmuch as it is largely dashed with potash, a bad thing to have inside the human economy. Not long ago a young man, not acquainted with the peculiar properties of the water of Salt Lake, tried to commit suicide by drowning himself in it. It was no use. The more he gave:himself up to sink the more 6afely and serenely he floated on the surface. Finally he abandoned the idea and swam ashore. Precisely the kind of water for shipwrecks is the great Salt Lake,only none ever happen there. Beautifully clear and crystalline is the water of this wonderful lake. No sparkling spring is more tempting to the thlrsty wanderer. Yet no 'man, ;animal or bird drink it. For disease and death lurk there. , Now let us rush our thoughts back to England and see why the two little letters that are to follow should have suggested the forbidden lake far.away ;in Utah— the land of the Mormons.</p>
        <p>" For several yeas," says the first, " I suffered from liver complaint and indigestion. I had great pain and weight at the right side. My appetite was poor, and after eating I had grest pain at the chest and laxcic. I became very weak and had many atta,...ks of giddiness. When out walking I would reel, and was obliged to catch hold of something to prevent me from falling. "In this state I continued for about three years, and quite thought my case was chronic. A friend then recommended Mother Seigel's Syrup. I got a bottle,and after taking it I was much better. I cantinued with it, and soon all the pain left me. When; I had used four bottles, I was as strong and well as ever, and have since been in good health. You can use this statement as you like." (Signed) John Prest, 38, Grimshaw Park, Black- burn, December 28th, 1897.</p>
        <p>"For many years," writes another, " my daughter Mary H., has suffered from indigestion. She felt low, weak and languid, and had no energy. Her appetite was poor, and after eating sh 3 was racked with pain, and re much swollen that she had to loosen her clothing. She had so much pain that she was afraid t) eat. She lost sleep at night, and became very weak. She took rard eines of various kinds, but obtained no relief from them.</p>
        <p>"I then recommended her to try a medicine which had done me an much good—called Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. She followed my advice, and after having used it aishort time:she could eat well, her food agreed with her, and she gained strength. By taking lan occasional dose she keeps in good health. You are free to publish this statement as you like." (Signed) Mrs. A. Datton, wife of Mr.Dutton, grocer, &amp;c.,Notting• ham-road, Borrowash, Darby, December 29. h, 1897. Be eood enough now to favoar M 3 with your especial attention for a moment. Those conditions of ths body which we call "diseases" are caused either by poisonous matters which enter from without, or by similar matter generated or produced within. The poisons from with- out are called " germs" and may enter by way of the lungs, the stomach or the skin. The point is that they get into the blood, by which they are circulated, setting up diseases each after its kind. Cholera, consumption, And the different fevers are thus caused.</p>
        <p>Bat perhaps the worse poisons wherewith the body has to deal are created within itself—manufactured, as we may say, on the premises. They consist in part of the wora-out material of the body itself, and in part of undigested, or partially digested food, winch ferments, rots, decomposes in the stomach and in the intestines. The disease (breeding and deadly principles ferns these unless speedily removed), render the blood impure and foul,and therefore unfit to sustain and nourish the sys- tem. This state of thing is what:we familiarily speak of as indigestion or dyspepsia—onelof the most dangerous of all our complaints. Attended as it usually is, by in- activity of the bowels, the resulting constipation makes the body like unto a tank full of festering mattar. This being so, can we wonder that people in such condition suffer as our correspondents tell us they did ? Certainly not.</p>
        <p>What (obviously) needs to b 3 done'? Two things. Fist, the stomach most be sst in order and strengthened eo as to b 3 able to digest all thi food required to keep us going. Second, the filth, the poisons, must be cleared out of the body at once—before worse come' of it. Isn't that common sense ? Yes. Well, that is what Mother Seigel's Syrup does, as no °Vier ntsdicine doss it, that we know about. When you don't feel right, the reason 'is, probably, indigestion and clogged liver and intes- tines. Look to it immedintely. Take the Syrup right out of hand.</p>
        <p>As to the Great Salt Lake ? Oh, to be sure. Well,the salt, which rnakm it practically a dead ilea, cornea fr,m the ground in which it lies. There is uo getting rid of it. Salt is good (on meat and in the sea), but not in a mountain lake.</p>
        <p>Neither ar3 uric acid and other indigestive poisons good in your blood. The Salt Lake has no outlet. Your body has several. Mother Seigel's Syrup will use them. Try and sweeten yourself from crown to toe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>SUNSET BY THE SEA.</head>
        <p>SUNSET BY THE SEA.</p>
        <p>The storm is hushed; the rolling waves, By adverse winds now lashed no more, Returning to their ancient caves, With ripples lave this lonely shore. And as day's Orb, descending slow, Draws nearer to the distant west, We trace his footsteps in the glow That flecks with gold the ocean's breast. On those tall cliffs that stately rise And Nature's wilder moods display, How beautiful his glory lies, While shadows round their bases stray. And yonder barque, by tempest tried, With sails aloft, and buoyant yet, Sails homeward with the flowing tide. To anchor ere the sun has set. 0 Thou, eternal Power Divine, All nature owns thy boundless will ; The storm and calm alike are thine, And show thy might and mercy still. Like you frail barque we too have known The heavings of life's troubled sea ; And oft in darkness, and alone, Have, trembling,turned for help to thee. But now our evening time has come, The broad'ning shadows round us creep ; And much we long to reach our home, While sunshine lingers on the deep. And as we near the closing scene— The port of rest for weary souls, 0 let no shadow intervene, And calm the sea that round it rolls. J. H. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
      </div>
