      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ketirrs'i to tbe ttor.</head>
        <p>ketirrs'i to tbe ttor.</p>
        <p>BAPTISMAL REG EN ERATI</p>
        <p>Sir,—fhe diffieulties of your cerrespond eat " C .nolic Baptist," are bsel on an ienormio elenchi : mistake of the point. In my tette: of the 27th ult., I citel the case of four Pretestent ministers in this town,each believing the Bible to be the Word of God, who by follow;ng the fundamental principle of Protestantism, private judg- ment which lays down that that interpretation of Scrip- ture is true which each man believes to be true and because he believes it to be true, were led into error of doctrine. From this I conclude that the Protestant principle el private judgment is a false principle. Oar Divine Ler d says :—" For them also I pray win shall believe in Me, that they may be one even as we also are one." It is there- fore the obvious teaching of Christ tlyst th . true Chureh s one. "One Lord, One Faith, one Baptism," (Eptics. 4-3). This unity of faith, i.e., all the members of the true Church united Ira the belief of the same doctrine 3 of Revelation and in the acknowledgement of the authority of the same pastors, is to be found only in the Catholic and Beeman Church. The principle of private judgment makes unity of faith theoretically impossib e. The rea- son is plain. The principle of private judgment asserts truth te be, "that which a man tr oar/en." As a result ef this false principle of private judgment we find, that in the Protestant Churches doctrine after doctrine has been fiest doubVd,next disputed,then denied,and finally abandoned ! The el octrine of the Sacraments has gone. And the doctrine of the atonement has gone. And Christs' Godhead and the Trinity and eternal punishment —and the inspiration of the Bible—all these cardinal doctrines wholly or in part have gone. Is it not a note- ZiOU3 fact that train the day of its birth Protestantism has been in a state of doctrinal flux ? At the outset, that religion proclaimed good works to be useless and faith alone to be necessary. Doss not the Protestantism of to-day declare faith to be useless and good works alone to be necessary? Again, Protestantism at the Outset rejected purgatory and accepted Hell. Does not the Protestantism of to-day accept Purgatory and reject Bolt ? For have we not b 'en assured by. well known Protestant divines in high places, that God is too good to burn the unrepentant sinner in endless flames. And this is what our friend C. B. calls a difference of " mould shape, size, and colour " ! The unity of the Catholic church is no mere" organic unity," but is undoubtedly a real unity of Fai.h, Doctrine, and Government. Now Is it true, that there is no proof of the Catholic doctrine of authority in Holy Scripture ? Most certainly not. The church has authority from God to t3ach regarding faith and morals ; and in her teaching she is preserved from error by the special guidance of the Holy Ghost. The prerogative of infallibility is clearly deduced from the attributes of the church mentioned in Holy Writ. The Church is One. Holy Catholic,and Apostolic, preach- ing the same creed everywhere, and at all times ; teach. inn holiness and truth ; he is of course, essen- tially unerring in her doctrine ; for teaming holiness and truth, what is one, holy, or unchangeable, must be infallibly true. That the church was infallible in the Apostolic' age, is denied by no Christian. We never question the truth of the Apostles declarations ; (see Gal. iv. 14 ; 1 These ii. 13), they were, in fact, the only authority in the Church for the first century. The New Testament was not completed till the close of the first century. There is VD just ground fo • denying to the Apostolic teachers of the nineteenth century in which we live, a prerogative clearly possessed by those of the first, especially as the divine Word nowhere intimates that this unerring , guidance was to die with the Apostles. On the contrary, as the Apostles transmitted to their successors their power to preach, to baptize, to ordain, to confirm, to offer sacrifice &amp;c., they must have handed down to them the no less essential gift of infallibility. God loves us as much as He loved the primitive Chr.e- tiaras ; Christ died for us as well as for them ; and we have as much need of unerring teachers as they had. It will not suffice to say : " We have an infallible ecrip• ture as a substitute for an infallible apostolate of the first century," for an infallible book is no use to me without an infallible interpreter, as the history of Pro- testantism too clearly demonstrates. But besides th .se presumptive arguments, we nave positive evidence from Scripture that the Church cannot err in her teachings. Our b'essed Lord in constituting St. Peter Prince of His Apostles, says to him : " Thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build My Church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it." (Matt xvi. 18.) Christ makes here a solemn prediction that no error shall ever in- vade His Church ; and if she f U into error, the gates of hell would have most certainly prevailed against her. The " Reformers "of the sixteenth contury affirm that the Church did fall into error. They cell into question the personal veracity of our Saviour. Our Lord metres a statement which they contradict. Who is to be believed Jesus or the " Reformers " ? Jesus sends forth His Apostles with plenipotentiary powers to preach the Gospel. "As the Father," Be says, " bath sent Me, I also send you." (John xx 21). "Going therefore, teach all nations, teaching them to observe all things whatso- ever I have commanded you." (Matt. xxviii, 19. 20). " Preach the Gospel to every creature." (Mark xvi 15). " Ye shall be witnesses unto Me in Jerusalem, and in all Judea, and Samaria, and even to the uttermost parts of the earth." (Acts 1 8). This commission eel lently applies not to the Irmo/ales only, but also to their successors,te the end of time, since it was utterly impo3sible for the Apostles personally to preach to the whole world. Not only does our Lord empower His Apostles to preach the Gospel, but He commands, and under the most severe penalties, those to whom they preach to listen and obey. Not- withstanding these plain declarations of Scripture, some persons think it an unwarrantable assumption for the Church to claim infallibility. But mark the conse- quences that follow from denying it. If your church is not infallible, it is liable to err for there is no medium between infallibility and liability to error. If your church and her ministers are fallible in their doctrinal teachings, as they admit, they may be preaching false- hood to you,instead of truth. If so, you are in doubt whether you are listening to truth or talsehood. If you are in doubt, you can have no faith, for faith excludes doubt,and in that state you displease God, for "without faith it is impossible to please God " (Eleb. xi. 8): In conclusion, I notice B.C. urges against me these two Contradictory objections; that on the one hand I, pet as much as a Protestant, exercise private judgment ; and, that,on the other hand, I am an intellectual bondsman because Ido not exercise private judgment. That these objections are mutually destructive does not seem to occur to this amateur disputant. I use my private judgment, no noubt,to find out which is the true church, jest as I use nay private judgment to find. out who as a good physician, or who is a good barrister; and the search is as easy in the former as in the latter cases. But having discovered the true Church by itg evident marks—say by her oneness of doctrine which is as con- spicuously present in the Catholic Chnrch as it is con- spicuously absent in the Protect ant Churches, I submit to her teaching ; just as having discovered a good doctor or a good lawyer I adopt his teaching in medicine or in law.—Yours Scc.,</p>
        <p>SURSUM CORDA. will with your kind prrnission, reply to our Ritualist friend Q.S. next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS REGAIN THE CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>The mee•ing of the St. [Plena and Evert ln water polo I teazle in the Liverpool and District Lerguo contest, to decide the championship posression, at Sc. Helens Cor- poration Baths, on Tuesday night, excited great interest, and there was a crowded attendance. The teams were —St. Helens : Aspinwall, goal ; Brett NI and Green, backs ; Ripley (captain).halt-back ; Preston, Killey,and Lawrenson, forwards; Everten : Morris, goal; Heves and Jones (captain), backs ; Newcombe, half-back ; Towers, Formby,and Trainer, forwards. After the open- ing exchanges, Ripley sent a strong shot which Morris cleared, and the visitors, putting in soars grand passing, enabled Formby to score UP first goal for Evertoa. Sub- sequently play was of an even character, each gqal in turn being endangered, but half-time arrived without any addition to the score, Evettoa leading by one goal to none. After some exciting izia.y in the second.periol Riney took a pass from Lawrenson and scored tne first goal for St. Helens amid great cheering, which was re- newed again and again,when Ripley,a few minutes later gave St. Helens the lead by scorng a second goal fro:n a pass by Lawreeson. There was no further scoring, and 'St. Helens wen an exciting contest by 2 goal to 1. Prior to the foregoing match, the third teams of the two clubs met in the third division of the League contest and Evert= third team won by 7 go Is to 4.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS y. MANCHES-FER OSBORNE. THE REPLAYED ENGLISH CHAMPIONSHIP TIE The question as to the data and venue of the replayed march in the third round of the English Water Polo cilaropionshipbetween St. Helens and Manctester Os- borne (champions) lass been settled. The teams played at Manchester last Fridly night with a drawn game of two goals each as the result. After the match the team met to fix the date and bath for the replayed tie. but could not agree, as Manchester refused to come to St. Helens, and sugaested that Warringthn should 13) the scene of the encount?r,but the St. Helens men naturally ohinted,as in the ordinary course toe match should be replayed at St. Helens, and the matter was laid before the Southern Countiss Association for decision. The committee have ci,oicied the plint,nd the match will be played at St. Helens this Friday night.</p>
        <p>_ --- WOMEN, AND ESPECIALLY MOT/MRS, are most competent to appreciate the purity, sweetness, and delicacy of Curcurre. SOAP, arid to discover new uses for it daily. Its remarkable emollient, cleansing, and purifying properties warrant its use in the form of washes or solutions for annoying irritations, inflamma- tions and chafing, for ulcerative weaknesses, or too /roe Perspiration, and for many or offensive sana.ive pm_ poses which will readily suggest themselves. many of the above, instances gentle applications of Cuncuna (ointment), supplemented in the severer forms of skint Irritations by the use of Crynctine, greatest of cchollien skin cures, in addition will prove of marked benefit. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DAHLIA SHOW AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturlay a Dahlia show promoted by Mr. George Sturgess. was held at the Hand and Banivr Hotel. This is the first show of the kind which has been held at tha H ind and Banner, but owing to the succesi attained, it is intended to mike it an annual affair. Tnere were 41 entries, and the flowers exhibited were of an excellent quality. Snne local gardeners officiated as judgel, and the following are their awards :—Six dahlias (double) : 1, C Simm ; 2 and 3, T Gregory. Three dahlias double) : 1 and 2, T Gregory ;3, W Kent. One dahlia (double) : 1. C Swam ;2, T. Lowe; Six dahlia (cactus), 1. J. Jones, J. Randall ; 3. T Lowe. Three dahlias (cactus) : 1, G Smith ;2 J Randall. Six dahlias (single) : 1, J Owen ;2, Mrs Sturgass ;3, C Sturgess, Six dahlias (pompones) : 1, W Lane; 2, J Randall ; 3, James Thompson. Three dahlias (pompones): UJRandall, 2, W Kent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>.. . ACCIDENT TO THE REV. T. R. MATTHAWS</head>
        <p>.. . ACCIDENT TO THE REV. T. R. MATTHAWS</p>
        <p>Sometime between Monday midnight and Tuesday morning an accident befel the Rev. T. R. Matthews, vicar of Earlestown, which, at his age, might have been attended with serious conse- quences. He retired to rest on Monday evening at half past eleven, apparently in his usual health,the only other inmate of the house being the servant, Miss Matthews at present being away. Mr. Matt- hews remembers notUing more until he found him- self lying on the first landing of the stairs shortly after three o'clock on Tuesday morning. He found he was injured and not desiring to disturb the eer- vant he managed to reach a sofa upon which he was found by the servant when she cnme down- stairs. Mr. Matthews injuries are not deemed to be of so serious a nature as to ciuse immediate alarm, but they are sufficiently severe to render him unfit for duty for sometime. How Mr. Matthews came to be in the position describ .d is not known, but he supposes he must have risen from his bed whilst asleep and fallen down the stairs. He thinks he must have lain on the landing unconscious about three hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ST.. n,LENS,</head>
        <p>ST.. n,LENS,</p>
        <p>ALLEGED KICKING AFFRAY AT ST. HELENS,</p>
        <p>John Hogartb, a collier, ago thirty-five, living at 96, Park-road, was admitted to the St. Helens Hospital on Friday night suffering from a broken leg. Hogarth worked at the Ashton's Green Colliery. It is alleged that after return;ng from work on Friday night he went on the spree, and, bocoming involvAd i's a row ia which clogs were freely used, had his ler broken by a kick. He Was attsndad by Dr. Jackson, ani nmovel to the hos- pital about half-past ten. SUICIDE LT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Ttrisday afternoon, Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy-coro- ner, held an inq ieit at S. H3lea3 Town Hall on the holy of William Vose, a labourer, of Fredrick-street, who committed suicide by cutting his throat with a pocket knife on Sunday morning. Evidence was given that Vase had been out of work or soms time, and Mrs. Vose siid she could only surmise that it was that fact that had led him to take his life. A verii?.t of "Suicide while of Lissomly-1 mind" was returned. A Dos EXCHANGE TRANSATION AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens County Court on Wednesday James Woreley, of S. Helens, claimed £7 10s, as the return of money tor a consideratioa which had failel in connec- tion with a dog exchange transaction with a Mr. E. A. Mills, of SloPpherdi Bush,Lon ion. Mr. H. L.Riley, who actel for the plaintiff, aopliel for costs in the matter. He stated that the defendant hal paid the full amount of claim into court, bat having done this less than five clear days before the hearing plaintiff was entitled to costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>DARING SHOPEREAKING AT Sr. HELENS.</head>
        <p>DARING SHOPEREAKING AT Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Taesday morning it was found that during the prece ling night the lock-up premises of Messrs. Voss and Ditchfield, grocers and provision dealers, Church-street, St. Heleos, had blen forcibly entered. All the tills and drawers had hero ranaacke3, and the sife in the office bore marks of having been tampered with,but it resisted the efforts made to open it. A small sum of money and a roll of tobacco were taken from one of the drawers, and with these the thief or thieves mad' off. It is sup posed that al entrance was gained through a trapdoo- eading to the cellar. SHOPDGOR THIFT AT ST. EIELENS.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Police Court °a Wednesday. before Councillor T. Burchall and Mr. T. Glover, William Smith of Birmingham,was charged with stealing a pair of shoes value 63 114., from the shopcloor of Mr. Stephen Milton in Church-street. Chief Constable Wood said that about half-past eight en Monday morning Mrs. Catherine Crowther, manageress for Mr. Haan, saw the boots safe at the shoploor,and about three o'clock in the afcer noon she missed them. About half-past nine on Monday night prisoner enteiel the Rising Sun public-house, aid offered the boots for site to the harm in, who refused.— The Bench fined him £1 ani costs, or fourteen days.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED STEALING AND RECEIVING AT Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Tuesday, James Galw;y, marine store dealer, of 18, Waterloo-street, together with four young men—viz., William Faramorth, John Leonard, and William Leonard, of 12, Cross—treet, and James Seohton, of 24, Cyril-stree', were charged with stealing three brass steps, value 30s the property of Messrs. Pil- kington Bros. Galway was also charged with receiving the same well knowing the goons to have ten stolen. All defendants pleaded not guilty.—They were let out on bail in their own recognisances of £5, except the boy Thompson. and the case adjourned t II Monday noxt at twelve o'clock.—Two other charges, in whi-h John Leonard and Jahn Thomas Thompson. of 10, Kirkland. s're.t, were charged with stealing two brass steps, value 24i from the same firm, and Galway was again charged with receiving, were also adjourned. FATAL ACCIDENT AT A ST. HELENS BOTTLE WORE. Thomas Langley, 45 years of age, a glassfounder, of 232. Boundary-road, St. Helens, died on Monday night from the effects of injuries r ,ceived at the glass-locttle works of Messrs. Nuttall and Cc. earli in the day. Langley's duties at the works, where be had be no em- ployed for over 26 years, iir.:luded attention to the gas producers. and necessitated his walking along a ganz- way about 13ft from the ground. On Monday he was found lying under the gangway by a man named Grundy, to whom he slated that he stumbl,d on the gangway and fell under the protecting rail on to the ground below. He was removed home and alt nled by Drs. Masson and Kinnear, who found that his spine was fractured. The unfort mate man died SOMA IV/Orli 104 t. An invest on the body of the decease-I was held at S. Efe'ens Town Hall on Tuesday afternoon before Mr. . A. Jones, deputy-coroner, and a verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TRAVELLING VAN TRANSACTION AT Sr. HELENS</head>
        <p>A TRAVELLING VAN TRANSACTION AT Sr. HELENS</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at the S. H-alens County Cur, before His Honour Judge Collier and a jury, William Lacaa, hawker, of St. Helens, sued Thomas H aytm. wheel- wright, of Eccleston, to recover £lO 10a. for breach of contract. Defendant counter-claimed for a simil.r amount. The diapute aross out of the building of a waggon, plaintiff alleging that defendant had been too dilatory, and that he ought to be refunded £8 les, pail on account, with damages. Defmdant's elle was that the work had been proceeded with in accordance with the contract, and that £lO 10a. renresentad the damages to which defendant was entitled.—Mr. E. W. Swift (Messrs. Swift and Garner) appeared for the plaintiff ; and Mr. H. L. Riley repre_ent the defendant.—His Honour, in curnininz-up, said that in reamer, of transac- tions of that kind when no writ'-en documenta were kept there was always sure to be a dispute.—The jury gave a verd et for the olaintiff for the return of a, and found that the defsmdant'a counter-claim was Oct proved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MIRLESTOWN SONS OF TEUPERA.NCE.</head>
        <p>MIRLESTOWN SONS OF TEUPERA.NCE.</p>
        <p>, The annual tea meeting and swill evening in Connec- tion with the Britains' Neel Division of the Sans of Tern- perance was held in the Co onerativa Hell on Tuesday evening. There were aroat 120 persons present 'o tea, land their wan's were atter:4l3d to by Mesdames Pearson. Warburton, Broster, Flughes, G. Clarke, Jolson, and Pickett. After tea, the usual meeting WAS held, over which Bro. T. Wheatley oresid-ri, ail he was anccm• paniel 01 the platform by Bro. G. Clarke, Holcroft, Chalmers (G.W.P.), Pe trson, Anderton, and Caish. The Chairman, in the course of a br ef alAross, extended a welcome on behalf of the Order to all who were pre3ent, and during his remarks on temperance matters,said that a day or two ago a prominent publican of Earlestown applied for a license ta enable him to sell spirits, and as they all knew, this was refused. 'He W3S glad to ere that the teetotallers who were said to be always poking their noses into other p-ople's business had a hand in putting down thi.3 application. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Pearson (F.S.) submitted a ranort pointing out the advantages accruing to members nf that society. He said they had at present 150 members. During an intrvsl Bro. Chalmera, G.W.P., presented Mr. D. H. RaNnson. oi t'e'laif of the Divltion, with an emblem,in recognition of his having passed through all the offices of the Division. _ _</p>
        <p>FIR also distributed ptivs to the following rwmbers of the Cadets lodges,for the ways named :—" For rearons aga'nst smsking," lst H. Judson, 2nd Jo=eph Porter ; " &amp;say on Father Matthew," 2nd C. F. Porter; " Essay g on Wine," 1t E. Judann,2nd F. Bolton. During the evenin the following programme vras suh- mitted :—Recitation, " The leak in the dyke." Miss E. Hughel ; meldv," In my teetotal ear." Mr. G. Clarke ; song, "The Better Land," Miss M. Barr ; Lancashire reading, Mr. D. Pickett.</p>
        <p>THE RECENT ARMY MANOEUVRES. The following was the field stato of the Southern Army, in the re-nt manceuvres, on Rey iew day :- 991 officers; 23,735 rank and file ; 4,553 hors." (military) ; 729 waggons (military) ; 109 guns, in - eluding 21 machine guns; 1,575 hired horses ; 678 hired vehicles. The arny inclu led four companies of the South Lancashire Regiment-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 16 198.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 16 198.</p>
        <p>ASSASSINATION OF THE EDIP 3ESS OF AUSTRIA.</p>
        <p>STABBED BY AN ANARCH IS T.</p>
        <p>The Empress of Austria was assassinated at Geneva on Saturday afternoon by an Italian Anarchist.</p>
        <p>Her Majesty had just left the Hotel Beau Rivage, and was walking to the steamboat landing on the Quai du Mont Blanc. She had reached the Brunswick Monument in the square when a man coming from the opposi`e direction suddenly rushed up and dealt the Empress a violent blow— to all appearance a blow with the fist. Her Majesty fell, but almost iLimeliatelyroce 0 her feet, and with the assistance of one of the ladies in waiting and some of the passers-by succeeded in reaching the steamboat pier. Her Majesty had hardly reached the deck of the steamer when she fainted. The captain at first hesitated to give the order for de- parture,but eventually the steamer put off. As the Empress did not regain consciousness the steamer was put about before she had left the harbour- and returned to the pier. Meanwhile the ladies in waiting, who had bees ecthavouring to bring the Empress back to cons3ious- nem, had observed a small stain of b:ood on her Majesty's und.r garments. When the pier was reached a stretcher was hastily improvised with oars and sail- cloth, and on this the Empress was carried into the Hotel Beau Rivage,</p>
        <p>Drs. Golay and Mayor and a priest, were immediately summoned, and a telegram was dispatched to the Emperor Francis Joseph with the sad intelligeace. All efforts to bring the Empress hack to life proved unavailing, and her M ijaty expired at three o'clock. The medical examinatian shiwed that the assassin must have us.d a small trianeular awl.</p>
        <p>ASSASSIN'S ATTEMPT TO ESCAPE. After striking the fatal blow the murderer made off. He ran along the Rue des Alpee with the intention evi- dent'y of entering the vast Square des Alpes, where be would ensly have bailed pursuit, but before reanh;ng the square he was seized by two cabmen, named Vict Villemain and Louis Chamartin, who had witnessed the crime from their stations on the cab rank on the qua-, and had at once set off in pursuit. They handed over their prisoner to a boatman named Albert Siaux and a gendarme named Kaiser, who conveyed him to the Pa- quis Police Station. The murderer dined no resistance. Oa the contrary, he appeared to be in high spirits, and sang snatches of popular songs. He remarked on the way : "My blow went home. She must be cl,ad "</p>
        <p>On being interrogated by the examining magist-ate in the presence of three memlyrs of the Cantonal Govern- ment, the Pirlo ic Prosecucor,the Secretary of the Depart- ment of Justice, and the Police Commissary, the man pretended he did not understand French. and refused to reply. On his po3lreti being searched his military pass- book was found, showing that he was enrolled in the army at Parma, and his name was Luigi Luccheni, and that he was born on April 21,1873, in Paris, though of Italian parents. TBE NEWS IN VIENNA.</p>
        <p>The scene in Vienna as the news cif the assassination spread was indescribable. The crowds which rushed from the suburbs could hardly bAieve the report until special editions of the papers appeared. Thy were literally fought for, and the wildest consternation and bewildsrment ens led.</p>
        <p>The following further particulars are given of the re- ception of the news by the Emperor at Shonbrunn Count Past had to break the news to the Emper, or, but the Premier and Count Go!uchowski also hurried to the Palace. One convulsive shudder, as when a man is stricken by lightning, shattered down the noble, upright figure of the poor Sovereign, en hearing the word "dead." He grasped his head, and sank into his chair. Then there was a long and terrible silence. A stream of tears broke fourth from his eyes, and his Majesty groaning said, " It appears that no bitterness of this earth can be spared to me." The Ministers and dignitaries around him were deeply affected, as they remembered at this moment his brother, the Emperor Maximilian, who was shot in Mexico, the Crown Prince Rudolph, the Duchess d'Alencon. the Empress Elisabeth's sister, who was burnt in Paris. After the tragedy of the Crown Prince Rudolph the Speaker of the Lower House came to condole with the Emperor, who said, " Tell the people it was only that noble-minde I lady, the Empress, who has sustained me." Now the last pillar is broken, now the great sufferer, the Emperor, stands alone. Luccheni will be tried at Geoeva in the ordinary man- ner unless the Austrian Government asks for lois extra- dition. Should this be done. the Swiss Government will probably accede to the reque?t in view of the excep- tional circumstances surrounding the crime he has corn• mitted, but not in any way as a precedent. It is b lieved, however, toat no such request will be made by Austria, and that the law will be a'lowed to take its course in Geneva. Capital punishment does not exist in Switzerland.</p>
        <p>THE REMOVAL OF THE REMAINS TO VIENNA. The special trian in which the remains of the late Empress were conveyed to Vienna arrival at Geneva at an early hour on *Marley morning. Apart from a special car for the coffin, it consisted of one sleeping car, two saloon cars for the high officials who acnm- panird the body, and another ordinary carriage. The interior of the funeral car is entirely draped in black and i 3 arranged with extreme simplicity. It is lighted by six largo windows with blackout-tains. In the centre is a low platform to which the coffin was fastened by broad straps, and this is divi lei off from the space round by an iiO3 railing. The four Court die,nitams sent by the Emperor to take charge of the coffin were met at the railway station by Gmeral flerzeyiczy,Grand Marshal of the Austrian Court, Count Kuefatein, Austro- Hungarian Minister to Switzerland, and others.</p>
        <p>_ The representatives of the Austrian Court were con- dulted on noir arrival to the death chamber, where the. coffin was still so far open that the fac3 of the dead was visibl.. There wa.3 a touching scene when the ladies of the CRirt s sw the eoffined remaics of their late mistress, and many tears were shed. TEIE LYING IN STATE.</p>
        <p>According to the arrangenty‘nti,-of Which public notice was issued on TuPsday,the coffin containing,' the remains of the Empr 's3 El zabeth, will be conveyed iu s )lemn procession at ten o'clock on Thursday evening from the Western Railway Station 1.9 the Flofburg. On Friday the coffin will be expcs.,cl from 8 a.m. till 5 p.m., and an Saturday from 8 a.m, till noon. At four o'clock on</p>
        <p>t+aturday afternoon the coffin will b; earned in—s'u-le-m—r; procession tl the Capuchin Church, whare, in the pre, sence of the E-apror and 013 f ;reign Sovereigns who attend, the blessing of the remains will take place. The coffin will then be lowered into the vault, the Emp.ror following, and after a prayer of henedTetfon while the body is icterred the key if till coffin will be handed over to the guardian of the Canwhin Church. COURT MOURNING IN ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>The London "Gazatte" on Tues lay contained an order for the Court to go into mourning for the Empress of Au.tfia for a fortnight from the 14th inst. The Court will change mourning on the 28th instant, and go out of mourning on October 12.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>DUKE OF ORLEANS AND LUCCHENL</head>
        <p>THE DUKE OF ORLEANS AND LUCCHENL</p>
        <p>Luccheli's confession that his first idea was to kill the Duks of Orleans has created moms sensation among the Finch Ito valist community in London. The Duk° smt this year from Eagfind a rneisago to his support7s in France s'rmgly condemning revolutionary principles, and it is believed this was the ressou of LuccheaVs de- sire for reverips. During the Duke's nrevious sojourns at York House, Twickenham,ond Wood Norton he received some threatening letters from unknown periona, bit took little notice of them. Telegrams of condolence have been received by the Duchess, who was a near relative of the late Empress of AuAria.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>EMPRESS IN THE HUNTING FIELD.</head>
        <p>THE EMPRESS IN THE HUNTING FIELD.</p>
        <p>HER VISITS TO CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Nowhere in England,Royal circlrs, of course,excepted, still the death of the ill-fated Enepress be received with more genuine sorrow than in Cheshire. Her Maj .sty, it will be remembered, occupied Comberm-re Abbey, the historic home of the Viscounts Combermere, daring the hunting se isnns of 1881-2. She was accompanied on each occasion by Count Kinsky and Count Lichtenstein and other members of her Austrian suite. The Empress was an export horaewoman,and always well mnunted— a thoroughbred bay horse wns her favourite mount—she was frequently in at the death of the fax. She was piloted during the first part of the season of 1881 by the Captain Bay Middleton, and afterwards by Major Balkeley, now Colonel Bulkeloy. The Empress was ex- tremely sensitive when visitors to the hunting field anxious to see her were apt to become too inquisitive. Her Majesty hunted more frequently with the South and North Cheshire packs, the former then as now huntod by Mr. Corbet, and the latter by the late Captain Park Yates ; and occasionally she was seen out with Sir Watt. kin Wynn and the North Stafford Hounds. Her Majesty left behind hr several interesting mementoes. Arningst the recipients of these were the whips of the South Cheshire hounds, each of whom received a gold pin sit with precious stones. Combermera Abbey, where her Majesty s'ayed, is situated in one of the prettie,t parts of Cheshire, and facing it is a magnificent lake. Memo• flats of the late Empress's ara to be found in her portrait hanging on the walls, in rare pieces of china with which her room overlooking the like was stored, and in traces of the gymnastic apparatus fitted up for her in her dress- ing-room.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDRAVOTIR AT LATCHFORD. On Monday evening a most enjoyable aocial in connection with the Latchford Baptist Christian I Endeavour Society was held in the schoolroom which had been prettily arranged by the Soci ti Committee, who had charge of all the arrangements and whoQe effort resu!ted in a very happy evening being epent. Fruit and refreshments were handed r3und at intervals. Songs and recitations were rendered by members and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>A. WARRINGTON SOLDIER IN THE SOUDA.N.</head>
        <p>A. WARRINGTON SOLDIER IN THE SOUDA.N.</p>
        <p>INTF,RESruta EXPERIENCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Mo.lee, uf Warring oa, hta receivA the fo:lowing iaterestmg letter from his brotier, Corporal Fred. Monks, now ser7ing in tie 2111 Battalion Rifle Brigade witi the Soudan fiold fo:co. Toe later was re- ceive' by Mr. Monks las: week : In Camp, Atbara, August 9, 1898. Dear take much plea mre in writing you a fay; lines to let you know how I am faring in this ungldly country. . . . You must excuse paper and the way it is written, In the first place, the paper for this one letter only cost me one piast:e (211.) an" I am</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>V7EtITING IT ON MY WATER IDOTT-LR;</head>
        <p>V7EtITING IT ON MY WATER IDOTT-LR;</p>
        <p>having no tve.t.r writing desk. Now ti my goings on since leaving Malta, which I will give you as near as I possib'y can. Laving M dta on July 12 in the transport Nabia, of the P. and 0. Line, we journeyed for three days to Alexandria, arr,ving there on Friday, July 15. We stopped on b iard at Alexandria until the following day. L7aviug Alexandria at 6 a.m. oa July 16 we travelled by train to Cairo, arriving there abut 1.30 pm. At this pace we had ten days to stay, so that I can eII you that I saw a great deal of Cairo in those ten days—in fact, more than a great many people d ) who stay there twelve month;. It is a very gay plae Will, th Jugh I should say one of the mist wicked iu the aanrld. People did say that Paris was a ine:e nothing to thia, and I can fully believe it. The gay places do not open until about 9 p.m.—l mean tseatri s and music halls of every description. Drink; are dear at Cairo. In one plate two of us wont in for a drink (a glass of beer), and they charged Lei five piastres, or is. 0.1. Rather thick, Will, 6.d. a glass, isn't it. On the 28th July we left Cairo (amidst great cheering from the Lancashire Fnsilliers by train at 9 a m., and after two days' travelling arrived at Sheilah, where we detrained, and had Lreaktaat before proceeding further. After breakfast we embarked on the Nile river boats for Wady Halts, arriving at the latter place at 11 p.m. on August 1 (Bank Holiday). We stopped on the boat until the following morning, when we disembarked and marched Mt) a native barrack; for breakfast and din- ner. This barracks contained as prisoners Mahmound and his relatives, who were taken prisoners in the last Atbara battle. At tais place we c ,uld see hundreds of</p>
        <p>DERVISH PRISONERS WOEKINGI IN CHAINS, both on hands and feet, and they wasn't dog chains either. After dinner we again entrained for Atbsra, at which place we arrived on the 3rd August at 2 a.m. We are encamped on the site of the last ba:tle, which contains the graves with a plain wood cross to mark the place where our men fell. A few miles from here can be seen skektans, spears Sze., where a portion of the fight toAr place. All tile tr34ps for the British Brigade are concentrating h re, when th 3 advance will be nude. This will take plac about the 18th of this month. We go up the river by gunbut for sixty miles, and disembarking. march the remainder of the way (about 120 miles). After this affair is ever, there is a great rumour of sending all British troops home again, to recruit the health and strength of the battalion, and a's3 to let the Landon people see who took part in these campaigns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>NOW ABOUT THREE DERVISHES..</head>
        <p>NOW ABOUT THREE DERVISHES..</p>
        <p>For my own part I don't fancy it will take us long to clear the whole of them out, but our geaeral (Gatacro thinks differently. We had a parale yesterday, and he formed us up and gave us a lecture, and these are his words as near as I can tell you, He said :—" Th 3 Der- vish is uAta!ly a very plucky fellow ; he can run time times as fast as a British sAdier, has a spear eighteen feet long with forks on, and can go for three or four days in this excessive heat without a drink of water." These were his words, and when I was at Wady Haifa I saw an E4yptian soldier . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>– HIT ONE OF THEM ACROSS THE FACE</head>
        <p>- HIT ONE OF THEM ACROSS THE FACE</p>
        <p>as hard as he could. The only thing he did was to hobble away and laugh as hard as he emit Had that been an Englishman he would have kill-id him. If ym want to kill a. nigger the only place you can do it with a stick is ia the stomaM. This will drop him immediately with• out a quiver. The whole affair,including the capture of Khartoum, is reckone 1 on being finished by the Lt of October. I hope I shall manage to pull tarough all right. There is art ho3pital here, and I can tell you there are some nice sights come in sometimes. There is a special tatters of Artillery going up with a shell called Lyddite. This shell will carry some 4,000 yards, and whatever it hits will cause destructim, it also has a rebound of 400 yards, kiting everything in its back ward course, and what it c10.,s not kill it smothers within 100 yards round it. I believe, from what I havc heard from mea of the battery who came out with us from Malta that this is a new invention, made specially In this Egyptian affair. Our own bullets are also very deadly, having a hole half way do ,vn the centre, which causes a very big wound whenever it eoraes out. Now, Will, I don't think the Dervishes will stick much of these deadly instruments of war, and I can assure you I don't want them to, for I am fed up with active service. Another thing, they are giving themselves up in camp here every day,and it is nothing irtsli to see some poor devil sailing, or rather swimming, on a plank as hard , as he can go down the Nile, or to see a ratt full of them deserting down the Nile under cover of the darkness, so as to give themselves up to the British. Just where our camp is thera is a village of blacks, who run about with nothing at all on ; is does not matter what sex they are. I am either tired or haven't more to say, Will,Eo must be drawing to a close for fear the alarm sounds, when I should not be able to finish it. Hoping you will remem- ber me to all at home, and drop me a few lines, I will conclude, and remain your affectionate brother. (Signed), FRED. T .e writer of the alms letter is Corporal Frederick Monks, second sou of the late Mr. Richard Monks, of Kerfoot-streA, Warringtoa,formerly an engine-driver on the London and North-Western Railway. Before enlist- ing Monks was a clerk at the Longford Wire and Iron Company's works.—" Liverpool Daily Post."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>INTERESTING MARRIAGE AT GREAT</head>
        <p>INTERESTING MARRIAGE AT GREAT</p>
        <p>SANKEY.</p>
        <p>JEANS-WORTHINGTON.</p>
        <p>Considerable local -interest—especially in the Great Sankey and Panketh distric B—was mani- fested in the marriage of Mr. William Dampier Jeans, inn,, son of Mr. William Dampier Jeans, of Great Sankey and Mies Lillie Worthington, younger daughter of Mr. Peter Worthington, of Penketh. The interesting event took place in St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey on We inesday afternoon and the nuptials were witnessed by a large congregation. The surplice I choir was in attendance and toe ser- vice, which was fully choral, commenced with the singing of "The voice that breathed o'er Eden," In the unavoidable absence of the Vicar, the officia- ting clergyman was the Rev. J. R. Jones (curate). The bride looked exceedingly handsome in a dress of white brccide eilk trimmed with chiffon. She also wore a wreath of orange blossoms suspended from which was a tulle veil. In her hand she carried a beautiful shower bouquet composed chiefly of lilies of the valley and wnite roses. She was given away by her father. There were three bridesmaids in attendance, namely Miss Emily Jeans and Miss Annie Jeans (elvers of the bridegroom) and Miss Dorothy Lewis (niece of the bride). They wore white ailk dresses trimmed with yellow silk and hats to match. Their bouquets were made up of yellow coloured flowers. Tao onerous duties of best man were satisfactorily discharged by Mr. J Stan- ley Francomh. The organist was Miss Mary Stand- ish, and as the party left the churcb she played Mendelseohn's well-known "Wedding March." A reception was afterwards held a. the house of the bride's father and was attended by a number of in- vited guests. Sts'ortly after four o'clock the newly- married couple departed for London en route for Paris. The bride's travelling dress was of navy- blue cloth. A large number of choice and valuable wedding presents was received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NEW COND1701:OR TO THE LIVERPOOL MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE NEW COND1701:OR TO THE LIVERPOOL MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>--- Mr. Frank H. Crossley, whose appointment as con- ductor to the Liverpool Musical S3ciety, we announced last week, formally assumed the baton of office last Thurs- day evening, when the Society's first meeting of the season was held. Mr. Emcee° Carey, President of the Society occupied the chair. Tne President paid a warmly eulogistic tribute to Mr. Crois'ey's artistic and scholastic attainments, stating that it gave him unfeigned pleasure to kno v the con• ductorship was in such capable hands. He considered it an augury of even greater artistic success in the future; and the committee had certainly appointed the right man to the office. Mr- Crossiev, on rising to respond, met with a most co!dial reeeption. In the course of a brief speech, he said : The kind remarks of your esteemed President, and the spontaneous warmth of your welcome are quite as overwhelming as they are unexpected and undesorved. I certainly— did not expect such a cordial greetiny, on this, my first appearance among you, and I thank you very heartily for it. I consider it a very great honour to have been selected for the post of conductor of this society, and desire to say tat no one can hope more than I for a very successful season. The committee have ma le a very gratifying selection of works for our rendition, and with the loyal co-operation lam sure I shall receive from you, I feel certain we shall make this season one of the most brilliantly successful in the society's history. The active work of the of:Jit was then commenced- and judging from tho manner in which the chorus at, tack d Mendeissohn's .• Elijah," the committee of tile Liverpool Musical Society will have no reason to be dis- appointed with the performances during the coming winter, under Mr. Cressiey's up€rvision. We learn that the Liverpool Society will give " Elij ih " on the 15th November, with (probably) Charles Bantley, the creator of the part, in the title role ; on the 27th December they will give the "Messiah," and other important works during the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURA.L DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURA.L DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A me.ting of tlis CrnucL was held at the Be prs.l,- street Cniinb •rs on Wednesday afcernoin wall Mr. Joshua Bill (in the chair). There were also prase Messrs. J. W. Kno wlei, T. Hilton, W Hough on, A. Brookes, J. Johnson with Mr. J. C. Butt m (clerk) Dr B.phton (ni3dical offiesr),Mr. F. K n.foot (inspector),and Mr. D. S. Stone for Mr. W. Owen (the surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>FL NANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FL NANCIAL.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that thPre Was a balance in favour of the general account of £569 10: 10d and £1029 16: Oi in favour of the Bunonwood Sewerage L)aa Account. The following, are the estimated extra expo aditure items for the half year ending March, 1399 : Bartonwo sewerage ".2.150 : Penketh, lighting,s.,vezage, and water, £lOO ; Poutsuo, lighting and water £4O ; Rixton, cein- tery, £6O; Winwick sewerage ad water £lO. Mr. BROOKES : Is not £6O a rather large precept for tie Rixton Cemetery ? Would not £4O meet it ? . .</p>
        <p>The CLERK : We like to be on the sife side as the balance always passes to the next account. We hive revisfcl the figures as cirefullv as we can.</p>
        <p>THE ND OF A MORTUARY AT GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>The Cceas said he had received a letter from the Local Government Board enclosing, a letter to them from the Clerk of the Greet Sankey Parish Council wit a a re- port by the same. The Clerk of the Council wrote on behalf of his authority calling attention to the necessity there was for a mortuary in the district and asking whether it would be legal for the Council to erect a mortuary and if not whetner they would be allowed to set apart a 110U90 for that purpose. They replied to the letter to thl effect that the power asked for was yes ad in the Rural District Council.</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR (Mr. Kerfoot) said that the statements recoived bore out Mr. Brighouse's opinion. They waive I to know whether if a body was found an the canal they were bound to take it to a private mertu try. Mr. KNOWLES : i it your idea to throw the responsi- bility on toe C anal Company. Mr. EfatzoN : 06, no. Are we bound to be chsrged with the full expense of this body being found ? Mr. KNOWLES Only the burial. The people who come worst off in these cases are the policemen ; they are generally something out of posket. I would suggest this. I think all bodies should be taken to the Chapel souse They would provide the temoorary room. If Mr. Unsworth chooses to make the charge will the Coun- cil pay ? I mean the District Council. It does not say we must provide the mortuary, but we may. Mr. fleuron : I may say the Parish Council were unanimous we should pay him £3 per annum. The CHAIRMAN : Oh, that is oust of all reason. Mr.KNowross : I never knew an innkeeper to refuse to take a body in, except in one instance. The CHAIRMAN : I would certainly let him make a small charge per body. Mr. EleuroN : I may say the last body was kept a week. The police say they wipe their hauls of it as soon as the inquest is over. Mr. ggsrowLEs then proposed that they should pax, ss. per body. Mr. FIALTON seconded and the resolution was carried. The CLERK : Is there a claim now ? Mr HALTON Yes, for two bodies. . •. The Ccmax : Let that claim be dealt with by your Inspec.or. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPIITON r Torted that during the month the ex- cess of births over death; was 12. The death rate from WI causes was equal to 21.7 per I,COO against 10 the avarag sof the last three months, and a.gainst 81 the corresponding month of at year. The death ra'e frern zym- tic diseases (7 principal), was equ ;1 to 4 per 1,000 against nil the average rate of the last three months and against 2.6 the c srresoonding. mcnth of last year. Tara six deaths in the zyml'ic class were due to diarrhce and all occurred in children under one rear of age. Two inquests were held, drowning Wino, the 03.L140 of death. During the mouth one case of infect'ous disease his bete reported under the Compulsory Notification Act. The report was unanimously adopted. TEM INSPHCTOII.B REPOFIT.--LONGFORD BROOK._</p>
        <p>Thl INSPECTOR in his report said he had visited Long- ford brook on several occasions. At tinass the water TM' yellow, or black, pnerally the colour of soap suds ; the stench on the whole not being so bad.</p>
        <p>MR. DIGGLR INTERVIEWS THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At this juncture Mr. Diggle presented himself, and his request for an audience was granted. After being intro- due c.l to the memb trs of the Council in a general sort of way, Mr. Diggle said that having given some attention to sanitary matters in Warrington, he fouled that the arrangements were wrong from the beginning to the end, so far as this town was concerned. He found that the people of Warrinron wore the greatnst polluters of Sankey Brook, Mr. Diggle then went on to ex.:lain his reasons. Be had a sample of pure water with him, and he would like to lecture on the matter if they would provide the means for doing so. Dr.SEPorrox : Where did you get the pure water ? Mr. Diggle: From snow. Dr. SEPHTON : Where did you celled the snow P M. DIOGLE said there was a large snow storm one Sunday, that '-as how he got it. Be had water from Buraley and Manchester. Dr. SEPHTON Does the p ire 'water contain any lime ? Mr. DIGOLE No. Dr. SEPHTON : Do you consider that a little lime in water is injurious ? Mr. DIGGILE : Yes. Mr. Diggie then explained that he proposed to deliver four lectures ; the Ist," The . . sanotatten of Warrington,and how largely Warrington contributes to the pollution of Sankey Brook; 2nd, 'rho supply of gas and a comparison between Warrington and Widnes 3rd, "Fire extinguishing;" showing plans of an improvement on the present system; 4th," Water, showing tests as to its suitability for the purposes in. tended." Mr. Diggle did not fix any date for his lectures, but said he was prepared at any time. No 0 oinion was expressed with regard to Mr. Diggle's suggestions, and the Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>RUIIIOURED UNIONIST SPLIT IN NEWTON DIVISION.</head>
        <p>THE RUIIIOURED UNIONIST SPLIT IN NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>The " ilanchester Evening News" says "It is strongly denied in Ministerial circles that Mr. T. W. Legh (the member for the Newton division) will suzcoed Mr. Cur- zon as Under-Secretary for Foteign Affairs. According to thoce who profess to know. Mr. Leigh, though admit- tedly regarded as an able m ,mber of the party, with the advantage of diplomatic experience,is not in the running and failing Mr. Wyndham, the new Under-SJcretary will be chosen from inside the ranks of the Government. however, the idea of appointing him had been enter- tained, it might have been knocked on the heal by the Colonist split which, it is understood, has occurred in the Nswron division. and the declaration that he and certain Conservatives are in f avour of disestablishment and undelominational schools, large as Mr. Legh's majority was in 1895, the party managers here are con- siderably concerned at the portentous incident reported from Lancashire." . . .</p>
        <p>Oor correspondent states that nothing is known officially of any Unionist split, but a sec ion of the Con. servative party Nrl by Mr. John Robinson of Hay dock. and a member of the Protestant League, has declared that in the event of another election here, they will run an inderondent candidate,who must first be a protestant and a Conservative after wards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>VOLUNTEER. INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER. INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>6ni V.B. (KING'S) LIVERPOOL REGIMENT.—"P" COMPANY, NEWTON DETACHMENT. The thirty-seventh annual prize meeting took place at Altcar on Saturday, the local range having been closed since the introduction of the use of the Lee-Metford rifts. The weather was fine, but a strong wind blew across the range from the right. The prizes were gained , as follows :</p>
        <p>CHALLEGE CuP (presented by Sir C. E. Hamilton, Bart.—Winner, Sergeant R. Wright, cup and one guinea. MARKSMEN'S COMPETITION.-1 Sergeant J Rimmer, 2 Sergeant R Wright, 3 Lance-Sergeant James Pullan, 4 Private T Boardman.</p>
        <p>HANDICAP PRIZES.-1 Private T Yapp, 2 Private T Boardman, 3 Lance-Sergeant J Pullan, 4 Sergeant J Rinuner 5 Private A Walker, 6 Sergeant W Rosa, 7 Ser- geant G McCormick. 8 Private J H Pallan.9 Sergeant R Wright, 10 Private G Fitz-Gerald, 11 Private W Devereux, 12 Lance-Corporal W Wallace, 13 Sergeant. Instructor Hancock, 14 Private R Williams, 15 Corporal N d)bs, 16 Lance-Corporal R H Jones, 17 Lance- Seroant W Kent, 18 Private T Wallace, 19 Private .1 Kenyon. 20 Private J Davidson, 21 Private T Plant, 22 Corp3ral R Hill, 23 Private E J Osborne, 24 Private T Carey,. 25 Lance-Corporal w Price,26 Private A. Spencer. Rxcaurrs PRIZES.-1 Private T Amlanse, 2 Private E Kirkman, 3 Privata H Burgess, 4 Private W Naylor, 5 Private J Red-ling, 6 Private W Newport, 7 Private R Naylor, 8 Private W Newns, 9 Private E Perry, 10 Private J H Banks, 11 Private E Jones. Dam. PRIZES.-1 Sergeant Rimmor, 2 Sergeant R Wright, 3 Corporal H Nobbs, 4 Sergeant G McCormick. 5 Lance-C,,rparal Wallace, 6 Col3ur-Sergeant Appleton. 7 Private T Wallace, 8 Lance-Sergeant F Appleton, 9 Privata J Anderson, 10 Private T. Yapp. SQUAD COMPETITVN (Cup presented lov Captain N McCorquodale).-1. No 7 squad, Sm•geant W. Rose; 2 No. 4 squad, Sergeant J Rimmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>ALLEGED NIGHT POACHING AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED NIGHT POACHING AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helen P gace Court on Monday, before Alder- man Sinclair, Albert Harper, collier, of Jury-street,West Leigh, was charged with night poaching that morning, at M 099 Sink on land belonging to Sir David Gamble. Bart., C.B. Mr. E. W. Swift (Messrs. Swift and Garner) prosecute, and s lid that at one o'clock that morning prisoner and four or five other men were found by Game- keeper D. Curran and his assistant, Middlehurst, in Nixon Gorse Wood, at Moss Bank. The men ran away, leaving five bags, two nets each sixty yards long, and nineteen rabbits, dead and alive, Mr. Swift said that order that inquiries might be made he had to apply tor a remand until to-day.—The remand was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON G qES AND</p>
        <p>RUG BY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington's Dm-,y Day tias been and gone, and with it a couple of L Incas lire L iague p&amp;nts. Tile fitture with Oldham is rightly considered the most important of the season, and if you want to see a rea ly good game under Northern Union Rugby roles you cannoCclo better than visit the Wilderspool ground oa the day that the crack organisation from Spindledom is dust) appear. Whichever side wins, both may be relied upon t) exert themselves to the utmost—Oldham b. CIUS3 they con- eider the W; rep ille!s one of the herds; team; to b at on their own ground, and Warrington Weems° they al- ways have had a keen desire to lower th 3 colours of the crack Oldham brigade. No more earl) -isin,g thing could happen in Ru4by football than that ()Alain should, at any part of the season, have an iadiffJrent Z•ra,n to rep- resent them. The continu,d succ es of the Oldham fifteen, last season's champions, is a s:riking example of the reward to be gained by enterprising management, and strict attention to training etc. A big price is paid for the men, bat the committeo are amply rowarthd for thoir outlay by results.</p>
        <p>The evening papers of tea days ago rerorte I the cap- ture and successful landing at Oldham of T. Davies, of Treherbert,considered to be one of the fines centre three- quarters in the Principality. Davies was signed on quite early in the year,but he was not en evidence when wanted for the first time. However he is securely fixed now,and must consider himself a professional henceforth, and at the same time quite biond the pale of the football in which he previously shone so refulontly. I must warm 'F congratulate the Oldham executive on having secured such an unquestionibly good man. His first appearance last Saturday conclusively prsved his worth, and what sort of a player he will develop into when he becomes thoroughly conversant with tha Oldham style and play, is difficult to imagine. I oaly wish he was included in the Warrington ranks. He is far more badly needed here than at Oldham.</p>
        <p>----- A sample of the best September weather favoured the encounter with its presence, and a crowd oE something like 8,000 psrsons surrounded the arena. With the ex- ception ot Jack Hurst, who occupied the position of snectator, the visitors bad their full strength up. while Warrington had tr., find subs itutes fer tae full-back and a couple of forwards. Thus, from the commence- ment the homesters were handicapped,whon they should have had instead, a start of their opponents. Of course they were beaten, though I, for one, won't deny that I had ventured to hope for a better result,. Warrington have occasionally surprised Oldham, last year but one for instance,and will probably do so again, but they had to knuckle under on Saturday. If ever a team was out- classed that team was Warrington, and especiallY in the ooncluding stages of the game. I have seen tam more heavily beaten,but never,to my recollection. have I seen their fees shown up so plainly. There was absolutoly no comparrison between the style of play adopted by the two teams. Oldham were speedy in all their move- moots, and had a definite end in view every time they secured the ball, while Warringtm ware slovr,inaccurate in cernbination,and haphazard in tactics.</p>
        <p>I don't say they failed to make a good show, or even to do therwelves jastice. Nothing cou:d have lo.en finer than the determined way ia which they buatlPd their foes about up to half-time. Oldham were,may be, content to play a waiting game against the wind and sun, but for all that they were fortunate to keep their record quite clean. For a gool portion of the game War- ringtora looked like making a close fight of it by slaPer determinati n, though it was hard to irna zinc that the finished play of the visitors would not finally be re- warded. The escapes from disaster were so narrow, that even who attacking Warrington were endangered more than once. When the interval arrived th.re was no score, and the game was " anybody's," so to speak.</p>
        <p>On changing ends however, Oldham soon gave indica. lions of what their intentions had been and were. With 1 the sun and breeze behind them they cinickly drove the Warrington men, alr2aly somewhat pump id with the exertions of the first half, back to their own line.Baveral brilliant movements s'emed certain to end in a score, when perhaps there was a "knock on" or Bones other informality. Martin was particularly unfortunate in this respect, and should really have nal two or three tries to his credit. At last a series of lightning exchanges between Lawton, Lees and T. Davies enabled the Welsh- man to round the home backs and register a fine try. Though the goal-kick was a failure, it was plain to be seen that Warrington nearly gave up all hope. They still defended excellently, but they were no match for the beautiful passing runs by the Oldham men, and it was not long before Ike Taylor raced in at du. corner. Martin was determined not to be out-done and at last got through after a particularly fine effort. Na goals re- I suited, and the game concluded with the b)ard reading Visitors 3 tries, Warrington nil.</p>
        <p>Some idea may be gained of the superiorit in attack of the winners by the fact that they crossed the War- rington line eight times during the afternoon, but only three trie3 were properly complete'. At least three of the other attempts were as near to succecs as possible but there was just that slip " between the cup and the lip "t 3 nollify them. It is im oossible to pick out any man for special praise on the Oldhim side, each played so well. There vas not a weak spot apparent anywhere. In my opinion Oldham were never represented by fif- teen more accomplished players, and ought to stand the best of chances for the present season's championship. On the home side the best work was done at half-back, where the two Bates performed prodigies of skill. This I was about the only department ha which Warrington were a match for their fors. In the threequarter-line Oldham bad praAically their own way. I,herwood and Moores strove gallantly and well to put a different com- plexion on the game, but the other wing, Hallam and Holcroft was far to feeble and slow. I tail to see how Warrington are to secure many successes unless a couple of really good men are introduced into the three-quarter line. The forwards pretty nearly held their own, Fair- hurst shining conspicuously in the open. it full-back Jones made a fair substitute for Boscow, though natur- ally troubled with nervousness.</p>
        <p>Now that both have been tried in the three-quarter line, some opinion can be formed of the capabilities of Hallam and Holcroft. Both work hard enouth, but they are not possessed of the speed, resource, and judgment nezessary for three-quarter play. 213 full back I think either would be a sacerss. They are both strong kickera and seem suited for the post of custodian. Ho!croft in particular, 330M9 just the man for full back Would it not be well to give them a tair trial in this position ? I see Flallam is chosen as fall-back for the " A team in to-day's match and it is to be hoped that Holcroft has a similar trial.</p>
        <p>The effect 3 of competition on the players' jerseys seems to be demoralising. About half-a-dozen failed to last the game out on Saturday, Hallam requiring three be- fore " time." There was no particular smartness in providing the denuded players with a change either, which is a matter worthy of closer attention.</p>
        <p>By the way, I must give the committee credit for having so greatly improved the press accommodation on the field. During the week following the Swinton match radical alterations were made. A staircase was built up at the back of the stand, a door inserted, and the trout of the press-box hermetically sealed, while the space inside was considerably enlarged. Now, it is possible for the reporters to have a good view of the game and carry on their work under comfortable condi- tions which was far hornbeing the case the previous week.</p>
        <p>The team chosen to oppose St. Helens differs only slightly from that doing duty previously. Duckworth introduced into the threequarter line vice Hallam, Boscow should be fit to turn out again, and I3arratt is now eligible to return to the forward ranks. Another question : Why is Cross left out in the cold ? He is more worthy ot a trial at centre than Duck worth,whose abilities are more in the ha'f-back line.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the following will represent Runcorn against Salford, at Runcorn :—Back, Samuel Houghton. (captain) ; three-quarters, T. Warden, T. Wilkinson, P Woods, A. N. Other ; halves, J. Jolley, .7. Niekson ; for wards, A. Taylor, H. Farmer, S. Walker, F. Gayter, W. Lightfoot, W. Betley, 0. Hughes. F. Jameson ; reserves. J. Tomlinson and W. Wilson.</p>
        <p>Representing the Runcorn second string at Salford, will ba :—back,L Haliwoad, threequarters ; R Tates, R Anderson, G Moston, Sefton ; halve;, George Wii G Gayter; forwards, F DArlington,S Langley,A Moulton. It Reece, W 11ece, W Barker, W Wilson, W Newt3n ; reserve, M Doran and A N other.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Rec's will oppose Warrington " A "at Run- corn, with!—A. Atherton, back ; T. Shallcrost, W. Sut- ton, T. Wright, A. G ' ayter three-quart,rB ; It. Dutton, J. Kenyon, halves; and J.Tatlock, J. Bronnan,J. Dixon, T. Lightfoot, C. Simpson, T. Jones, W. Cobb, G. Moly- neux,forwards, with J. Whitlow,P. Walker, P. Faulkner. and R. Deakin as reserves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-09-16_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The Warrington eleven are finishing up the season in a creditable manner,and the decisive victory they achieved over Sefton is all the more commendable from the fact that the Liverpool team have been playing in capital form lately. The visitors batted first and Dr. Smith and Connor faced them. With the pitch in its favour, the bowling was simply unplayable, wicket after wicket being taken with unfailing regularity for a small score, until the whole had fallen for the total of 34. The credit</p>
