        <p>IN=M</p>
        <p>is due to Dr. Smith and E. Connor who desparihed tbe eleven, taking five wickets each br 18, and 12 runs VS' pectively.</p>
        <p>Smile vol batting was performed by the hems teyllo. who vvere subjict to frequent change iin the bowlo=l B Silcock by capital ba,:.ting compiled 21 runS. Th Awns retired with half a century to his credit. more and Bibby contributed 11 and 10 respectively. 0" innings closinz for a Otal of 114. ,</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyans on Saturday visited High . The home team batted first. After a fine perforao in which 108 runs were scorel for tie toss of ". wickAs, High Lagh declared their innings closed. ILI; Wesleyans were not in such good scoring form as ul hut one or two wickets reali,ed fair scores. Cis' Upson, and Light, batted well far 22,18 and 17 reri tively. The innings totalled 75 for six wickets. Tt'll interfered Ito favoJr of the visitors, the gams bliag draw.</p>
        <p>a Logb• tan 51i”4" Time</p>
        <p>On Saturday the secretary of the A roley Cricket 2ad eleven, received a letter from Messrs. Gelling st, Srookfields, Liverpool,announcing them winners of tli" competition. The competition was open to all within a radius of twenty milts from Liverpool. M Gelling and Brookfield s as a prize offer a large ' graph of the team to each member. During the . they have played 17 m itches. wan 13, lost two, dril" two.</p>
        <p>'t a i We, phOP. Sea° a</p>
        <p>CYCLING. Montreal has been selected as the place of meeting next year's World's Cycliar! Championship Races. ° Vienna, on Saturday, Palmer (England) was the ornc starter out of twelve who entered for the 100 kilom3tr.le Professional Championship of tots Word. roe Hall-cal,' Amateur handicap was won by Sumraersgill (Englaao,' who started from scratch. „raile</p>
        <p>On Monday morning Miss Faster b3at the fiftv--,,, road racord by eight minutes,h3r time being 2hr 33r1w,i The previous best was 21z 41 mm. viPn Mr. J. EL. ste timed.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Warring!' RI Cycling Club had tbel! annual fishing match. Tne committ3e of the Warrin! ton Angler's Association kindly placed their private P7O serve at Lynam at their dispostl, and 19 wheel Pleasantly classed an afternoon in tollowing the custorny of old Izaak Walton. The result of a most exciting 10., test was—lst prize, W. Barker ; 2nd prize, J. Podrnorfe Afterwards the wheelmen ran to Warburton, where triuo numbers were augmented to atout 40, and sat down 4, an excellent repast,provided by Host and Hosteis 13310' at the Saracen's Head, The evening was spmtininns.lt, and song, and a jolly time ensued, dlessrs. Ireland, ton, Mounfifid,fluxton, and Captain Waring doing best to entertain the c3mp3oy. The run and outl, were thoroughly enjoyed, and the officials Messrs. Vto more, D. Davies, Litton, Booth, and Pick%vance, ar be congratulated on the success of the gathering. ing the afternoon photographs of tha party were takw by Messrs. S. Baldry and P. Booth.</p>
        <p>BOWLING. id The games between Warrington South and Whiteh-.nr at Stretford on Saturday in the third round of the competition were very close. The former may cre„,"-; themselves with a well-des?rved victory. Another gB'.4 in the third round of this competition was brough.,"a at the Cemetery Green, Warringtou, in which Le..„'b defeated Lower Broughton by 15 points. Ratcliffe. Lherwood of - decisive style.</p>
